WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.400
- The following is a
conversation with Pavel Durov,

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.300
founder and CEO of Telegram,

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.433
a messaging platform

00:00:07.433 --> 00:00:11.166
actively used by over 1 billion people.

00:00:11.166 --> 00:00:14.833
Pavel has spent his life
fighting for freedom of speech,

00:00:14.833 --> 00:00:17.466
building tools that
protect human communication

00:00:17.466 --> 00:00:20.366
from surveillance and censorship.

00:00:20.366 --> 00:00:22.500
For this, he has faced pressure

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.300
from some of the most powerful governments

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.733
and organizations on earth.

00:00:26.733 --> 00:00:28.566
In the face of this immense pressure,

00:00:28.566 --> 00:00:30.300
he has always held his ground

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:33.000
continuously fighting
to protect user privacy

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.333
and the freedom of all of us humans

00:00:35.333 --> 00:00:37.666
to communicate with each other.

00:00:37.666 --> 00:00:40.033
I got the chance to spend
a few weeks with him

00:00:40.033 --> 00:00:41.700
and can definitively say

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:43.266
that he's one of the most principled

00:00:43.266 --> 00:00:45.666
and fearless humans I've ever met.

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:50.833
Plus, when I posted that
I'm hanging out with Pavel,

00:00:50.833 --> 00:00:52.800
a lot of people, fans of his,

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.566
wrote to me asking if he
does in fact privately live

00:00:56.566 --> 00:00:59.800
the disciplined aesthetic
life he's known for,

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:02.100
no alcohol, stoic mindset,

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:03.900
strict diet and exercise,

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:08.333
including a crazy amount of
daily pull-ups and pushups,

00:01:08.333 --> 00:01:10.900
no phone except to occasionally
test Telegram features,

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:12.166
and so on.

00:01:12.166 --> 00:01:15.400
Yes, he's 100% that guy,

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:17.900
which made the experience
of hanging out with him

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:19.733
really inspiring to me.

00:01:19.733 --> 00:01:20.933
I'm grateful for it,

00:01:20.933 --> 00:01:23.566
and I'm grateful to now be
able to call him a friend.

00:01:24.733 --> 00:01:27.866
This podcast conversation
is in parts philosophical

00:01:27.866 --> 00:01:29.766
about freedom, life, human nature,

00:01:29.766 --> 00:01:33.366
and the nature of
government bureaucracies,

00:01:33.366 --> 00:01:35.900
and it is also in part super technical,

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:38.133
because to me, it's fascinating

00:01:38.133 --> 00:01:41.000
that Telegram has a relatively
small engineering team

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.266
and yet is able to basically out-innovate

00:01:44.266 --> 00:01:45.833
all of its competitors

00:01:45.833 --> 00:01:50.833
with an insane rate of
introducing new, unique features.

00:01:50.966 --> 00:01:54.500
Just like the meme of "The
Simpsons" did it first,

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:57.033
you consider all the
features we know and love

00:01:57.033 --> 00:01:59.066
in our communication apps,

00:01:59.066 --> 00:02:02.100
in almost every case,
Telegram did it first.

00:02:02.966 --> 00:02:04.333
So we discuss it all,

00:02:04.333 --> 00:02:06.000
from the Kafkaesque situation

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.700
he's in the midst of in France,

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:10.033
to the rollercoaster
of his life and career,

00:02:10.033 --> 00:02:12.466
to his philosophy on technology,

00:02:12.466 --> 00:02:14.433
freedom, and the human condition.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.633
And by the way, while
this entire conversation

00:02:17.633 --> 00:02:19.000
is in English,

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.100
we'll make captions and
voiceover audio tracks

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:24.566
available in multiple languages,

00:02:24.566 --> 00:02:29.300
including Russian,
Ukrainian, French, and Hindi.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:32.400
On YouTube, you can switch
between language audio tracks

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.800
by clicking the settings gear icon,

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.233
then clicking Audio track,

00:02:37.233 --> 00:02:40.233
and then selecting the
language you prefer.

00:02:41.333 --> 00:02:44.400
Huge thank you once again to ElevenLabs

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:47.366
for their help with
translation and dubbing

00:02:47.366 --> 00:02:49.566
and with the bigger mission

00:02:49.566 --> 00:02:52.500
of breaking down barriers
that language creates.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:54.933
They are truly one of the
most remarkable companies

00:02:54.933 --> 00:02:57.600
I've ever had the
pleasure of working with.

