00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:06.433
And now, dear friends, here's Pavel Durov.

00:03:07.433 --> 00:03:11.533
- You've been an advocate for freedom for many
years, writing that you should be ready

00:03:11.633 --> 00:03:14.966
to risk everything for
freedom. What were some

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:21.600
influences and insights that helped you
arrive at this value of human freedom?

00:03:21.666 --> 00:03:25.633
- I get to experience the difference
between a society with freedom and a

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:29.566
society without freedom pretty
early in life. I was four years old

00:03:29.633 --> 00:03:33.600
when my family moved
from the Soviet Union to

00:03:33.666 --> 00:03:37.533
northern Italy, and I could see
that a society without freedom

00:03:38.233 --> 00:03:45.800
cannot enjoy the abundance of
opinions, of ideas, of goods,

00:03:45.833 --> 00:03:49.033
and services. Even for a four
or five year old kid, it was

00:03:49.066 --> 00:03:53.066
obvious. That you can't experience all the

00:03:54.333 --> 00:03:58.066
toys, the ice cream of sorts, the cartoons

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:02.433
in the Soviet Union that you could access
in Italy. And then I got to realize

00:04:02.533 --> 00:04:06.166
something even more important.
You don't get to contribute

00:04:06.466 --> 00:04:14.200
to this abundance without freedom. And at
this point, it was pretty obvious to me.

00:04:14.333 --> 00:04:17.933
- You also wrote, "Svoboda vazhne
deneg." It translates to:

00:04:17.933 --> 00:04:24.633
"Freedom matters more than money." How
do you prevent these values for freedom

00:04:24.666 --> 00:04:29.733
corrupted by money, by people with
influence, by people with power?

00:04:29.766 --> 00:04:32.966
- Well, the biggest enemies
of freedom are fear and

00:04:33.033 --> 00:04:37.000
greed. So you make sure that
they don't stand in your

00:04:37.066 --> 00:04:40.600
way. If you imagine the worst thing that

00:04:40.633 --> 00:04:44.500
can happen to you, and then make
yourself be comfortable with

00:04:44.533 --> 00:04:51.066
it, there's nothing more left to be
afraid of. So you stand your ground,

00:04:51.933 --> 00:04:55.600
and you remember that it's worth
living your life according

00:04:55.666 --> 00:05:02.266
to the principles that you believe
in, even though this life can end up

00:05:03.166 --> 00:05:07.933
being shorter than a longer
life, but lived in slavery.

00:05:08.233 --> 00:05:12.033
- Do you contemplate your mortality?
Do you think about your death?

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:13.033
- Oh, yes.

00:05:13.066 --> 00:05:14.600
- Are you afraid of it?

00:05:14.666 --> 00:05:18.600
- In a way, you have to go
against your instinct of

00:05:18.666 --> 00:05:22.533
self-preservation. And
it's not easy. We are all

00:05:22.566 --> 00:05:27.833
biological beings hard-coded to be
afraid of death. Nobody wants to die.

00:05:28.866 --> 00:05:32.533
But when you approach it
rationally, you live and then you

00:05:32.566 --> 00:05:36.333
die. There's no such thing
as your death in your

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:40.366
life. You stop experiencing
life once you die.

00:05:40.433 --> 00:05:43.933
So you have to ask yourself this
question, "Is it worth living a

00:05:44.066 --> 00:05:47.966
life full of fear of death?" Or, it's

00:05:48.033 --> 00:05:52.366
much more enjoyable to forget
about this and live your life

00:05:53.166 --> 00:05:58.166
in a way that makes you immune
to this fear, at the same time

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:03.166
remembering that death exists
so that every day would count.

00:06:03.233 --> 00:06:08.166
- Yeah. Remembering that death
exists makes you deeply feel

00:06:09.166 --> 00:06:11.400
every moment that you do get.

00:06:11.466 --> 00:06:15.366
- That's why I love reminding
myself that I can die any day.

00:06:15.433 --> 00:06:19.133
- In many ways you live a pretty
stoic existence. I got a chance to

00:06:19.233 --> 00:06:23.200
spend a couple of weeks with
you. In many ways, you seek to

00:06:23.266 --> 00:06:26.666
minimize the negative effects
of the outside world on your

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:30.366
mind. You've written, quote,

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:34.666
"If you want to reach your full potential
and maintain clarity of mind, stay

00:06:34.766 --> 00:06:38.533
away from addictive substances.
My success and health

00:06:39.066 --> 00:06:42.633
are the result of 20 plus years
of complete abstinence from

00:06:42.733 --> 00:06:46.466
alcohol, tobacco, coffee,
pills, and illegal

00:06:46.466 --> 00:06:50.100
drugs. Short-term
pleasure isn't worth your

00:06:50.166 --> 00:06:53.933
future." Let's talk about
each one of these. Alcohol.

00:06:53.966 --> 00:06:57.766
What's been your philosophy behind that?

00:06:57.866 --> 00:07:01.733
- That one is quite easy.
When I was 11 years old, my

00:07:01.766 --> 00:07:05.766
biochemistry teacher, he gave
me this book he wrote, it

00:07:05.833 --> 00:07:09.733
was called The Illusion
of Paradise. In there, he

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:16.833
would describe the biological and
chemical processes that happen in your

00:07:16.866 --> 00:07:23.800
body once you consume this or that
substance. It was mainly related to

00:07:24.733 --> 00:07:28.166
illegal drugs, but alcohol was one of
these addictive substances that he

00:07:28.233 --> 00:07:36.100
covered. So it turns out that when you drink
alcohol, the thing that happens is that

00:07:36.166 --> 00:07:41.366
your brain cells become
paralyzed. They become literally

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:49.966
zombies. And then next day, some time after
the party is over, some of your brain

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:54.066
cells die and never get
to normal. So think about

00:07:54.066 --> 00:07:58.000
this. If your brain is this most
valuable tool you have in your

00:07:58.033 --> 00:08:03.733
journey to success and happiness, why
would you destroy this tool for short-term

00:08:03.833 --> 00:08:06.433
pleasure? This sounds ridiculous.

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:10.066
- In many ways, it's a poison we
let in our body. But by way of

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:14.033
advice, what advice would you give to people
who consider not drinking? You know, a

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:21.066
lot of people use alcohol to enable
them to have a vibrant social

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:25.033
life. There's a lot of
pressures from society,

00:08:25.066 --> 00:08:27.733
you know, at a party to drink so they can

00:08:27.766 --> 00:08:31.066
socialize. So, what advice
would you give to them,

00:08:32.433 --> 00:08:37.633
To people who imagine having a
social life without alcohol?

00:08:37.666 --> 00:08:41.733
- Well, first of all, don't be afraid
to be contrarian. Set your own rules.

00:08:43.666 --> 00:08:47.633
Secondly, if you feel you
need to drink, there must

00:08:47.666 --> 00:08:51.433
be some problem you're trying to
conceal. There's something that, some

00:08:51.566 --> 00:08:58.100
fear you're not ready to confront,
and you have to address this fear.

00:08:59.633 --> 00:09:02.866
If there is a good-looking girl
you're afraid to approach,

00:09:04.166 --> 00:09:08.000
get rid of this fear, approach her,
practice, do it again and again. It's pretty

00:09:08.066 --> 00:09:11.300
banal... but this advice works.

00:09:11.366 --> 00:09:15.133
- Fix the underlying problem, which
is usually at the very bottom is

00:09:15.166 --> 00:09:17.566
always going to be fear. Work on that.

00:09:17.666 --> 00:09:21.333
- I don't know. Very often, people are trying
to escape something in their lives with

00:09:21.366 --> 00:09:25.300
alcohol. What is it they're
trying to escape? What is this

00:09:25.333 --> 00:09:31.433
problem? You have to get to the bottom
of it. Your mind is trying to tell you

00:09:31.566 --> 00:09:38.033
something valuable, and instead of
addressing it directly, you are

00:09:38.166 --> 00:09:46.033
flooding it in alcohol, which is sort
of a spiritual painkiller, but works

00:09:46.066 --> 00:09:51.333
only temporarily, and then you have
to pay the debt with interest.

00:09:51.333 --> 00:09:54.233
- So, what do you do? I mean, you've
been in a lot of gatherings, a lot of

00:09:54.266 --> 00:09:58.600
parties. Is there some
challenges to saying no?

00:09:58.633 --> 00:10:05.533
- For me, not at all. I've been always ready
to stand my ground and say no when I feel

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:10.566
something's not right. And
it's extraordinary how easily

00:10:11.733 --> 00:10:18.833
we humans are affected by what we perceive
as majority, because nobody since ancient

00:10:18.933 --> 00:10:22.866
times, since millions of
years ago, wants to be left

00:10:22.933 --> 00:10:34.366
out by the tribe. We are scared that we
won't become accepted anymore, which

00:10:34.366 --> 00:10:37.833
thousands or millions of years ago
meant we're going to starve to

00:10:37.833 --> 00:10:46.133
death. So, we have to consciously
fight this inclination

00:10:47.266 --> 00:10:53.033
to be agreeable with everything that
the majority imposes on you, because

00:10:53.033 --> 00:10:56.200
it's quite clear that many things that the

00:10:56.233 --> 00:10:59.766
majority... in many activities
the majority is engaging in

00:11:01.366 --> 00:11:02.933
are not bringing you any good.

00:11:03.466 --> 00:11:06.233
- So, that's another fear you have
to face. Going into a party and

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:11.100
the fear of being the outcast at
that party, of being different than

00:11:11.166 --> 00:11:15.066
others at that party, at that
social gathering in the crowd

00:11:15.100 --> 00:11:17.700
of humans, be different. That's a fear.

00:11:17.766 --> 00:11:21.466
- That's a fear, and it's quite
irrational if you think about it.

00:11:21.566 --> 00:11:28.600
It was something that made a
lot of sense 20,000 years ago.

00:11:28.666 --> 00:11:32.100
It makes zero sense today,
because if you think about it, if

00:11:32.166 --> 00:11:36.100
you do the same thing everybody
else around you is doing,

00:11:36.133 --> 00:11:42.233
you don't have any competitive advantage,
and you don't get to become outstanding

00:11:43.266 --> 00:11:45.066
at some point in your life.

00:11:45.133 --> 00:11:48.100
- Yeah, that's one of the
things we talked about

00:11:49.333 --> 00:11:53.033
sort of by way of advice is, if you want
to be successful in life, you want to be

00:11:53.333 --> 00:11:53.833
different.

00:11:55.266 --> 00:11:56.133
- Definitely.

00:11:56.166 --> 00:11:59.733
- And perhaps, I think you
said you want to achieve

00:11:59.833 --> 00:12:03.233
mastery at a niche, so find
a niche at which you can

00:12:03.266 --> 00:12:06.866
pursue with all your effort
and achieve mastery,

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:10.733
and the niche being
different than anything that

00:12:10.766 --> 00:12:13.833
anybody else is doing. Can you
explain that a little bit more?

00:12:13.900 --> 00:12:18.166
- So, obviously, in order to
contribute to the society

00:12:19.533 --> 00:12:22.766
you're in, to the economy
of the country you live

00:12:22.833 --> 00:12:26.766
in, you have to do something
that is valuable. But

00:12:26.833 --> 00:12:30.133
if you're doing something that
everybody else is doing anyway,

00:12:31.933 --> 00:12:35.833
what's the value of it?
Now, it sounds easier

00:12:35.933 --> 00:12:39.266
than it is done to do something
that nobody else is doing,

00:12:39.766 --> 00:12:43.433
because we humans are surrounded
by all kinds of information

00:12:43.533 --> 00:12:46.833
which makes us want to copy what we are

00:12:46.866 --> 00:12:50.800
perceiving. At the same time, there
are so many areas which you can

00:12:50.833 --> 00:12:54.466
explore that have nothing to do with the

00:12:54.500 --> 00:12:57.566
information you receive on
the daily basis. So, it's

00:12:57.633 --> 00:13:01.933
extremely important to curate the
information sources that you have,

00:13:03.533 --> 00:13:08.700
so that you wouldn't be
somebody who is left to

00:13:09.866 --> 00:13:15.833
the will of AI-based algorithmic
feed telling you what's

00:13:15.866 --> 00:13:19.766
important, so that you
end up consuming the same

00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:23.233
information, the same stuff, the same
memes, the same news as everybody

00:13:23.266 --> 00:13:29.766
else. But rather, you should be
proactive. You should deliberately try to

00:13:29.866 --> 00:13:36.733
set a goal an area that you want to
explore, and then actively search

00:13:36.766 --> 00:13:42.866
information that is relevant to
this field, so that one day,

00:13:43.866 --> 00:13:47.466
you can become the world's number one

00:13:47.600 --> 00:13:51.333
expert in this field. And it's
not quite... it's not that

00:13:51.400 --> 00:13:55.200
difficult to do that. You have to

00:13:55.333 --> 00:13:59.100
just remain consistent, because
nobody else is trying to do

00:13:59.133 --> 00:14:03.033
that. Everybody else is just reading
the same news and discussing the same

00:14:03.066 --> 00:14:08.833
news every day. But this way, they don't
get to have a competitive advantage.

00:14:08.966 --> 00:14:12.566
- Yeah. The majority of the
population become slaves to the

00:14:12.666 --> 00:14:16.433
AI recommender systems, AI-driven
recommender systems, and so the

00:14:16.466 --> 00:14:20.333
content everybody's fed is the same
thing, and we all become the same.

00:14:20.366 --> 00:14:24.300
On that point, one of the
different things you do is you

00:14:24.333 --> 00:14:28.200
don't use a phone, except
occasionally to test Telegram

00:14:28.233 --> 00:14:31.833
features. But I've been with you for
two weeks. I haven't seen you use a

00:14:31.866 --> 00:14:35.533
phone at all in the way that most
people use a phone, like, for their

00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:39.966
social media. So, can you describe
your philosophy behind that?

00:14:40.166 --> 00:14:44.100
- I don't think a phone is
a necessary device. I

00:14:44.166 --> 00:14:46.533
remember growing up, I
didn't have a mobile phone.

00:14:48.133 --> 00:14:51.966
When I was a student at the
university, I didn't have a mobile

00:14:52.033 --> 00:14:55.833
phone. When I finally got
to use a mobile phone, I

00:14:55.866 --> 00:15:03.600
never used phone calls. I was always
in airplane mode or mute. I hated the

00:15:03.666 --> 00:15:14.900
idea of being disturbed. My philosophy
here is pretty simple. I want to define

00:15:15.933 --> 00:15:21.700
what is important in my
life. I don't want other

00:15:21.766 --> 00:15:29.400
people or companies, all kinds of
organizations telling me what is

00:15:29.433 --> 00:15:33.300
important today and what

00:15:33.366 --> 00:15:37.333
I should be thinking about. Just
set up your own agenda, and

00:15:37.400 --> 00:15:40.066
the phone gets in your way.

00:15:40.066 --> 00:15:44.000
- It provides distractions.
It guides what you

00:15:44.033 --> 00:15:47.833
should be looking at, what you will be looking
at, so you don't want that. You want to

00:15:47.900 --> 00:15:54.933
quiet the mind. You want to choose what
kind of stuff you let inside your mind.

00:15:54.966 --> 00:15:57.166
- Yes, because this way I can contribute

00:15:57.966 --> 00:16:01.933
to the progress of society, or at
least I like to think this way, and

00:16:01.966 --> 00:16:03.300
this makes me happier.

00:16:03.366 --> 00:16:07.033
- How often do you find quiet time
to just think and focus deeply on

00:16:07.100 --> 00:16:13.166
work without any distractions? You mentioned
to me that you value quiet mornings.

00:16:13.533 --> 00:16:18.366
- Yes. So the thing I'm trying to do, I
try to allocate as much time as possible

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:23.733
for sleep. Now, even if
I allocate, say, 11 or

00:16:23.766 --> 00:16:27.700
12 hours for sleep, I won't
sleep for 11 or 12 hours.

00:16:27.766 --> 00:16:31.466
So what I end up doing is I end up

00:16:31.666 --> 00:16:35.433
lying in bed thinking, and
some people hate it. They

00:16:35.466 --> 00:16:39.166
say, "Oh, you have to take a sleeping
pill," but I never take pills.

00:16:39.800 --> 00:16:42.366
I love these moments. I get

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:47.166
so many brilliant ideas, or at
least they seem brilliant to me at

00:16:47.200 --> 00:16:51.166
the moment, while I'm lying in bed, either

00:16:52.266 --> 00:16:56.166
late in the evening or early in the morning.
That's my favorite time of the day.

00:16:56.233 --> 00:17:01.466
Sometimes I wake up, I go take
a shower, still without the

00:17:01.500 --> 00:17:05.366
phone. Beautiful ideas can come to you

00:17:05.500 --> 00:17:09.366
while you're doing your
morning exercise, your

00:17:09.400 --> 00:17:15.000
morning routine, without a phone. If you
open your phone first thing in the morning,

00:17:15.833 --> 00:17:19.166
what you end up being
is a creature that is

00:17:19.200 --> 00:17:26.166
told what to think about for the rest
of the day. Same is true in a way if

00:17:27.400 --> 00:17:30.666
you've been consuming news from
social media late at night.

00:17:32.066 --> 00:17:35.866
But then how do you define
what is important and

00:17:35.966 --> 00:17:39.866
what you really want to become
in life? Now, I'm not saying

00:17:39.933 --> 00:17:43.833
you have to completely stay
away from all sources of

00:17:43.866 --> 00:17:47.633
information, but take some time
to think about what's really

00:17:47.666 --> 00:17:51.433
important for you and what you
want to change in this world.

00:17:51.500 --> 00:17:55.333
- So you definitely try to avoid digital devices
for as many hours as possible in the morning,

00:17:56.866 --> 00:18:00.300
just to have the quiet thinking
time? Plus the crazy amounts of

00:18:00.333 --> 00:18:02.266
push-ups and squats.

00:18:02.566 --> 00:18:05.633
- I know it's kind of
counterintuitive because

00:18:07.100 --> 00:18:11.033
I founded one of the largest social
networks in the world, after which I

00:18:11.266 --> 00:18:14.000
founded the second-largest
messaging app in the world, and

00:18:15.133 --> 00:18:19.000
you're supposed to be
really connected. But the

00:18:19.033 --> 00:18:22.800
conclusion you reach very early
is that the more connected and

00:18:22.866 --> 00:18:26.333
accessible you are, the
less productive you are.

00:18:28.766 --> 00:18:32.400
And then how can you run this
thing if you're constantly

00:18:32.466 --> 00:18:38.966
bombarded by all kinds of information,
most of which is irrelevant to the success

00:18:40.400 --> 00:18:46.933
of what you're trying to build? You
know, the entire world can be fascinated

00:18:47.333 --> 00:18:51.266
by a fight, a quarrel between
the world's richest man and

00:18:51.300 --> 00:18:55.266
the world's most powerful man. But
for the vast majority of these people

00:18:55.366 --> 00:19:01.866
following this saga, it's irrelevant.
It won't change their lives.

00:19:01.933 --> 00:19:08.033
And in any case, they can't affect it, so
it's a bit pointless. Of course, there are

00:19:08.066 --> 00:19:11.866
people who are engaged in activities

00:19:11.966 --> 00:19:15.500
that require them to be up-to-date
of everything that's going

00:19:15.566 --> 00:19:19.566
on, but 99% of people aren't.

00:19:19.633 --> 00:19:24.400
- Yeah. The internet, social
media presents to us drama

00:19:26.100 --> 00:19:30.000
in such a way that we think it's the biggest
thing in the world, the most important thing in

00:19:30.066 --> 00:19:33.833
which the tides of history will
turn when in reality, most things

00:19:33.900 --> 00:19:37.866
will not turn the tides of history.
And so, I guess our challenge

00:19:37.900 --> 00:19:41.766
is to figure out what is
the timeless thing? What is

00:19:41.800 --> 00:19:45.066
the thing that's happening today that's
still going to be true in 10, 20

00:19:45.100 --> 00:19:48.800
years? And from that,

00:19:48.866 --> 00:19:52.566
decide what you're going to do. And
that's very difficult on social media

00:19:52.600 --> 00:19:56.466
'cause everybody's outraged. The news
of the day, whatever the quarrel is,

00:19:56.900 --> 00:20:00.866
that's the thing that everyone
thinks the world will end because

00:20:00.933 --> 00:20:04.733
of this thing, and then another
thing happens the next day.

00:20:04.766 --> 00:20:07.766
- And they're trying to
influence your emotions.

00:20:08.466 --> 00:20:13.900
And that's how you get into trouble, because
you can be forced to make conclusions

00:20:14.766 --> 00:20:17.066
that are not in your best interest.

00:20:17.100 --> 00:20:20.500
- I've seen you be, once again,
quite stoic about your

00:20:20.533 --> 00:20:22.900
emotions. Do you ever get
angry? Do you ever get

00:20:23.900 --> 00:20:27.366
lonely? You ever get sad?
The rollercoaster of human

00:20:27.433 --> 00:20:31.133
emotion. And what do you do with that?
What do you make difficult decisions?

00:20:31.166 --> 00:20:34.466
- I'm a human being like everybody
else. I do get to experience

00:20:34.566 --> 00:20:38.400
emotions, and some of them
are not very pleasant. But I

00:20:38.466 --> 00:20:42.700
believe that it's the
responsibility of every one of us-

00:20:44.266 --> 00:20:47.566
...to cope with these
emotions and to learn to

00:20:48.366 --> 00:20:52.166
work through them.
Self-discipline is particularly

00:20:52.166 --> 00:20:57.100
important, because without
it, how can you overcome

00:20:58.233 --> 00:21:03.500
this seemingly endless
loop of negativity or

00:21:03.566 --> 00:21:09.566
despair that ultimately leads to depression
for some people? I normally never

00:21:09.566 --> 00:21:13.400
have depression. I don't remember
having depression in the last 20

00:21:13.466 --> 00:21:21.233
years at least, maybe when I was a teenager.
But one of the reasons for that is, I

00:21:21.366 --> 00:21:30.266
start doing things. I identify the
problem, I can see a solution, and I start

00:21:31.233 --> 00:21:39.033
executing the strategy. If
you are stuck in this loop of

00:21:39.066 --> 00:21:42.566
being worried about something,
nothing's ever going to change.

00:21:43.566 --> 00:21:47.566
And people often make this mistake,
thinking, "Oh, I should just

00:21:48.466 --> 00:21:52.300
have some rest and then
regain energy." This is

00:21:52.300 --> 00:21:55.866
not how it works. You gain energy by doing

00:21:55.933 --> 00:21:59.766
something. So if you start doing
something, then it happens. You

00:21:59.866 --> 00:22:05.733
feel motivated, you feel inspired,
and then ultimately you do

00:22:05.933 --> 00:22:09.800
something else, a little bit more, a
little bit more, and in a few years,

00:22:09.833 --> 00:22:11.666
who knows, you may end up
achieving great things.

00:22:12.300 --> 00:22:15.300
- Yeah, that's the thing that people
really confuse. If you're stuck

00:22:16.833 --> 00:22:20.700
in a depressive cycle,
even when you really,

00:22:20.733 --> 00:22:23.966
really, really, really don't
wanna do anything, just do

00:22:24.033 --> 00:22:27.966
something. Try to make progress
because the good feeling comes

00:22:28.033 --> 00:22:31.800
in the end of that. The whole point is to do
first and then feel, not feel and then do.

00:22:33.333 --> 00:22:37.066
- Exactly. And going to the
gym is a good example.

00:22:37.100 --> 00:22:40.000
There are many days when you
don't want to start working

00:22:40.000 --> 00:22:46.466
out, but you have to overcome
this initial reluctance, and

00:22:46.533 --> 00:22:50.500
then you get to a point that
you enjoy it, and you think,

00:22:50.533 --> 00:22:54.200
"Oh my God, it was such a good idea
to come to the gym today." But

00:22:54.300 --> 00:23:00.200
it's similar to pretty much every
activity. You get to write some

00:23:00.233 --> 00:23:04.133
code, write a small
piece of code first, and

00:23:04.166 --> 00:23:06.966
then you get inspired.
Then you come up with more

00:23:07.033 --> 00:23:12.033
ideas. You need to write a novel
or just write the program.

00:23:13.166 --> 00:23:15.500
This is pretty obvious, and

00:23:16.400 --> 00:23:20.300
it's not a secret, but because
we are bombarded with all kinds

00:23:20.366 --> 00:23:25.933
of information that is not really
important for us in terms of becoming

00:23:25.966 --> 00:23:32.533
successful, we often forget the important
things, and this is one of them.

00:23:32.566 --> 00:23:38.366
- We've been working out every single day.
You have been working out for many years

00:23:40.133 --> 00:23:43.666
pretty intensively, so I think
a lot of people would love to

00:23:44.366 --> 00:23:46.766
know what's your perfect
daily workout regimen,

00:23:48.366 --> 00:23:50.300
let's say on a daily, on a weekly basis?

00:23:50.800 --> 00:23:54.766
- I do 300 push-ups and 300
squats every morning, and in

00:23:54.833 --> 00:23:58.633
addition to that, I go to
the gym normally five or six

00:23:59.000 --> 00:24:03.966
times a week, spending between
one and two hours every day.

00:24:04.033 --> 00:24:07.200
- So push-ups and squats are still
a big part of your routine?

00:24:07.233 --> 00:24:10.800
- Yes, this is how I start
my day. I'm not sure they

00:24:11.166 --> 00:24:16.633
do a lot in terms of changing your body,
but they're definitely a good way to

00:24:18.133 --> 00:24:19.300
practice self-discipline,

00:24:20.366 --> 00:24:24.333
because you don't want to do these
push-ups in the morning most of the days.

00:24:24.366 --> 00:24:27.766
Squats are particularly
boring. They're not that

00:24:27.833 --> 00:24:35.033
hard. They're just boring. But you
overcome it, and then it's much easier to

00:24:36.333 --> 00:24:43.000
start doing other things related
to your work, for example. When I

00:24:43.066 --> 00:24:47.000
can, I also take an ice bath
because it's another exercise of

00:24:47.033 --> 00:24:50.866
self-discipline. I think the main
muscle you can exercise is this

00:24:50.900 --> 00:24:54.833
muscle, the muscle of
self-discipline. You know,

00:24:54.833 --> 00:24:59.266
not your biceps or your
pecs or anything else,

00:25:00.133 --> 00:25:07.500
because if you get to train that one,
everything else just comes by itself.

00:25:07.533 --> 00:25:11.533
- Yeah, everything else becomes easy.
We should mention, I went with you

00:25:11.600 --> 00:25:16.133
to Banya, and I think it's
fair to say you're nuts

00:25:17.433 --> 00:25:21.366
in terms of how much you
can handle, and I didn't

00:25:21.400 --> 00:25:25.033
even see the worst of it. Can you just
speak to your crazy escapades in the Banya,

00:25:25.533 --> 00:25:29.100
what value you get from it, so
both the heat and the cold?

00:25:31.333 --> 00:25:36.333
- I don't know if it's crazy. I think it's
quite natural and normal by this time.

00:25:36.833 --> 00:25:42.433
But maybe I just got used to it.
So Banya is this extreme kind of

00:25:43.333 --> 00:25:47.000
sauna practiced by Eastern Europeans.

00:25:49.133 --> 00:25:55.766
But it is done in a way that
maximizes heat, and they also

00:25:55.833 --> 00:26:01.566
use all kinds of herbs and branches, and
it's a much more holistic and natural

00:26:01.600 --> 00:26:08.133
experience. Then a necessary
part of it is you get the cold

00:26:08.233 --> 00:26:12.200
plunge, and then you go back. And

00:26:12.266 --> 00:26:16.166
again, this is one of those
things that maybe in the moment

00:26:16.566 --> 00:26:20.200
is not always that pleasant,
particularly if you go to extreme

00:26:20.733 --> 00:26:24.700
temperatures. You don't
feel great. I don't always

00:26:24.733 --> 00:26:29.433
feel great, but this feeling is
passing. It's only a few minutes.

00:26:30.966 --> 00:26:34.900
Same with the ice bath.
You have to suffer a bit,

00:26:36.433 --> 00:26:40.866
and then you get to feel great
for hours and days after.

00:26:42.633 --> 00:26:46.600
What's more, it gives you these
long-term health benefits. In a

00:26:46.666 --> 00:26:50.466
way, you can look at it as alcohol in

00:26:50.533 --> 00:26:54.366
reverse. Alcohol will give
you this short, fleeting

00:26:54.666 --> 00:27:00.600
pleasure for an hour, for a couple of
hours, but then you will be paying

00:27:01.666 --> 00:27:09.033
for it with long-term negative consequences.
I'd rather do Banya and ice bath.

00:27:09.166 --> 00:27:13.033
- We swam the length of a large lake
in France a couple times. Can you

00:27:13.133 --> 00:27:17.366
talk through why you value
these multi-hour swims?

00:27:17.433 --> 00:27:25.100
- I love swimming for hours. The longest
I swam was five and a half hours in

00:27:25.133 --> 00:27:29.100
Finland, it was quite cold.
I got lost in the process,

00:27:29.700 --> 00:27:33.233
barely could find my way back. But

00:27:35.533 --> 00:27:39.400
the reason I do it, yes, you feel
great after. You're shaking a little

00:27:39.466 --> 00:27:43.033
bit, but you feel great after. We
cross a huge lake, and I cross many

00:27:43.133 --> 00:27:50.133
lakes, Geneva Lake, Zurich Lake. And
every time, you feel this achievement

00:27:51.166 --> 00:27:56.900
which makes you happy, makes you
feel strong, and then you're

00:27:56.933 --> 00:28:04.633
more ready to other challenges. And of
course, when you know you are going to start

00:28:05.700 --> 00:28:09.300
a journey that will last a few
hours, you're reluctant to do

00:28:09.400 --> 00:28:14.966
it. But you swim for 10 minutes, and then
for 20 minutes, and then for 30 minutes,

00:28:16.666 --> 00:28:18.566
and it teaches you this
incredible patience

00:28:20.000 --> 00:28:23.833
that I think is necessary if you
want to achieve anything in life.

00:28:23.866 --> 00:28:27.500
- And it's pretty meditative,
lake versus ocean.

00:28:27.566 --> 00:28:33.833
- Yes. And you don't have to go too fast.
You can be slow and enjoy the moment.

00:28:33.866 --> 00:28:37.733
- Until you get lost, and it's five and a
half hours. Did you panic if you were

00:28:37.766 --> 00:28:39.900
gonna be able to find the
shore or find your way out?

00:28:39.933 --> 00:28:46.100
- Not really. I'm a reasonably stress-resilient
person. I didn't panic at that moment,

00:28:46.166 --> 00:28:49.433
and there were worse swims I
had that were shorter, but

00:28:50.266 --> 00:28:53.933
involved accidents, and you
know about some of them. So

00:28:54.200 --> 00:28:58.066
that wasn't the worst by
far. But an important thing

00:28:58.100 --> 00:29:05.733
about swimming and physical activity in
general is that it makes your mind clear,

00:29:05.833 --> 00:29:12.200
and your thinking process is becoming
more efficient. Because at the end

00:29:12.233 --> 00:29:19.366
of the day, the efficiency of our brain is
limited by how much sugar and oxygen our

00:29:19.700 --> 00:29:23.666
heart can push through blood to
our brain. So how can you make

00:29:23.733 --> 00:29:29.233
this go faster, or how do you make your
lungs more efficient? How do you make

00:29:30.533 --> 00:29:34.466
your heart more efficient in
doing that? Physical activity is

00:29:34.500 --> 00:29:40.033
the only way I know of. So
it's not just staying healthy

00:29:41.133 --> 00:29:52.200
or trying to look good. It's also
being productive. It's also being

00:29:52.300 --> 00:29:56.233
stress-resilient. All of these qualities

00:29:56.300 --> 00:30:01.400
are necessary if you want to run a large
company, if you want to start a company.

00:30:02.500 --> 00:30:06.300
I'm surprised, when I started doing this

00:30:07.433 --> 00:30:12.800
more than 10 years ago, that more
CEOs didn't engage in sports.

00:30:14.000 --> 00:30:17.033
The situation changed in the
last several years, which is

00:30:17.100 --> 00:30:21.666
great. Because back in the day, if you take
20 years ago, there was this stereotype

00:30:22.700 --> 00:30:27.433
that if you were strong, you
must be not very smart, and vice

00:30:27.533 --> 00:30:34.300
versa, which is complete lunacy. Very
often, these two things go together.

00:30:34.366 --> 00:30:38.266
- So for you, working out is not just about staying
healthy. It's actually valuable for the work that

00:30:38.300 --> 00:30:43.300
you do as a tech leader, as an
engineer, as a technologist?

00:30:43.366 --> 00:30:49.700
- Oh, yes. When I can't
train, I can instantly feel

00:30:50.933 --> 00:30:55.366
that stress is creeping on me. Like...

00:30:57.233 --> 00:30:59.933
So even in situations where I'm
constrained, I can't go to the

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:05.933
gym, I just keep doing push-ups.
I just keep doing squats.

00:31:06.033 --> 00:31:09.966
- Yeah. I mean, that's the cool thing
about bodyweight exercise, you

00:31:10.033 --> 00:31:13.933
can just do it anywhere.
You could just pop

00:31:13.966 --> 00:31:16.300
off 50 or 100 push-ups before a meeting.

00:31:16.400 --> 00:31:21.100
- Don't you feel weird when you have
a day without physical activity?

00:31:21.366 --> 00:31:25.533
- Yeah. If I go a day without doing
push-ups, at the very minimum,

00:31:26.333 --> 00:31:27.666
that's a shitty day.

00:31:27.766 --> 00:31:30.033
- And if you can do pull-ups,
it's even better.

00:31:30.100 --> 00:31:33.433
- Yeah. I gotta ask you about
your diet, too. No processed

00:31:33.500 --> 00:31:37.333
sugar, no fast food, no soda,
intermittent fasting sometimes

00:31:37.466 --> 00:31:41.433
once a day only, sometimes a
couple times a day. So take me

00:31:41.466 --> 00:31:47.900
through your philosophy on the no
sugar, no soda, just clean food.

00:31:47.933 --> 00:31:51.800
- Well, sugar is pretty easy,
because it's addictive.

00:31:51.800 --> 00:31:55.766
The more you consume sugar, the more
you want it, the hungrier you get.

00:31:57.166 --> 00:31:59.766
So if you want to stay
efficient and healthy,

00:32:01.500 --> 00:32:06.666
why consume processed sugar? You'll
just end up snacking all the time.

00:32:08.100 --> 00:32:11.900
Intermittent fasting,
eating only within six

00:32:11.900 --> 00:32:20.266
hours or not eating for 18
hours every day also brings

00:32:20.400 --> 00:32:27.800
structure into your day and
into your eating habits, so you

00:32:27.833 --> 00:32:31.300
don't crave sugar anymore. Because,
you know, if you eat sugar and

00:32:31.333 --> 00:32:39.000
then you're unable to snack, you're just
punishing yourself. I read a few books on

00:32:39.000 --> 00:32:45.233
longevity. I think something
everybody agrees on is that sugar is

00:32:46.133 --> 00:32:53.933
harmful. Now, I'm not militant about
sugar. You can eat berries, fruit, if you

00:32:54.033 --> 00:32:59.366
feel your body needs it.
But it's not true to think

00:33:01.100 --> 00:33:04.733
it's necessary to consume
sweet things, not for

00:33:04.900 --> 00:33:08.633
children, not for adults. Red meat, I

00:33:08.700 --> 00:33:12.533
stopped eating it about 20 years
ago, because I just felt heavy

00:33:13.733 --> 00:33:17.100
every time I had it. So I
guess it's individual. It's my

00:33:17.133 --> 00:33:25.333
metabolism, my digestive system
isn't agreeing with this kind of

00:33:25.366 --> 00:33:32.266
food. So I normally eat seafood of
all kinds and vegetables. This is

00:33:33.800 --> 00:33:36.966
the basic source of calories for me.

00:33:37.033 --> 00:33:41.000
- Yeah, and like all things you
said, short-term pleasure isn't

00:33:41.066 --> 00:33:44.966
worth your future. So, a lot of things we
all know, that alcohol is destructive to the

00:33:45.000 --> 00:33:52.600
body, tobacco, pills, processed food, sugar.
But society puts that on you, makes it

00:33:52.633 --> 00:33:56.300
very difficult to avoid. So, I guess
it all boils down to just discipline.

00:33:56.333 --> 00:34:02.400
- Yes, and trying to identify the
real cause of an issue you're

00:34:02.400 --> 00:34:10.033
experiencing. If you're experiencing a
headache, one solution would be to take

00:34:10.100 --> 00:34:16.400
a pill, and then the headache disappears.
What this pill would actually do, in most

00:34:16.466 --> 00:34:24.400
cases, it would mute the consequence,
your feeling of pain. It's a painkiller.

00:34:25.600 --> 00:34:29.400
It will not eliminate the root
cause, so you have to ask

00:34:29.433 --> 00:34:34.733
yourself, "What is it that is causing
this headache? Do I need to drink some

00:34:34.800 --> 00:34:41.200
water? Is the air quality here bad?
Do I need to start getting more

00:34:41.233 --> 00:34:44.633
sleep? Is there something
wrong with people around me

00:34:45.500 --> 00:34:49.233
that are stressing me out?" There
must be some reason why you're

00:34:49.233 --> 00:34:53.233
experiencing a headache.
But if you take a pill,

00:34:53.266 --> 00:34:58.733
you're not removing this reason. You're
actually making it worse, because this

00:34:59.600 --> 00:35:04.966
harmful factor is still there. It's
like you're piloting a helicopter,

00:35:05.900 --> 00:35:09.666
and there are some red signals,
some red lamps start to blink

00:35:09.733 --> 00:35:13.300
and it starts producing bad, unpleasant

00:35:13.366 --> 00:35:16.833
noise. What would you do?
You would try to figure

00:35:16.900 --> 00:35:20.700
out the cause and eliminate
it. Maybe there is some

00:35:21.266 --> 00:35:25.100
mountain next to you and you have
to avoid it, or you take a hammer

00:35:25.133 --> 00:35:28.700
and smash the signal.

00:35:28.733 --> 00:35:32.633
I think the good answer is quite
