system administrators.

00:42:02.766 --> 00:42:10.200
- Can you speak to the philosophy behind
running a company with so few employees?

00:42:10.233 --> 00:42:12.566
- Well, what we realized
really early is that

00:42:14.300 --> 00:42:17.600
quantity of employees doesn't translate
to quality of the product they

00:42:17.633 --> 00:42:22.800
produce. In many cases, it's the opposite.

00:42:24.333 --> 00:42:28.333
If you have too many people, they have
to coordinate their efforts, constantly

00:42:28.366 --> 00:42:32.833
communicate, and 90% of
their time will be spent on

00:42:33.666 --> 00:42:39.766
coordinating the small pieces of work
they're responsible for between each other.

00:42:41.833 --> 00:42:45.766
The other problem with having
too many employees is that

00:42:47.166 --> 00:42:51.133
some of them won't get
enough work to do. And if

00:42:51.166 --> 00:42:54.966
they don't get enough work to do, they
demotivate everybody else by their mere

00:42:55.000 --> 00:42:58.500
existence. They're still there,
they're still getting the

00:42:58.533 --> 00:43:02.233
salary, but they don't do
anything. And if they don't do

00:43:02.300 --> 00:43:06.533
anything, more often than not,
they will start trying to

00:43:08.700 --> 00:43:12.800
find their purpose elsewhere,
maybe inside your team,

00:43:13.600 --> 00:43:20.633
but not by doing productive work, but
by finding problems that don't exist

00:43:21.700 --> 00:43:25.800
within the team. And that
can disrupt the team and

00:43:27.666 --> 00:43:32.900
the mood inside it even
further. Also, when you

00:43:34.133 --> 00:43:38.400
intentionally don't allow
some of your team members

00:43:39.233 --> 00:43:46.433
to hire more people to help them, they
will be forced to automate things. In

00:43:47.400 --> 00:43:51.233
our case, you know, we have

00:43:52.100 --> 00:43:58.533
tens of thousands of servers around the
world, almost 100,000, distributed across

00:43:59.600 --> 00:44:04.900
several continents and data centers.
If you try to manage this system

00:44:06.666 --> 00:44:09.333
manually without automation,

00:44:10.133 --> 00:44:14.033
you will probably end up hiring thousands
of people, tens of thousands of people.

00:44:14.066 --> 00:44:19.500
But if you rely on algorithms
and the team is forced to

00:44:19.533 --> 00:44:25.700
put together algorithms in order to
manage it, then it becomes much more

00:44:25.700 --> 00:44:30.633
scalable, and much more efficient, and
interestingly, much more reliable as well.

00:44:31.200 --> 00:44:34.833
- And more resilient to the
changing geopolitics, to

00:44:34.933 --> 00:44:39.100
the changing technology, all of
that. Because if you automate

00:44:40.233 --> 00:44:43.500
the distributed aspect of
the data storage and all the

00:44:43.533 --> 00:44:47.300
compute, then that's going to be resilient
to everything the world throws at

00:44:47.333 --> 00:44:54.166
you. I suppose if you have people managing
all of it, it becomes stale quickly.

00:44:54.266 --> 00:45:01.600
- Yes. Humans are attack vectors. And
if you have a distributed system

00:45:01.633 --> 00:45:05.533
that runs itself automatically, you have a

00:45:05.566 --> 00:45:09.433
a chance at increasing the security
and speed of your service.

00:45:09.600 --> 00:45:13.500
and speed of your service. This
