when we've been trying to come up with this design,

00:46:15.833 --> 00:46:19.633
been trying to come up with this design,
we always invested a lot of effort

00:46:19.666 --> 00:46:23.000
invested a lot of effort into making
sure that nobody can mess with it.

00:46:23.733 --> 00:46:27.366
into making sure that nobody can mess
with it. Like if you hire an employee

00:46:27.400 --> 00:46:29.833
employee or any of the existing
employee, they can't break the system

00:46:30.666 --> 00:46:33.933
they can't break the system in a way that
would allow them to access messages of users.

00:46:34.000 --> 00:46:37.833
access messages of users. And then of course,
we launched end-to-end encrypted messaging

00:46:38.266 --> 00:46:42.166
end-to-end encrypted messaging that is even
more protected, but it has certain limitations,

00:46:42.200 --> 00:46:45.800
but it has certain limitations, so you still have
to rely on encrypted cloud. So an interesting

00:46:45.866 --> 00:46:49.833
encrypted cloud. So an interesting

00:46:50.000 --> 00:46:53.733
engineering challenge was how do you make
sure that no point of failure can be created

00:46:54.200 --> 00:46:58.633
that no point of failure can be
created within your team or outside?

00:46:58.700 --> 00:47:02.533
- So no employee can even access user messages. So
that's the thing. You know, we talk about encryption,

00:47:02.533 --> 00:47:06.466
thing. You know, we talk about encryption, we talk about
privacy, we talk about security, all these kinds of things.

00:47:06.500 --> 00:47:10.500
all these kinds of things. I think the number one thing
that people are concerned about, about which there's also

00:47:10.566 --> 00:47:16.366
about which there's also misinformation, is about
private messages. So Telegram is very, very protective

00:47:16.400 --> 00:47:22.900
private messages. So Telegram is very, very protective
