They can't assemble. It's over.

00:53:39.000 --> 00:53:42.366
- So you see Telegram as a place
that people from all walks of

00:53:42.433 --> 00:53:45.033
life, from every nation can have a place

00:53:46.600 --> 00:53:50.066
to speak their mind, to
have a voice. In the

00:53:50.100 --> 00:53:54.000
context, in the geopolitical context
that you're mentioning that governments

00:53:54.033 --> 00:53:57.733
when they become autocratic,
naturally it's the way of the

00:53:57.766 --> 00:54:01.566
world, human nature and the nature of
governments, they become more censorious.

00:54:02.300 --> 00:54:06.200
They begin to censor, and
always justifying it in their

00:54:06.233 --> 00:54:08.766
minds perhaps assuming
that they are doing good.

00:54:08.800 --> 00:54:12.766
- Perhaps some of them assume
they are doing good, but

00:54:12.833 --> 00:54:20.200
interestingly, it always results in the
state accumulating more power at the

00:54:20.233 --> 00:54:27.700
expense of the individual. And then
where does it stop? You know, we

00:54:27.800 --> 00:54:30.733
humans are not very good
at finding the right

00:54:30.833 --> 00:54:34.700
balance, and in this case, the
right balance between chaos

00:54:34.733 --> 00:54:44.000
and order, between freedom and
structure. We tend to go to extremes.

00:54:44.033 --> 00:54:47.933
- I think you still consider yourself a
libertarian. There is something about

00:54:47.966 --> 00:54:54.366
government that always, over time,
naturally builds a larger and larger

00:54:54.466 --> 00:54:58.333
bureaucracy, and in that
machine of bureaucracy, it

00:54:58.400 --> 00:55:04.900
accumulates more and more power. And
it's not always that some one individual

00:55:04.966 --> 00:55:06.966
member of that bureaucracy

00:55:07.800 --> 00:55:11.633
is the one that corrupts the initial principles
on which the government was founded, but

00:55:11.700 --> 00:55:19.000
just something over time, you forget.
You begin to censor. You begin to limit

00:55:19.200 --> 00:55:23.000
the freedoms of the individual,
the ability of the individuals

00:55:23.233 --> 00:55:28.233
to speak, to have a voice, to vote.
It just gradually happens that way.

00:55:29.066 --> 00:55:32.366
- And the government is not some
abstract notion. The government

00:55:32.433 --> 00:55:39.700
consists of people, and these people
have goals. They would naturally be

00:55:39.733 --> 00:55:43.666
inclined to increase
their level of influence,

00:55:43.700 --> 00:55:47.333
to have more subordinates,
to have more resources,

00:55:48.400 --> 00:55:55.300
and that's how you end up in an endless
loop of, you know, ever-increasing

00:55:55.366 --> 00:56:03.233
taxes, ever-increasing regulation,
which ultimately just suffocates

00:56:03.900 --> 00:56:07.766
free market, free enterprise,
and free speech. So, you

00:56:07.800 --> 00:56:15.333
do want to have very, very strict
limitations on the extent the

00:56:15.400 --> 00:56:18.433
government can increase its
powers at the expense of

00:56:18.500 --> 00:56:22.500
citizens. Ironically, you
don't have those limitations.

00:56:22.533 --> 00:56:28.333
You're supposed to, in all countries
which are considered to be free.

00:56:29.900 --> 00:56:32.700
It's supposed to be the Constitution
that protects everybody,

00:56:34.500 --> 00:56:39.100
but interestingly, it doesn't always
work this way. They are able to find

00:56:40.900 --> 00:56:45.400
very tricky phrasings in order
to carve out exceptions,

00:56:46.800 --> 00:56:48.400
and then the exception becomes the rule.

00:56:49.233 --> 00:56:50.100
- On this topic,

00:56:51.933 --> 00:56:55.900
I'd love to talk to you about the
recent saga of you being arrested in

00:56:56.000 --> 00:56:59.633
August of last year in France.
I think I should say that

00:57:00.433 --> 00:57:08.066
it's one of the worst overreaches of power
I've seen as applied to a tech leader in

00:57:08.100 --> 00:57:11.233
recent history, in all history.

00:57:12.600 --> 00:57:16.533
So it's tragic, but I think
speaks to the thing that we've

00:57:16.533 --> 00:57:20.400
been talking about. So
maybe can you tell the full

00:57:20.500 --> 00:57:24.233
saga what happened? You
arrive in France...

00:57:24.300 --> 00:57:29.466
- I arrived in France last year in
August just for a short two-day trip,

00:57:31.500 --> 00:57:38.466
and then I see a dozen armed policemen
greeting me and asking me to follow

00:57:38.500 --> 00:57:50.133
them. They read me a list of something like
15 serious crimes that I'm accused of,

00:57:52.900 --> 00:58:00.533
which was mind-boggling. At first, I
thought there must be some mistake. Then I

00:58:00.566 --> 00:58:02.066
realized they're being serious,

00:58:03.633 --> 00:58:07.666
and they're accusing me of all possible
crimes that the users of Telegram have

00:58:09.066 --> 00:58:17.533
allegedly committed, or some users. And they
think I should be responsible for this,

00:58:18.433 --> 00:58:22.333
which again, like you said, is something
that never happened in the history

00:58:22.400 --> 00:58:26.500
of this planet. No country, not even an

00:58:28.033 --> 00:58:35.566
authoritarian one, did that
to any tech leader, at

00:58:35.600 --> 00:58:41.166
least at this scale. There are good reasons
for that, because you're sacrificing

00:58:42.100 --> 00:58:45.366
a big part of your economic growth
by sending these kinds of messages

00:58:46.666 --> 00:58:54.200
to the business and tech
community. So they put me in

00:58:54.233 --> 00:59:01.700
a police car, and I found
myself in police custody.

00:59:01.866 --> 00:59:10.466
A small room, no windows.
Just a narrow bed

00:59:11.300 --> 00:59:15.733
made of concrete. I spent
almost four days there.

00:59:17.066 --> 00:59:20.866
In the process, I had to
answer some questions of the

00:59:20.900 --> 00:59:29.866
policemen. They were interested
in how Telegram operates.

00:59:31.966 --> 00:59:37.033
Most of it is public
anyway, and I was struck by

00:59:39.233 --> 00:59:43.500
very limited understanding, or should
I say, even a lack of understanding

00:59:44.700 --> 00:59:51.100
on behalf of the people who initiated
this investigation against me about how

00:59:51.966 --> 00:59:57.600
technology works, how encryption
works, how social media work.

00:59:57.666 --> 01:00:01.066
- I mean, there's something darkly
poetic about a tech founder of a

01:00:01.100 --> 01:00:04.933
platform where a billion people are
communicating with each other, and

01:00:04.966 --> 01:00:08.866
you're on concrete, no pillow, for days,

01:00:08.900 --> 01:00:12.800
no windows. It's like a book. I mean,
it reminds me, I'm a huge fan of Franz

01:00:12.833 --> 01:00:16.166
Kafka, and he's written about
the absurdity of these kinds of

01:00:16.200 --> 01:00:20.166
situations, hence the Kafkaesque
stories. There's a story

01:00:20.233 --> 01:00:24.200
literally about the situation
that he wrote, perhaps predicted,

01:00:24.233 --> 01:00:27.766
called "The Trial," where a
person is arrested for no

01:00:27.833 --> 01:00:30.766
reason that anybody can explain
and is stuck in the judicial

01:00:30.833 --> 01:00:38.366
system for a long time. Fascinatingly,
in that story, neither the person

01:00:38.366 --> 01:00:42.266
arrested nor any individual
member of the system itself

01:00:42.533 --> 01:00:47.000
fully understands what is happening.
Nobody can truly answer the questions,

01:00:47.966 --> 01:00:51.900
and eventually the person,
spoiler alert, is mentally broken

01:00:51.966 --> 01:00:55.833
by the whole system, which is
what bureaucracy can do in

01:00:55.866 --> 01:01:01.400
its most absurd forms. It breaks the spirit,
the human spirit latent in all of us.

01:01:02.833 --> 01:01:05.166
That's the negative side of bureaucracy.

01:01:05.266 --> 01:01:14.200
- I agree with you on the absurdity of
this thing, because if this was a

01:01:15.000 --> 01:01:17.366
good faith attempt to fix an issue,

01:01:19.266 --> 01:01:23.233
there were so many ways
to reach out to Telegram,

01:01:23.233 --> 01:01:28.400
to reach out to me personally,
voice their concerns, and solve

01:01:29.500 --> 01:01:36.366
any alleged problem in a way that is
conventional and diplomatic, the way every

01:01:36.600 --> 01:01:40.300
other country on this
planet solves its problems,

01:01:40.366 --> 01:01:43.433
including with Telegram, and
we did it dozens of times.

01:01:43.500 --> 01:01:47.466
- Yeah, you have a nice page showing this.
This is kind of like details that most

01:01:47.500 --> 01:01:54.666
people don't really think about. But
Telegram was at the forefront of

01:01:55.900 --> 01:02:02.766
moderating CSAM and terrorist groups. There's
a nice page, telegram.org/moderation,

01:02:02.800 --> 01:02:06.433
that shows just the incredible
amount of groups and

01:02:06.500 --> 01:02:10.333
channels that are engaged in
terrorist activity and CSAM

01:02:10.566 --> 01:02:14.333
activity that are blocked,
actively blocked, found and

01:02:14.400 --> 01:02:18.300
blocked by Telegram. And a lot of this work,
like you said, because of the automation

01:02:18.300 --> 01:02:22.200
that's done with machine learning,
just the scale is insane. This

01:02:22.266 --> 01:02:26.233
is stuff that most noobs like me who are
just chatting it up on Telegram don't

01:02:26.266 --> 01:02:29.433
think about. But there's
just an immense number of

01:02:31.400 --> 01:02:35.266
people essentially doing
things that violate the

01:02:35.300 --> 01:02:39.233
law on there, and you have to find
them immediately and catch it. I guess

01:02:39.300 --> 01:02:42.833
all platforms have to deal with it,
and Telegram was doing a great job of

01:02:43.566 --> 01:02:47.700
dealing with that kind of content. And what
you're saying is the French government

01:02:49.000 --> 01:02:51.500
had no idea.

01:02:51.700 --> 01:02:53.233
- Do they even know what
machine learning is?

01:02:53.700 --> 01:02:57.400
- It's a concept that is
challenging to explain to them,

01:02:57.500 --> 01:03:01.200
but I think they will learn much
more about it by the end of this

01:03:01.233 --> 01:03:02.633
investigation. That's my hope.

01:03:03.766 --> 01:03:08.400
In any case, you're right. I mean, if you
look at Telegram, we've been fighting

01:03:09.800 --> 01:03:14.466
harmful content that is publicly
distributed on our platform

01:03:15.333 --> 01:03:19.200
since 10 years ago, actually, since the

01:03:19.300 --> 01:03:23.766
time we launched public
channels on Telegram. And since

01:03:24.700 --> 01:03:29.666
something like eight years ago,
we had daily transparency reports

01:03:31.666 --> 01:03:38.400
on how many channels related to
child abuse or terrorist propaganda

01:03:39.466 --> 01:03:46.433
we've taken down daily. Every day, we've
taken like, maybe hundreds of them.

01:03:47.633 --> 01:03:54.833
And if you include all kinds of content
that we remove, all the accounts,

01:03:54.866 --> 01:03:59.166
groups, channels, posts,
that would amount to

01:04:00.100 --> 01:04:04.900
millions of pieces of content every
week, hundreds of thousands every day.

01:04:05.766 --> 01:04:09.366
And then somebody would read
the newspaper, get enraged

01:04:09.433 --> 01:04:15.600
because they would read something about
child porn, and this is a subject that

01:04:16.600 --> 01:04:24.800
is very emotionally charged, and start
doing something not based on data

01:04:26.500 --> 01:04:35.700
and logical thinking and laws, but based
on emotions driven from inaccurate input.

01:04:36.133 --> 01:04:39.966
- Yeah, I think we should make it pretty clear
that there's no world, no reason that the

01:04:40.000 --> 01:04:43.366
French government should have arrested you,
but here we are. That's the situation you're

01:04:43.433 --> 01:04:47.200
in. So to be clear, you have
to show up in front of a

01:04:47.300 --> 01:04:51.233
judge. Now all of this is beautifully
absurd. It would be hilarious if it wasn't

01:04:51.300 --> 01:04:54.533
extremely serious. You have to
show up in front of a judge

01:04:55.933 --> 01:05:01.766
every certain amount of time. And
what is that experience like?

01:05:01.833 --> 01:05:05.566
- In France, they have this role of
investigative judge. I don't think you have it

01:05:06.000 --> 01:05:10.133
in many other places in the
world. It means I'm not on trial,

01:05:11.200 --> 01:05:12.733
I'm being investigated.

01:05:14.366 --> 01:05:18.166
And in France, it's not just the
police or prosecutor asking me

01:05:18.233 --> 01:05:23.433
questions, it's a judge, which,
in my experience, is more like

01:05:24.400 --> 01:05:32.133
still a prosecutor, but it's called a judge,
and that makes it harder to appeal. So

01:05:32.333 --> 01:05:35.800
if you're limited in, say,
countries where you can

01:05:35.833 --> 01:05:39.700
travel, then to appeal that restriction
will take you a lot of time.

01:05:39.733 --> 01:05:49.666
The investigation itself should have never
been started. It's an absurd and harmful

01:05:51.500 --> 01:05:57.366
way of solving an issue as complicated as

01:05:58.966 --> 01:06:03.033
regulating social media.
It's just the wrong tool.

01:06:04.300 --> 01:06:10.600
So we objected and
appealed the investigation

01:06:10.633 --> 01:06:15.933
itself. We did last year, I
believe. We're still not even given

01:06:17.766 --> 01:06:25.033
a hearing date for the appeal, because
the process is painfully slow,

01:06:26.900 --> 01:06:30.733
not just for me but for
everybody, which made

01:06:30.800 --> 01:06:35.866
me realize the system may be
broken on many levels. You have

01:06:36.933 --> 01:06:41.266
other entrepreneurs affected
by the French justice system

01:06:42.633 --> 01:06:49.833
telling me horror stories about
their experiences, where businesses

01:06:50.700 --> 01:06:58.500
got paralyzed by very unnecessary actions
of investigative judges that ended

01:06:58.600 --> 01:07:06.533
up being unjustified and biased. And in
the end, you can perhaps solve it when

01:07:06.600 --> 01:07:09.933
you reach a higher court

01:07:11.233 --> 01:07:17.533
and you'll get justice, but you'll lose
a lot of time and energy in the process.

01:07:18.933 --> 01:07:22.366
So this is the only thing
that is, I hope, different,

01:07:23.633 --> 01:07:31.000
and will be different in this case
compared to the story you told from Kafka.

01:07:31.066 --> 01:07:34.566
- I mean, but it does, as Kafka
describes, break a lot of people

01:07:34.966 --> 01:07:38.800
with time. So when do you hope...
We should say that you were

01:07:38.833 --> 01:07:42.033
for a long time not allowed
to travel out of France. Now

01:07:43.400 --> 01:07:47.233
you can travel to Dubai.
We're now in Dubai. Got to

01:07:47.333 --> 01:07:51.033
meet many of the people
that work at Telegram.

01:07:51.266 --> 01:07:54.900
Telegram is headquartered in Dubai. But
you're not allowed to travel anywhere

01:07:54.966 --> 01:08:01.233
else. When do you think you're coming to
Texas to hang out with me over there?

01:08:01.300 --> 01:08:07.033
- That's a hard question to answer because
it doesn't depend on just my actions.

01:08:08.300 --> 01:08:19.533
I can just say this: I am patient. I
will not let this limitation on my

01:08:21.600 --> 01:08:31.800
freedom dictate my actions. I will,
if anything, double down on defending

01:08:31.833 --> 01:08:40.400
freedoms because I experienced firsthand
what the absence of freedom feels like, at

01:08:40.466 --> 01:08:45.433
least during those four days in
police custody when you are stuck,

01:08:47.566 --> 01:08:54.000
just stuck, unable to
communicate with people

01:08:55.200 --> 01:09:00.300
that are important to you. When you don't
even know what's going on in the world

01:09:02.400 --> 01:09:06.333
in relation to you personally.
So I have no crystal ball that

01:09:06.400 --> 01:09:09.800
would tell me the future.
I can't say that I'm

01:09:09.833 --> 01:09:15.033
pessimistic. I think we've
been able to gradually remove

01:09:16.200 --> 01:09:23.566
most of the restrictions initially
imposed on my freedom last August.

01:09:23.666 --> 01:09:26.600
- If the French government or
the French intelligence agency

01:09:27.800 --> 01:09:32.533
want to have a backdoor to
access private user messages,

01:09:34.033 --> 01:09:37.833
what would you say to them? Is there

01:09:37.866 --> 01:09:42.933
anything they can do to get access
to the private user messages?

01:09:43.000 --> 01:09:50.700
- Nothing. My response would
be very clear... but it

01:09:50.766 --> 01:09:52.766
won't be very polite, so I'm not sure.

01:09:52.800 --> 01:09:55.500
- It's good to say here.

01:09:55.500 --> 01:09:57.933
- It's good to say because
you're wearing a tie and-

01:09:58.033 --> 01:10:01.266
- Yeah. This is a serious
adult gentleman-like

01:10:01.333 --> 01:10:05.233
program. But it is a concern
that people have is when

01:10:05.366 --> 01:10:09.200
you have so much pressure from
governments, that over time, they'll

01:10:09.266 --> 01:10:14.733
wear you down and you'll give in.
And then, of course, other places

01:10:15.833 --> 01:10:19.733
use that as propaganda, try to attack you.
You get attacked by basically every nation.

01:10:19.766 --> 01:10:22.800
So it's a difficult

01:10:23.166 --> 01:10:26.866
medium in which to operate. It's
difficult to be you, fighting for

01:10:26.866 --> 01:10:30.733
freedom, fighting to preserve people's
privacy. But is there something you could say

01:10:30.766 --> 01:10:34.500
to reassure people that
you're not going to sacrifice

01:10:35.066 --> 01:10:38.933
any of the principles that
you've just expressed? If the

01:10:38.966 --> 01:10:42.133
French government just
keeps wearing you down?

01:10:42.200 --> 01:10:47.466
- I think the French government is losing
this battle. This battle is wrong.

01:10:48.633 --> 01:10:59.300
The more pressure I get, the more
resilient and defiant I become.

01:11:00.200 --> 01:11:03.166
And I think I have proven
that in the last several

01:11:03.233 --> 01:11:10.900
months, when there were attempts to use my
situation, being stuck here in France, by

01:11:10.933 --> 01:11:14.000
approaching me and asking me to do things

01:11:14.833 --> 01:11:17.300
in other countries,
blocking certain channels,

01:11:19.733 --> 01:11:23.233
changing the way Telegram
works. And not only I

01:11:23.266 --> 01:11:28.666
refused, I told the world about it, and
I'm going to keep telling the world

01:11:29.566 --> 01:11:31.066
about every instance

01:11:33.133 --> 01:11:36.666
any government, in this case, in
particular, the French government,

01:11:38.433 --> 01:11:41.466
tries to force me to do anything.

01:11:42.800 --> 01:11:46.666
And I would rather lose
everything I have than yield to

01:11:46.700 --> 01:11:50.233
this pressure, because if you

01:11:50.333 --> 01:11:54.133
submit to this pressure and agree
with something that is fundamentally

01:11:54.166 --> 01:11:58.233
wrong and violates the rights
of other people as well,

01:12:00.100 --> 01:12:04.633
you become broken inside. You
become a shell of your former self

01:12:06.500 --> 01:12:09.500
on a deep biological and spiritual level.

01:12:10.866 --> 01:12:14.300
So, I wouldn't do that. There are probably
other people in the world that would

01:12:15.033 --> 01:12:20.733
consider that, but I don't care. Telegram
disappears, too. Something people don't

01:12:20.766 --> 01:12:27.533
understand, including in these intelligence
services or governments. I don't

01:12:27.600 --> 01:12:37.433
care. I'll be fine. If they put
me into prison for 20 years,

01:12:37.500 --> 01:12:42.933
which, let's be clear, it's not something
that I think is realistic, but let's

01:12:45.033 --> 01:12:50.133
just think about it as a
hypothetical situation,

01:12:51.466 --> 01:12:56.833
I would rather starve myself to death
and die there, reboot the whole game,

01:12:58.533 --> 01:12:59.833
than do something stupid.

01:13:01.466 --> 01:13:05.233
- Let me ask you about an example of the thing
you're talking about. Tell the saga of

01:13:05.266 --> 01:13:08.666
Telegram in the Romanian
election. So, amidst all

01:13:08.733 --> 01:13:12.066
this, you are still fighting
to preserve the freedom of

01:13:12.166 --> 01:13:16.266
speech. What happened, and what were
some of the decisions you had to make?

01:13:16.300 --> 01:13:25.066
- So, when I got stuck in France, unable to
leave the country for a few months, I was

01:13:25.166 --> 01:13:31.233
offered to meet the head of state foreign
intelligence services through a person I

01:13:32.233 --> 01:13:33.866
know quite well. He's actually a

01:13:35.466 --> 01:13:39.333
well-known tech entrepreneur in France,
and he's well-connected, and he

01:13:39.433 --> 01:13:43.400
said, "This guy wants to meet you." I
said, "Okay, fine. Let's do that, but I'm

01:13:43.433 --> 01:13:48.500
not promising anything." I
took the meeting, and in this

01:13:48.633 --> 01:13:57.400
meeting, I was asked to restrict what
I see as restriction of freedom of

01:13:57.466 --> 01:14:05.966
speech in Romania. I don't know if you
follow the whole saga with the Romanian

01:14:05.966 --> 01:14:09.933
elections. They had presidential
elections last year.

01:14:09.966 --> 01:14:13.200
The results were- got canceled.

01:14:13.266 --> 01:14:17.233
Now, Romania, at that point when I
had this meeting, was preparing for

01:14:17.266 --> 01:14:23.900
a new presidential election. The
conservative candidate was not somebody who

01:14:24.700 --> 01:14:28.733
the French government was
supportive of, so they asked me

01:14:29.900 --> 01:14:34.133
whether I would be shutting
down, or ready to shut down,

01:14:35.666 --> 01:14:41.933
channels on Telegram that supported
the conservative candidate, or protest

01:14:42.000 --> 01:14:46.733
against the pro-European
candidates, so they

01:14:46.766 --> 01:14:50.666
called the guy they liked. I said, "Look,

01:14:50.733 --> 01:14:54.366
if there is no violation of the
rules of Telegram, which are quite

01:14:54.433 --> 01:15:00.233
clear, you can't call to
violence. But if it's a peaceful

01:15:00.266 --> 01:15:07.166
demonstration, if it's a peaceful debate,
we can't do this. It would be political

01:15:07.233 --> 01:15:11.066
censorship. We protected
freedom of speech in

01:15:11.133 --> 01:15:15.033
many countries in the world,
including in Asia, in Eastern

01:15:15.133 --> 01:15:20.766
Europe, in the Middle East. We're not
going to start engaging in censorship in

01:15:21.300 --> 01:15:25.166
Europe, no matter who's asking

01:15:25.233 --> 01:15:29.066
us." I was very clear to the guy who
was the head of French intelligence.

01:15:30.366 --> 01:15:35.933
I said, "If you think that because I'm
stuck here, you can tell me what to

01:15:35.966 --> 01:15:42.200
do, you're very wrong. I would
rather do the opposite every

01:15:42.233 --> 01:15:53.033
time." And in a way, that's what I
did. I um... had a small debate with

01:15:53.066 --> 01:15:56.966
him about the morality of this,
this whole thing, and then

01:15:57.000 --> 01:16:00.966
at a certain point, just
disclosed the content of this

01:16:00.966 --> 01:16:04.866
entire conversation, because I never
signed an NDA. I don't ever sign

01:16:04.933 --> 01:16:10.433
NDAs with any people like that. I want to
be able to tell the world what's going on.

01:16:12.933 --> 01:16:16.766
And that's quite shocking to me,

01:16:17.566 --> 01:16:24.200
that you would have people in the French
government trying to get an advantage

01:16:25.333 --> 01:16:29.300
of this situation. Of course,
if, you know, they had nothing

01:16:29.333 --> 01:16:33.366
to do with the start of
this investigation itself,

01:16:35.966 --> 01:16:45.800
and use it to reach their political
or geopolitical goals. I consider it

01:16:47.200 --> 01:16:49.833
an attempt to humiliate
myself personally and

01:16:51.266 --> 01:16:55.066
millions of Telegram users
collectively. And it's quite

01:16:55.166 --> 01:16:59.133
strange that the same agency asked us to
do certain things in Moldova as well.

01:16:59.166 --> 01:17:03.100
do certain things in Moldova as
well. So even before that, I

01:17:03.200 --> 01:17:06.733
think it was October of
last year, or September. I

01:17:06.833 --> 01:17:10.766
was arrested in Paris in late August,

01:17:12.866 --> 01:17:16.633
and then again approached through an
intermediary, and asked, "Would you

01:17:16.766 --> 01:17:22.833
mind taking down some channels in Moldova?
Because there is an election going on,

01:17:23.666 --> 01:17:29.266
and we're afraid there's going to be
some interference with these elections.

01:17:30.900 --> 01:17:38.333
Could you please connect with the
representatives of the government of Moldova

01:17:39.200 --> 01:17:44.200
and take care of it?" We said, "We're
happy to take a look at it and see if

01:17:45.766 --> 01:17:48.666
there is content there that is in violation
of our rules." And they sent us a list

01:17:48.733 --> 01:17:52.033
rules." And they sent us a
list of channels and bots.

01:17:52.066 --> 01:17:56.000
bots. Some of them were... So
it was a very short list, and

01:17:56.033 --> 01:17:59.966
some of these channels and bots were

01:18:00.000 --> 01:18:05.000
in violation indeed of our rules, and
we took them down, only a few of them.

01:18:06.400 --> 01:18:11.833
The rest were okay. Then they said,
"Thank you," and sent us another list of

01:18:12.866 --> 01:18:15.500
dozens of channels, many, many channels.

01:18:16.400 --> 01:18:20.366
We looked at these channels, we realized
that there is no solid foundation

01:18:22.600 --> 01:18:29.133
to justify banning them, and we
refused to do that. But interestingly

01:18:29.166 --> 01:18:44.966
enough, the French intelligence services
that were asking us to do this in Moldova

01:18:47.800 --> 01:18:51.233
let me know through their contact

01:18:52.466 --> 01:18:58.133
that after Telegram banned
the few channels that were in

01:18:58.166 --> 01:19:03.833
violation of our rules in
Moldova, they talked to my

01:19:03.966 --> 01:19:07.666
judge, the investigative judge
in this investigation that is

01:19:08.266 --> 01:19:10.600
started, is has been started against me,

01:19:12.433 --> 01:19:17.433
and told the judge good things
about me, which I found very

01:19:17.466 --> 01:19:22.700
confusing, and in a way shocking, because

01:19:23.833 --> 01:19:28.333
these two matters have
nothing in common. Why would

01:19:29.166 --> 01:19:36.433
anyone talk to an investigative judge that
is trying to find out whether Telegram

01:19:37.066 --> 01:19:40.366
did a good enough job in

01:19:41.866 --> 01:19:50.166
removing illegal content in France?
What does Moldova have to do with it?

01:19:50.233 --> 01:19:55.500
I got very suspicious at that moment.
Remember, it happened after we

01:19:56.933 --> 01:20:00.666
blocked a few channels that
violated our rules, but

01:20:00.766 --> 01:20:04.633
before we refused to block a
long list of other channels

01:20:05.033 --> 01:20:08.866
that were completely fine, which
is people expressing political

01:20:08.966 --> 01:20:15.600
views, which I may not agree with,
but it's their right to express them.

01:20:16.400 --> 01:20:22.033
Not extreme views, not views
that call to violence.

01:20:23.833 --> 01:20:29.333
That was extremely alarming.
That was a moment when

01:20:30.933 --> 01:20:34.800
I told myself that there may be more

01:20:35.800 --> 01:20:38.966
going on here that I initially
thought. Initially, I thought,

01:20:40.700 --> 01:20:46.766
"Yeah, some people are confused about
how technology works." And here,

01:20:48.000 --> 01:20:50.666
after this case in Moldova,

01:20:52.900 --> 01:20:59.266
I got much more suspicious. So by
the time the head of intelligence

01:21:00.166 --> 01:21:06.366
services met me to ask about Romania,
to help them silencing conservative

01:21:06.433 --> 01:21:18.100
voices in Romania, I was already
wary of what could be going on next.

01:21:18.133 --> 01:21:21.700
- Yeah, so clearly this was a systematic

01:21:21.966 --> 01:21:25.800
attempt to pressure you to censor
political voices that the French

01:21:25.833 --> 01:21:29.666
government doesn't agree with. And we
should say that you have fought for

01:21:29.766 --> 01:21:35.566
freedom of speech for left-wing groups
and right-wing groups, it really doesn't

01:21:35.633 --> 01:21:39.233
matter. So it's not, you don't have a

01:21:39.266 --> 01:21:43.300
political affiliation, political
ideology that you fight for. You're

01:21:44.266 --> 01:21:50.233
creating a platform that, as long as
they don't call for violence, allows

01:21:50.266 --> 01:21:54.233
people from all walks of life, from all
ideologies to speak their mind. That's the whole

01:21:54.300 --> 01:21:58.100
point. And it happens to be
conservative voices in the Romanian

01:21:58.133 --> 01:22:01.933
election that the French government
wanted to censor, because currently the

01:22:01.933 --> 01:22:04.900
French government leans left. But
if you flip everything around

01:22:05.766 --> 01:22:09.433
and the government would be
right-wing, you'd be fighting against

01:22:09.433 --> 01:22:13.466
censorship of left-wing voices. And
you have in the past, many times.

01:22:13.900 --> 01:22:17.700
- Exactly. Ironically, we
received a request from the

01:22:17.733 --> 01:22:23.600
French police to take down a channel
of far-left protesters on Telegram

01:22:24.700 --> 01:22:32.200
in France. We refused to do that. We looked
at the channel, peaceful protesters.

01:22:33.600 --> 01:22:38.333
It doesn't matter for us whether we're
defending the freedom of speech of people

01:22:39.133 --> 01:22:44.700
leaning right or leaning left.
During COVID, we were protecting

01:22:46.900 --> 01:22:51.433
activists that were organizing
the Black Lives Matter

01:22:53.100 --> 01:22:58.866
events, and the other side,
the protesters against

01:22:59.633 --> 01:23:04.266
lockdowns. We protect
everybody as long as they are

01:23:05.600 --> 01:23:10.100
not crossing the lines and not starting to

01:23:12.133 --> 01:23:18.000
call to violence or
incite damage to public

01:23:18.100 --> 01:23:25.233
property. It's a fundamental right
to assemble. It's interesting that

01:23:28.133 --> 01:23:33.566
people who haven't had this
experience of living in countries

01:23:34.433 --> 01:23:38.766
that don't have freedoms
don't always realize

01:23:40.366 --> 01:23:45.833
how dangerous it is to
gradually compromise

01:23:48.233 --> 01:23:51.733
your values, your principles,
your freedoms, your rights,

01:23:53.833 --> 01:23:56.500
because they don't
understand what's at stake.

01:23:56.533 --> 01:24:00.400
- Yeah, these things become a
slippery slope. So for many,

01:24:00.433 --> 01:24:05.200
many years, including currently, you have
spoken very highly of France. You love

01:24:06.166 --> 01:24:13.700
French history, French culture. I think
this situation, this historic wrong that's

01:24:13.733 --> 01:24:20.733
been done is simply just
a gigantic PR mistake

01:24:21.200 --> 01:24:25.133
for France. There's no
entrepreneur that aspires to

01:24:25.166 --> 01:24:29.033
be the next Pavel Durov to create
the next Telegram, sees this

01:24:29.233 --> 01:24:32.800
and wants to operate in France
after seeing this. There is

01:24:33.733 --> 01:24:37.500
no justification for this arrest,
there's a misapplication of the law, all

01:24:37.533 --> 01:24:39.266
kinds of pressures, all kinds of

01:24:40.100 --> 01:24:44.033
behavior that seems politically motivated,
all that kind of stuff, all the excessive

01:24:44.066 --> 01:24:47.866
regulation and bureaucracy. A
nightmare for entrepreneurs that

01:24:47.900 --> 01:24:51.833
dream to create something impactful
and positive for the world. So what do

01:24:51.900 --> 01:24:55.400
you think needs to be fixed about
the French government, the French

01:24:55.433 --> 01:24:59.133
system? And then zooming out, because
you have seen similar kinds of things in

01:24:59.200 --> 01:25:04.000
Europe that could enable
entrepreneurs, that could

01:25:04.100 --> 01:25:08.000
reverse the trend that we
seem to be seeing in Europe

01:25:08.033 --> 01:25:13.166
that is becoming less and less
friendly to entrepreneurs.

01:25:15.600 --> 01:25:18.533
What can be fixed? What should be fixed?

01:25:20.733 --> 01:25:30.633
- I think the European society must decide
where they want the ever-increasing

01:25:32.166 --> 01:25:36.600
public sector to stop
increasing, what they think

01:25:38.100 --> 01:25:43.700
should be the right size of government.
Because today, if you take France, for

01:25:43.733 --> 01:25:50.000
example, which is a beautiful country
with a lot of talented people, but

01:25:50.900 --> 01:25:56.566
public expenses are 58% of
the country's GDP. It's

01:25:58.400 --> 01:26:04.200
maybe as much or more than in the
latest stage of the Soviet Union.

01:26:04.300 --> 01:26:13.033
So you have this balance
where you have many more

01:26:14.000 --> 01:26:20.400
people representing the state as opposed
to people trying to bring the country's

01:26:20.433 --> 01:26:26.533
economy forward by creating great
products and great companies.

01:26:26.666 --> 01:26:32.800
The startup field, in my field, social media
field, has been affected by it immensely.

01:26:34.000 --> 01:26:43.300
There was one great startup in this realm
in France in the last 10 years. It was

01:26:43.333 --> 01:26:47.200
a location-based social
network. It was eventually sold

01:26:47.233 --> 01:26:51.066
to Snapchat, but before it was
sold, the founder asked me

01:26:51.800 --> 01:26:54.600
whether he should sell. I told him, "Never

01:26:54.733 --> 01:26:58.700
sell. You have a great thing
going. You have lots of users.

01:26:58.733 --> 01:27:02.666
You have organic traction
in many countries."

01:27:02.666 --> 01:27:07.166
And the first of this kind
of success story in France.

01:27:09.433 --> 01:27:13.300
But then he sold anyway in a
couple weeks. And later, I met

01:27:13.400 --> 01:27:17.366
him, he's trying to do a new thing
now. I met him and I asked him,

01:27:17.533 --> 01:27:22.700
I was trying to understand what went wrong.
And one of the things he told me about

01:27:23.833 --> 01:27:31.400
is that while he was trying to run his
company, you know, competing with Facebook,

01:27:31.500 --> 01:27:34.833
Instagram, Snapchat, having
all this pressure from

01:27:34.866 --> 01:27:40.333
investors, trying to hire the best
people and persuade them to go to Paris,

01:27:42.300 --> 01:27:46.033
and he did a great job, by the way.
But while he was trying to do that,

01:27:47.133 --> 01:27:57.166
he got also attacked by some silly
investigation, again involving data protection

01:27:57.266 --> 01:28:04.933
issues, which lasted forever, and
was gradually sucking the blood of

01:28:04.966 --> 01:28:10.166
his team and his company,
constant interrogations,

01:28:11.466 --> 01:28:15.666
disclosure requests. And
this is a young company.

01:28:17.300 --> 01:28:21.633
It significantly increases the
level of stress, and at some

01:28:21.666 --> 01:28:28.066
point, I think the pressure was too much.
He decided, "I'm going to just sell

01:28:28.133 --> 01:28:32.666
it." Eventually, it turned out that

01:28:34.233 --> 01:28:37.766
there was no issue. The investigation
ended, as far as I understand,

01:28:37.800 --> 01:28:43.700
with no charges. But such investigations,
they have a price, they have a

01:28:43.733 --> 01:28:49.966
cost. And unless the
society realizes the cost

01:28:51.033 --> 01:28:54.533
of projects, of companies, of startups

01:28:55.033 --> 01:28:58.833
that are never created, or are sold to
the United States at the very early

01:28:58.900 --> 01:29:06.333
stage, or other countries, resulting
in decreased economic growth,

01:29:06.366 --> 01:29:12.433
things won't change. I think we
just talked to a guy a few days ago

01:29:14.333 --> 01:29:18.033
who left France and started a business
here in Dubai, and one of the

01:29:18.066 --> 01:29:22.766
reasons he had to leave France is that
the government started an investigation

01:29:24.933 --> 01:29:29.633
on his company, and they froze his
bank accounts, and this investigation

01:29:31.033 --> 01:29:34.600
that involved taxes lasted for many,
many years. I believe he said eight

01:29:34.700 --> 01:29:37.466
years. And at the end of these eight

01:29:37.566 --> 01:29:41.400
years, the government reached
the conclusion that there

01:29:41.433 --> 01:29:47.066
was nothing wrong. "He's good.
It's okay." In the meantime,

01:29:48.500 --> 01:29:53.633
his corporate bank accounts
were frozen. His business died.

01:29:55.933 --> 01:30:00.033
The only reason why he was able

01:30:02.066 --> 01:30:05.766
to retain sanity is because he
moved to Dubai and started a new

01:30:05.833 --> 01:30:08.600
company, which is incredibly
successful, and now he's

01:30:10.066 --> 01:30:16.066
enriching this city, which we're in right
now, with his great ideas and creativity.

01:30:17.200 --> 01:30:20.666
- And by the way, having interacted
with him, there's like a

01:30:20.933 --> 01:30:23.833
fire in his eyes, the
human spirit that fuels

01:30:23.866 --> 01:30:27.633
entrepreneurship. Whatever that is, he
doesn't have to do. He's made a lot of

01:30:27.666 --> 01:30:31.533
money. He probably doesn't have to do
anything, but he still wants to create.

01:30:31.633 --> 01:30:35.433
And that fire's what fuels great
nations. Build, build, build,

01:30:35.466 --> 01:30:40.100
build new stuff, expand, all of that,
and regulation suffocates that.

01:30:40.133 --> 01:30:41.500
- You have to cherish those people.

01:30:42.333 --> 01:30:46.200
But I guess the French public, or
some part of the French public, was

01:30:46.233 --> 01:30:50.133
misled, and I don't know
when, perhaps since

01:30:50.166 --> 01:30:53.233
the time of the French
Revolution, to believe

01:30:53.333 --> 01:30:59.333
that entrepreneurs are somehow their
enemies. They're the evil rich people

01:31:00.133 --> 01:31:05.433
that are the cause of all
problems, as if only you could

01:31:06.833 --> 01:31:12.966
make the rich share their
ill-gotten wealth with the rest

01:31:13.866 --> 01:31:16.666
of the population, then every
problem will be magically

01:31:16.733 --> 01:31:20.700
solved. In reality though, a
lot of these people, that are

01:31:20.733 --> 01:31:24.666
starting such companies with
fire in their eyes, are

01:31:24.800 --> 01:31:28.766
sacrificing their lives, their
livelihood. They're working 20 hours

01:31:28.800 --> 01:31:35.966
a day. They're experiencing immense
stress in order to fulfill their vision

01:31:36.933 --> 01:31:40.866
and bring value and good to
the society around them. They

01:31:40.966 --> 01:31:44.333
create jobs. They create great
services. They create great

01:31:44.433 --> 01:31:48.000
goods. They make your country
grow. They make your people

01:31:48.133 --> 01:31:52.100
proud. You have to cherish them. But

01:31:52.133 --> 01:31:59.900
what does the system do to them? It
squeezes them out, because, perhaps there

01:32:00.033 --> 01:32:09.066
was somebody in the tax authority that
decided to advance their career, and

01:32:09.133 --> 01:32:12.600
perhaps, you know, was
too ambitious and not too

01:32:12.700 --> 01:32:15.466
smart, so as a result, the company was

01:32:15.500 --> 01:32:19.266
destroyed. And now the same
entrepreneur, by the way, who we

01:32:19.366 --> 01:32:23.300
talked to, is invited to
come back to France. He's

01:32:23.366 --> 01:32:26.866
being offered really good terms. He
said, "Are you gonna open this new

01:32:26.966 --> 01:32:30.366
venue on the Champs-Élysées?
We're gonna give you the best

01:32:30.433 --> 01:32:34.266
location. We're gonna fund
part of it, tax breaks."

01:32:34.300 --> 01:32:38.266
And he said, "Never.
Just forget about it."

01:32:38.266 --> 01:32:41.766
"It's impossible. I'm not
coming back to France."

01:32:41.866 --> 01:32:45.766
He's traumatized by the experience,
and he's French. He was

01:32:45.800 --> 01:32:49.400
born there. He has a French passport. So,

01:32:49.466 --> 01:32:53.300
unless things like this
change, France will,

01:32:53.366 --> 01:32:57.533
and the rest of Europe, will keep
struggling with economic growth,

01:32:58.533 --> 01:33:04.966
with budget deficits, with unemployment,
and all the other relevant social and

01:33:05.000 --> 01:33:06.333
economic metrics.

01:33:06.400 --> 01:33:10.333
- Yeah, it's heartbreaking.
As many of these nations, I

01:33:10.366 --> 01:33:14.300
appreciate the historic and the
cultural value, and I hope Europe

01:33:15.400 --> 01:33:19.333
and France flourish, but these are
not the components required for

01:33:19.366 --> 01:33:26.133
flourishing. Quick pause, I need a
bathroom break. All right, we had

01:33:26.133 --> 01:33:29.966
some tea. We're back.

01:33:30.000 --> 01:33:33.966
Let's go back a bunch of years to the
beginning. You mentioned you went

01:33:34.000 --> 01:33:37.833
to school with a super intensive
education. So, I thought it'd be really

01:33:37.866 --> 01:33:41.566
interesting to look at some of
the powerful aspects of that

01:33:41.600 --> 01:33:44.433
education, from the languages
to the math. Can you actually

01:33:44.466 --> 01:33:48.466
describe some of the rigorous aspects
of it and what you gained from it?

01:33:48.466 --> 01:33:52.266
- At the age of 11, I got
the opportunity to enter

01:33:52.933 --> 01:33:56.566
an experimental school in
St. Petersburg where I

01:33:56.566 --> 01:34:01.100
lived, and we had to pass a
rigorous test to get accepted.

01:34:02.166 --> 01:34:09.733
The idea behind the school was
that if you try to squeeze as

01:34:09.766 --> 01:34:16.100
much information as possible
into a brain of a teenager,

01:34:16.366 --> 01:34:23.266
making a focus on math and foreign
languages, then there will be

01:34:23.433 --> 01:34:27.266
some changes in the brain
of the student that will

01:34:27.333 --> 01:34:34.566
allow the student to understand most
other disciplines. But we had a

01:34:34.666 --> 01:34:38.533
class as a result that didn't
have any single focus. It was

01:34:38.900 --> 01:34:42.800
very widespread across
a lot of disciplines.

01:34:42.866 --> 01:34:48.433
You would have at least four foreign
languages, including Latin, English, French,

01:34:48.733 --> 01:34:52.066
German. In addition, you can get Ancient

01:34:52.166 --> 01:34:58.766
Greek. You would have classes like
biochemistry or psychoanalysis, evolutionary

01:34:58.766 --> 01:35:05.866
psychology. The difference of this class
as opposed to other classes in the

01:35:05.933 --> 01:35:09.166
same school, which was part of the
Saint Petersburg State University

01:35:10.233 --> 01:35:12.333
and called Academic Gymnasium, was that

01:35:13.166 --> 01:35:17.166
unlike other classes, which
were specialized in some

01:35:18.233 --> 01:35:24.733
single subject like physics or math
or history, this one tried to get

01:35:26.666 --> 01:35:30.633
the best from all of these
specialized classes and bring it into

01:35:30.733 --> 01:35:38.366
one curriculum. Since it was
an experimental class, it

01:35:38.466 --> 01:35:43.633
wasn't possible to become a straight-A
student, to be excellent in all the

01:35:43.666 --> 01:35:48.666
subjects. It was considered
crazy to even try.

01:35:48.733 --> 01:35:52.066
- So just assume nobody's able to handle it,
you're just pushing the limits of the human

01:35:52.133 --> 01:35:55.733
mind. Four languages in parallel, math,

01:35:55.766 --> 01:35:59.666
evolutionary psychology, just overwhelming
the mind and see what happens.

01:35:59.766 --> 01:36:02.333
- Yes, see what happens.
This was an experiment.

01:36:02.666 --> 01:36:05.200
And it was in the middle of
the '90s, remember, when

01:36:06.366 --> 01:36:09.766
Russia, particularly its
educational system, wasn't

01:36:09.966 --> 01:36:13.533
regulated as much as it is today. It was

01:36:15.666 --> 01:36:19.033
in the middle between the
two stages of the Russian

01:36:19.100 --> 01:36:23.033
history, the Soviet's history
and the modern Russian history

01:36:23.833 --> 01:36:32.833
of the 21st century. In any case, I learned
a lot from that experience. First of

01:36:32.866 --> 01:36:36.766
all, why I got into this
course is because I kept being

01:36:36.800 --> 01:36:38.533
kicked out from other schools.

01:36:38.566 --> 01:36:39.833
- Challenging authority?

01:36:39.866 --> 01:36:43.466
- I was good at all subjects, but not
behavior, you know. We had this

01:36:45.000 --> 01:36:48.233
behavior grade in the
Soviet Union, in early

01:36:48.233 --> 01:36:51.633
'90s. Perhaps they even
have it today, I'm not

01:36:51.666 --> 01:36:55.600
sure. I was very bad at behavior. Always

01:36:55.666 --> 01:36:59.166
challenging the teachers, always
pointing out their mistakes.

01:36:59.200 --> 01:37:02.000
- By the way, that's not such a bad
thing, right? Like, if you were looking

01:37:02.033 --> 01:37:05.666
back, there's some value to
that, right? For young people

01:37:05.833 --> 01:37:14.166
to, maybe respectfully, but challenge
the authority, the wisdom of old, right?

01:37:14.333 --> 01:37:19.466
- I think I was very lucky
to be able to do that

01:37:19.566 --> 01:37:22.933
and to be able to get
away with it in the end.

01:37:22.933 --> 01:37:26.066
Because normally if you keep
challenging authorities,

01:37:27.533 --> 01:37:31.266
you just get kicked out of all
schools, and then you end up

01:37:31.333 --> 01:37:34.866
nowhere. So I eventually
got into a school where

01:37:35.933 --> 01:37:39.833
challenging teachers
was not fully okay, but

01:37:39.866 --> 01:37:43.666
it was something that you could
do and then you would start a

01:37:43.666 --> 01:37:50.466
debate with the teacher, and normally they
would allow you to express your point of

01:37:50.500 --> 01:37:55.166
view, and then some objective truth
may come out of it as a result.

01:37:58.333 --> 01:38:01.966
But at that point, I was
pretty bored with my life, you

01:38:02.033 --> 01:38:05.633
know? Every teenager gets to a
point when they have this sort of

01:38:05.666 --> 01:38:11.533
existential crisis. What's the point
of life? What am I even doing here?

01:38:12.600 --> 01:38:18.700
At some point, I decided since
I have to go to school anyway,

01:38:20.133 --> 01:38:23.633
I might as well try to do something
impossible and become the best

01:38:23.700 --> 01:38:29.866
student and get an A, or what we
called five in the Russian system,

01:38:31.766 --> 01:38:38.166
on every single subject. And
that kept me busy for a while.

01:38:40.166 --> 01:38:46.933
It was incredibly difficult because
you didn't have enough time.

01:38:48.166 --> 01:38:50.400
Even if you just studied all the time,

01:38:51.566 --> 01:38:57.233
not doing anything else, you didn't have
any time left to prepare all the homework

01:38:58.266 --> 01:39:02.100
tasks and get ready for
all the tests. So I

01:39:02.133 --> 01:39:07.333
ended up using the breaks
between classes, but I got to

01:39:08.233 --> 01:39:12.633
the result I wanted to get
to. I got the excellent

01:39:14.033 --> 01:39:19.633
mark in every subject, and that
kept me happy for a while.

01:39:19.666 --> 01:39:20.666
- What did you understand about

01:39:21.766 --> 01:39:25.066
an effective education system from studying
foreign languages at the same time,

01:39:25.666 --> 01:39:29.766
doing such a diversity? Like, if you were
to design an education system from scratch

01:39:30.633 --> 01:39:34.333
for young people, especially in the
21st century, what would that look

01:39:34.433 --> 01:39:39.433
like? You posted about the value of
mathematics as a foundation for everything.

01:39:39.500 --> 01:39:43.400
- Yeah, I still think math is
essential. It's something that

01:39:43.466 --> 01:39:51.266
shapes your brain. It teaches you to rely
on your logical thinking to split big

01:39:51.333 --> 01:39:58.500
problems into smaller parts, put them in
the right sequence, solve them patiently,

01:39:59.966 --> 01:40:03.733
trying again if it doesn't
work. And this is exactly the

01:40:03.833 --> 01:40:07.166
same skill you need in programming,

01:40:08.000 --> 01:40:12.033
in project management, and start it
when you start your own company.

01:40:13.500 --> 01:40:19.166
And it's one of the few subjects
in school which encourages you to

01:40:21.700 --> 01:40:25.633
develop your own thinking
as opposed to rely on

01:40:25.700 --> 01:40:32.833
what other people have to say and just
repeating their opinions. That is extremely

01:40:32.866 --> 01:40:40.200
valuable. And of course, once you're good
at math, you can apply it in physics, in

01:40:40.266 --> 01:40:46.266
engineering, in coding. And
it's not surprising there that

01:40:47.566 --> 01:40:53.433
most of the most successful tech founders
and CEOs are very good at math and coding

01:40:54.633 --> 01:41:05.733
because ultimately, it's the same
mental skill that you rely on. But

01:41:06.600 --> 01:41:10.866
back then in the school, I realized
something else as well: it's that

01:41:13.600 --> 01:41:20.700
competition is really important. Competition
is key. This is what motivates a lot

01:41:20.733 --> 01:41:22.600
of teenagers

01:41:23.400 --> 01:41:30.000
when they're at school. And if you
remove competition out of the education

01:41:30.066 --> 01:41:36.933
system, you will end up forcing kids
to start competing elsewhere, for

01:41:36.933 --> 01:41:39.700
example, in video games.

01:41:40.833 --> 01:41:43.733
It's a trend you see now in many
countries, including in the West,

01:41:45.133 --> 01:41:49.000
when well-meaning authorities or
parents say, "We don't want our

01:41:49.033 --> 01:41:55.966
kids to be too stressed. We don't want them
to feel anxiety. So let's just get rid

01:41:56.033 --> 01:42:00.300
of all the public grading
system, all these

01:42:00.500 --> 01:42:04.433
rankings of who won, who
lost. We don't want any

01:42:04.500 --> 01:42:08.966
of that." And part of it is
justified, but as a result,

01:42:11.733 --> 01:42:15.633
some kids lose interest. Yes, you

01:42:15.700 --> 01:42:21.666
eliminate the losers, but you end
up eliminating the winners as well.

01:42:22.666 --> 01:42:31.100
And then if you're overprotective of
the kids in that age, they grow up,

01:42:32.233 --> 01:42:36.133
graduate schools or universities, and
they are still not prepared for real

01:42:36.200 --> 01:42:41.466
life because real life is constant
competition for jobs, for promotions,

01:42:43.133 --> 01:42:49.433
for customers, and it's more brutal.
What you have as a result is

01:42:49.533 --> 01:42:53.266
high suicide rates, high
unemployment, all the

01:42:53.400 --> 01:42:57.333
things and negative trends
you see now in many

01:42:57.400 --> 01:43:01.900
countries which thought eliminating
competition from their education systems

01:43:02.833 --> 01:43:03.566
was a good idea,

01:43:05.933 --> 01:43:09.700
they still persist. They still think
competition's a bad thing. They try to

01:43:09.733 --> 01:43:15.800
eliminate competition from their economy
as well to an extent, saying, "We're gonna

01:43:16.900 --> 01:43:26.966
make sure the losers don't lose and
the winners don't get too much."

01:43:27.000 --> 01:43:30.233
But as a result, they make
their entire systems less

01:43:30.266 --> 01:43:34.866
competitive, their entire
economies. Some of them in Europe

01:43:36.466 --> 01:43:41.400
are now struggling to keep
up with China, with South

01:43:41.500 --> 01:43:45.333
Korea, with Singapore, with
Japan, and other places

01:43:45.400 --> 01:43:53.433
where the education system was based on
ruthless competition. So this is a hard

01:43:53.600 --> 01:43:56.166
choice any civilization has to make.

01:43:58.500 --> 01:44:02.133
We support competition, understanding
that eventually it leads to

01:44:02.200 --> 01:44:08.533
progress in science and technology
and abundance for society at large.

01:44:09.600 --> 01:44:15.066
Or we remove competition thinking that
somehow we can shield the future generations

01:44:16.233 --> 01:44:21.833
from the stress that
competition inevitably causes.

01:44:22.266 --> 01:44:26.200
- Yeah, I mean, it's grounded in a good
instinct of compassion. You don't want people

01:44:26.266 --> 01:44:30.100
who are, who suck at a thing to
feel pain, but it seems like

01:44:30.200 --> 01:44:33.200
struggle is a part of life. Either
you do it early or you do it later.

01:44:34.400 --> 01:44:38.300
And it's true, that's such a
good point that competition does

01:44:38.300 --> 01:44:41.533
seem to be a really
powerful driver of skill

01:44:41.600 --> 01:44:45.533
development, like you
mentioned, pursuing mastery.

01:44:46.433 --> 01:44:49.600
There's something in human nature
that, especially for young

01:44:49.700 --> 01:44:53.566
people, if you can compete at a thing,
you're gonna be really driven to

01:44:53.600 --> 01:44:57.533
get good at that thing. If you can direct that
in the education system as China does, as

01:44:57.566 --> 01:45:01.333
many, as many nations like you
mentioned do, then you're going to

01:45:01.366 --> 01:45:05.200
develop a lot of brilliant people,
resilient people, people that

01:45:05.200 --> 01:45:08.300
are ready to create epic
stuff in the world.

01:45:08.300 --> 01:45:11.366
- I think there is a lot of evidence
proving that we are biologically

01:45:11.433 --> 01:45:17.400
wired to compete and establish
our understanding of

01:45:19.133 --> 01:45:25.300
what our qualities are and talents are
in relation to other people around us.

01:45:26.500 --> 01:45:30.300
And this is one of the ways
society self-regulates.

01:45:30.433 --> 01:45:34.166
- Speaking of competition,
your brother, Nikolai,

01:45:34.300 --> 01:45:40.633
he's a mathematician, programmer, expert
in cryptography. He has won the IMO,

01:45:40.800 --> 01:45:44.433
International Mathematics
Olympiad. He got gold medal three

01:45:44.533 --> 01:45:51.566
times, ICPC Programming two times,
has two PhDs in mathematics.

01:45:51.633 --> 01:45:55.233
And you have worked together for
many years, creating incredible

01:45:55.266 --> 01:45:59.433
technologies that we've been talking
about. So what have you learned about

01:46:00.366 --> 01:46:02.300
just life from your brother?

01:46:02.333 --> 01:46:05.800
- Well, first of all, I must
say I learned pretty much

01:46:05.900 --> 01:46:12.433
everything from my brother, everything I
know. Because when we used to be kids,

01:46:13.400 --> 01:46:18.366
we slept in the same bedroom, like
beds a few feet away from each other.

01:46:19.700 --> 01:46:26.800
and I kept bugging him with questions.
I would ask him about dinosaurs and

01:46:27.300 --> 01:46:31.866
galaxies and black holes and Neanderthals,

01:46:33.400 --> 01:46:36.733
everything I could think
of, and he was my Wikipedia

01:46:37.466 --> 01:46:41.400
back in the time when we didn't
have internet access. He's a

01:46:41.500 --> 01:46:45.366
unique prodigy kid,
probably one in a billion.

01:46:45.966 --> 01:46:49.766
He started reading at the
age of three, I think, and

01:46:49.866 --> 01:46:53.866
he pretty fast got so
advanced in math that

01:46:53.900 --> 01:46:57.766
by the age of six, he could
already read really sophisticated

01:46:57.966 --> 01:47:03.366
books on astronomy. Sometimes when
he did it in public places like

01:47:04.433 --> 01:47:12.333
buses or metro, my mom was criticized
by people who were witnessing it.

01:47:13.166 --> 01:47:16.800
They would tell her, "Why are you
mocking your own kid with this

01:47:16.866 --> 01:47:20.700
serious book? It's obvious
the kid can't understand

01:47:20.766 --> 01:47:24.666
everything there. It's too complicated.
Even we don't understand anything there.

01:47:24.700 --> 01:47:28.866
There are some formulas."
And he was already

01:47:31.233 --> 01:47:36.766
sucking in this knowledge.
He just has this thirst for

01:47:36.800 --> 01:47:42.166
information. So, he was
the source of all kinds of

01:47:44.300 --> 01:47:48.900
great facts, useful
things, inspiring things.

01:47:50.133 --> 01:47:53.400
He taught me pretty much
everything I know. At the same

01:47:53.433 --> 01:47:59.933
time, he is incredibly
modest and kind, and this is

01:48:01.066 --> 01:48:04.400
something I think a lot of people

01:48:05.733 --> 01:48:09.733
that think they're smart but not
genuinely intelligent, lack.

01:48:11.500 --> 01:48:16.366
More often than not, people who are
truly intelligent, they're also kind

01:48:17.166 --> 01:48:18.866
and compassionate.

01:48:18.866 --> 01:48:20.466
- And he is that.

01:48:20.500 --> 01:48:21.866
- Definitely.

01:48:22.066 --> 01:48:25.966
- You actually have been staying out of the
public eye for the most part. You've done very

01:48:26.000 --> 01:48:29.733
few interviews. You're pretty
low-key. But your brother is on

01:48:29.800 --> 01:48:34.233
another level. He's been staying out
of the public eye. What's behind that?

01:48:34.266 --> 01:48:40.600
- Part of it is his natural modesty.
He doesn't need to do it.

01:48:42.766 --> 01:48:48.200
He doesn't feel this urge to
show off, brag about stuff.

01:48:49.700 --> 01:48:53.266
I tried to avoid it as well, but at
a certain point, I realized that

01:48:53.800 --> 01:48:57.233
me being too private, too secretive,

01:48:57.933 --> 01:49:02.733
becomes a liability because it creates
this void, this emptiness that

01:49:06.033 --> 01:49:11.033
people and organizations that don't like
Telegram very much are willing to fill

01:49:11.933 --> 01:49:15.666
with inaccurate information, and
they're willing to spread narratives

01:49:15.866 --> 01:49:19.666
about Telegram, which can result in

01:49:22.100 --> 01:49:27.800
strange situations, some of
which we discussed earlier,

01:49:29.900 --> 01:49:32.400
for example, this French investigation.

01:49:32.466 --> 01:49:36.266
- Yeah. I've gotten to know you
more and more, and there's a deep

01:49:36.300 --> 01:49:40.166
integrity to you that I
think is good to show to the

01:49:40.200 --> 01:49:44.133
world. There's a lot of attack
vectors on user privacy, and I

01:49:44.133 --> 01:49:45.766
think the most important, the last

01:49:47.166 --> 01:49:51.133
wall of protection is the actual
people that are running the company.

01:49:51.166 --> 01:49:54.500
So it's important to some degree for you
to be out there, showing your true self.

01:49:55.233 --> 01:49:59.600
So we should say that also you didn't
mention, but you're a programmer

01:50:00.700 --> 01:50:04.566
From an early age, you started
coding at 10. First things you

01:50:04.666 --> 01:50:08.300
built were a video game at 11, and then

01:50:08.366 --> 01:50:12.066
eventually 10 years later, at 21, you
programmed the initial versions of

01:50:12.100 --> 01:50:15.966
VK single-handedly. Can you talk
to me about your programming

01:50:16.033 --> 01:50:19.300
journey that led to the
creation of VK? What was the VK

01:50:19.400 --> 01:50:25.300
stack? Was it PHP mostly? How did you
figure out how to program websites,

01:50:26.133 --> 01:50:26.666
all of that?

01:50:27.466 --> 01:50:31.333
- I wasn't interested in programming websites
at first. I didn't even have access to

01:50:31.966 --> 01:50:35.766
the internet when I was 10
years old. But I liked video

01:50:35.866 --> 01:50:43.500
games. I didn't have enough of them,
and the scarcity forced me to start

01:50:43.533 --> 01:50:47.566
building them, more computer
games just to play myself.

01:50:49.266 --> 01:50:51.333
It's actually an interesting thing that

01:50:52.866 --> 01:50:55.800
we sometimes don't realize
it, but scarcity leads to

01:50:55.866 --> 01:51:01.100
creativity, and one of the reasons
you have so many people who love to

01:51:02.266 --> 01:51:07.566
code coming from the Soviet
Union or other places which

01:51:07.600 --> 01:51:13.200
didn't have much access to modern
technology, and more importantly, modern

01:51:13.366 --> 01:51:22.300
entertainment, is that perhaps we were not
so much distracted by all this abundance

01:51:23.400 --> 01:51:27.166
of different entertainment options,
which is not to say it's bad to

01:51:27.200 --> 01:51:31.200
have those options. It's just a fact

01:51:31.200 --> 01:51:35.966
that we sometimes don't appreciate.
So I started to build computer games.

01:51:37.633 --> 01:51:42.633
My brother would sometimes guide me. For
example, I would create this turn-based

01:51:43.000 --> 01:51:48.766
strategy, of course two-dimensional. Back
then, three-dimensional was too much for

01:51:48.833 --> 01:52:00.133
me. But it wasn't as slick in terms of
the scrolling FPS, frames per second,

01:52:01.133 --> 01:52:06.400
parameter, and asked my
brother how to optimize it.

01:52:06.466 --> 01:52:12.600
He would guide me, and
this kind of learning and

01:52:14.766 --> 01:52:21.766
training really shaped my coding skills
when I was younger. Then I started to

01:52:21.800 --> 01:52:25.633
create video games for
my classmates. When we

01:52:25.700 --> 01:52:29.633
played, for example, tic-tac-toe
on an infinite field in

01:52:29.700 --> 01:52:34.500
my class during the breaks, you know,
and not tic-tac-toe, the three in a row.

01:52:34.600 --> 01:52:38.566
This was a bit five in a
row, and an infinite field.

01:52:38.600 --> 01:52:40.433
This is a much more interesting game,

01:52:41.466 --> 01:52:44.600
and it gets quite complicated
if you keep playing

01:52:44.633 --> 01:52:48.400
it. My classmates used to
love it, and some of my

01:52:48.433 --> 01:52:51.933
classmates were really smart,
you know, champions of math

01:52:52.000 --> 01:52:55.466
Olympians, sons and daughters
of professors at the

01:52:55.500 --> 01:52:59.000
university, and I decided, "No, I
want to win every single time."

01:53:00.166 --> 01:53:03.666
I don't want to lose even a single time,
so how do I win? I need to practice

01:53:03.700 --> 01:53:07.400
more. But how do I practice more?
I need an opponent stronger than

01:53:07.433 --> 01:53:11.300
myself. So, I coded this
game so that I would

01:53:11.300 --> 01:53:15.300
play against the computer,
and the computer would

01:53:15.300 --> 01:53:21.533
calculate, I think, four moves
in advance to choose the optimal

01:53:21.666 --> 01:53:25.533
strategy. That wasn't enough, four moves

01:53:25.666 --> 01:53:29.000
in advance. I would still
win over it. If I tried to

01:53:29.333 --> 01:53:33.266
calculate five or six, it was too
slow. So, I asked my brother, "Help

01:53:33.300 --> 01:53:39.666
me out here." So, he made
this algorithm. Eventually, I

01:53:41.833 --> 01:53:45.766
trained myself to win every single
time, even with the computer back

01:53:45.800 --> 01:53:53.466
then. We didn't have modern CPUs
and I could still retain some

01:53:53.533 --> 01:54:00.300
self-confidence. I would go back to school
during breaks, play with my classmates, and

01:54:00.500 --> 01:54:04.400
soon people started to lose interest.
None of my classmates wanted

01:54:04.433 --> 01:54:13.233
to play this game anymore. I killed the
game. There's no- So after that, when I

01:54:14.400 --> 01:54:16.333
got into St. Petersburg State University,

01:54:18.900 --> 01:54:22.866
it was quite boring just to
study because it was too easy.

01:54:22.933 --> 01:54:26.633
So, I thought, "What can I do
there?" I created a website for the

01:54:26.733 --> 01:54:34.800
students of my faculty first. I
organized the creation of digital

01:54:36.400 --> 01:54:42.533
answers to all exams and a digitized
version of all lectures, which was

01:54:42.633 --> 01:54:48.433
something very unique back then.
Remember, it was 25 years ago.

01:54:49.966 --> 01:54:54.800
I would put together a
website where I would

01:54:55.733 --> 01:54:59.866
publish all these materials, and
pretty soon it became super popular.

01:55:01.066 --> 01:55:05.000
I opened a discussion forum
there. In a few years, I expanded

01:55:06.600 --> 01:55:10.233
to the university with all of its other

01:55:10.333 --> 01:55:14.566
departments, and then to other
universities. We ended up having

01:55:16.433 --> 01:55:22.866
tens of thousands of users just as a
student support tool. We had all kinds of

01:55:23.300 --> 01:55:31.033
social features there: friends lists, photo
albums, profiles, blogs, all of it. It was

01:55:31.100 --> 01:55:35.833
quite successful, and after
I graduated the university,

01:55:36.633 --> 01:55:39.933
one of my ex-classmates from the school

01:55:40.633 --> 01:55:44.433
reached out to me after
reading about my successes in

01:55:44.533 --> 01:55:48.066
a newspaper, the main
business newspaper of St.

01:55:48.100 --> 01:55:50.800
Petersburg, and he asked me,
"Are you trying to build

01:55:51.833 --> 01:55:58.766
a Russian Facebook?" I said, "I'm
not sure. What's Facebook?" So, we

01:55:58.900 --> 01:56:02.666
met. He, since he graduated in American

01:56:02.800 --> 01:56:07.566
university two years before that,
he showed me Facebook. I thought,

01:56:07.566 --> 01:56:13.500
"Well, I kind of already have all of this
technology, but it's valuable to know

01:56:14.633 --> 01:56:19.600
which elements I should get rid
of in order to scale this thing

01:56:21.233 --> 01:56:27.866
and have millions of users." This is also
something people don't appreciate that

01:56:28.833 --> 01:56:34.100
sometimes in order to move forward
and have more success, you have to

01:56:35.033 --> 01:56:39.933
get rid of things, including technology.
Getting rid of features is super important.

01:56:40.000 --> 01:56:43.266
- Simplify, both for
scaling and for making it

01:56:45.500 --> 01:56:50.733
amenable to just growing the user
base where people get it immediately.

01:56:50.833 --> 01:56:54.600
- Yes. Otherwise, it's just too
complicated for the new user. The

01:56:54.600 --> 01:56:57.900
existing users will be happy.
They'll be praising you. They'll

01:56:57.933 --> 01:57:04.533
be asking you to add more stuff to make it
even more complicated. So, it's easy to

01:57:06.733 --> 01:57:15.566
lose track and get disoriented if you are
only relying on the feedback of existing

01:57:15.666 --> 01:57:23.233
users. As a result, I started
the website called Vkontakte, or

01:57:23.300 --> 01:57:27.200
VK. It means "in touch"
in Russian. Initially to

01:57:27.266 --> 01:57:31.033
solve my own personal
problem, I graduated the

01:57:31.066 --> 01:57:34.533
university that same year and I
wanted to be in touch, remain in

01:57:34.633 --> 01:57:38.600
touch with my ex-classmates from the university and
other fellow students. And of course, as a 20-year-old,

01:57:38.633 --> 01:57:42.633
other fellow students. And
of course, as a 20-year-old,

01:57:42.766 --> 01:57:46.733
I wanted to meet other people,
including good-looking girls.

01:57:46.766 --> 01:57:50.500
So, I started to build it
from scratch. For that one

01:57:50.800 --> 01:57:55.733
I thought, "I'm not going to
use any third-party libraries,

01:57:56.633 --> 01:58:02.833
modules because I want to make it as
efficient as possible." I was obsessing

01:58:02.933 --> 01:58:06.800
over every line of code. But then how

01:58:06.866 --> 01:58:10.433
do you start something that
large? Like, I didn't have any

01:58:10.766 --> 01:58:17.133
prior experience of creating a project
of that scale, which would involve

01:58:17.200 --> 01:58:23.600
everything. Before, I
would reuse some existing

01:58:23.700 --> 01:58:28.300
solutions. Here, I wanted to build
from scratch, so I called my brother.

01:58:29.733 --> 01:58:33.533
He was a postdoc student in Germany at the

01:58:33.666 --> 01:58:37.533
time in the Max Planck
University, and I asked him,

01:58:38.866 --> 01:58:45.100
"What should I start from?"
And he told me, "Just build

01:58:47.100 --> 01:58:53.600
a module to authorize users." Just
not a way to log in, you know?

01:58:54.000 --> 01:58:56.766
Not even to sign up, just to log in,

01:58:58.000 --> 01:59:01.933
because you can pre-populate the
database with credentials and emails

01:59:01.933 --> 01:59:06.100
and passwords. It doesn't really
matter, but once you see that you can

01:59:07.000 --> 01:59:12.400
type in your password and email and
you're in, and it tells you "hello"

01:59:13.333 --> 01:59:21.566
using your name. Then you will have a clear
understanding where to go from there.

01:59:21.600 --> 01:59:24.266
- Yeah, I mean, that's true.

01:59:24.800 --> 01:59:28.300
- That's one of the best advice
I've ever got in my life.

01:59:28.400 --> 01:59:33.000
It worked perfectly, by the way. I
started to build it and before I knew it,

01:59:34.100 --> 01:59:37.833
there on that website,
photo albums, private

01:59:37.866 --> 01:59:40.833
messages. This guest book
we used to call "the wall"

01:59:41.633 --> 01:59:46.033
back on VK and, I guess, in
the early days of Facebook,

01:59:47.033 --> 01:59:50.666
we ended up building something
even more sophisticated

01:59:50.700 --> 01:59:53.033
than Facebook at the time, with more

01:59:53.100 --> 01:59:57.100
features. I had a girlfriend at
the time, I asked her, "We need to

01:59:58.200 --> 02:00:04.733
somehow come up with a database
of all Russian schools and

02:00:04.800 --> 02:00:10.600
universities and their departments and
subdivisions." She did a great job

02:00:10.633 --> 02:00:14.333
trying to source all this information
online or sometimes writing

02:00:14.466 --> 02:00:17.800
emails to universities saying,
"Which departments do you have

02:00:17.900 --> 02:00:21.900
exactly at this point?
We need to know," or

02:00:21.933 --> 02:00:24.066
reaching out to the
Department of Education,

02:00:25.366 --> 02:00:27.733
both in Russia and then
in Ukraine, and then

02:00:28.600 --> 02:00:33.500
eventually in Belarus, in Kazakhstan,
and other countries where VK ended up

02:00:34.800 --> 02:00:40.900
to be the largest and most popular social
network. So we did a few things that were

02:00:42.233 --> 02:00:50.066
quite unique at the time, and for the
first almost a year, I was the single

02:00:50.333 --> 02:00:55.633
employee of the company. I was the
backend engineer, the frontend

02:00:55.700 --> 02:01:02.933
engineer, the designer. I was
the customer support officer.

02:01:03.866 --> 02:01:07.800
I was the marketing guy as well, coming

02:01:07.900 --> 02:01:10.600
up with all the wordings
and the announcements,

02:01:12.066 --> 02:01:15.266
coming up with competitions to promote

02:01:15.333 --> 02:01:20.266
VK, which worked quite well. That
was an incredible experience that

02:01:22.633 --> 02:01:30.033
gave me knowledge of every aspect
of a social networking platform.

02:01:30.100 --> 02:01:32.666
- Also, an understanding of how
much a single person can do.

02:01:32.733 --> 02:01:36.700
- Exactly. It's one of the reasons
why I like to think I'm an

02:01:36.733 --> 02:01:44.333
efficient project manager and product
manager inside Telegram, because

02:01:45.633 --> 02:01:55.800
I will not take anything but ambitious
deadlines from my team members. If somebody

02:01:56.900 --> 02:02:00.800
gives me, "Oh, I need three weeks to
do that." I would reply, "Well, I

02:02:00.900 --> 02:02:04.000
built the first version of VK in just
two weeks. Why would you need three

02:02:04.100 --> 02:02:08.000
weeks? It seems like something you could

02:02:09.800 --> 02:02:13.600
make real in just three days.
Three weeks? What are you

02:02:13.633 --> 02:02:17.066
going to do the rest of the three
weeks apart from those three days?"

02:02:18.900 --> 02:02:22.133
And, you know, the team
knows me, and that's why

02:02:22.233 --> 02:02:26.666
we are able, today at Telegram,
to move at a very good

02:02:27.700 --> 02:02:32.100
pace of innovation every
month. We're pushing

02:02:33.200 --> 02:02:38.600
several meaningful features, I think,
out-competing everybody else in this

02:02:39.600 --> 02:02:43.300
industry in terms of what you can do

02:02:44.266 --> 02:02:48.400
within a short timeframe.
So yes, that experience was

02:02:49.366 --> 02:02:54.900
invaluable. As for the
stack, I started from

02:02:55.733 --> 02:03:02.200
PHP and MySQL, Debian
Linux, but very soon I

02:03:02.233 --> 02:03:07.366
realized I needed to optimize
this. I started using

02:03:07.433 --> 02:03:15.033
Memcached. Apache servers were not
enough anymore. We had to set up NGINX,

02:03:15.033 --> 02:03:18.966
and my brother was still living in
Germany, so he couldn't help me much

02:03:19.000 --> 02:03:24.200
for the first year of building VK.
Sometimes I would manage to get through

02:03:25.300 --> 02:03:29.700
to him through a call. I would use an
old-school phone to call him with wires

02:03:30.966 --> 02:03:34.366
and say, "What do I do? How do I install
this thing called NGINX? I'm not a

02:03:34.466 --> 02:03:38.600
Linux guy." If he felt

02:03:39.633 --> 02:03:43.433
particularly kind that day and not too
busy, he would show me the way to do it

02:03:43.466 --> 02:03:51.166
or set it up himself, but for the most
part, I had to rely on just myself.

02:03:53.166 --> 02:03:59.000
Having him there, though, helped when
we started to grow fast and scale

02:03:59.033 --> 02:04:02.200
it, because at first

02:04:03.500 --> 02:04:07.900
you realize, "Right now, one server is
not enough. I need to buy another one,

02:04:09.233 --> 02:04:12.433
then another one and another
one. The database should

02:04:12.500 --> 02:04:16.266
be in a different server. Then you have to

02:04:16.300 --> 02:04:20.233
split the database into tables.
Then you have to come up with a

02:04:20.300 --> 02:04:23.633
way to shard the tables using some

02:04:23.700 --> 02:04:26.733
criteria that would make sense,
that wouldn't break your user

02:04:26.800 --> 02:04:30.266
experience. When we got to over a million

02:04:30.400 --> 02:04:34.200
users and beyond, a
dozen servers surviving

02:04:34.233 --> 02:04:38.133
without the input from my
brother in terms of taking

02:04:38.200 --> 02:04:45.000
care of the scaling aspect of it, became
impossible. I remember asking him to come

02:04:45.100 --> 02:04:49.000
back. I said, "You need to help
me with this thing. It's starting

02:04:49.033 --> 02:04:53.166
to be really big." What was worse is that

02:04:54.033 --> 02:04:57.966
since we became popular,
somebody started to

02:04:58.066 --> 02:05:01.700
do DDoS attacks on us,
as it always happens.

02:05:02.166 --> 02:05:03.166
- Right.

02:05:03.166 --> 02:05:07.500
- And then we had people that
wanted to buy a share of VK.

02:05:08.433 --> 02:05:14.166
And interestingly, every time we had
a negotiation day, the DDoS attacks

02:05:14.200 --> 02:05:21.066
intensified. So we had
to come up with a way to

02:05:22.366 --> 02:05:30.066
fight it. I remember having many
sleepless nights trying to figure it out.

02:05:30.100 --> 02:05:34.400
- So that was your introduction to
all kinds of bad actors, DDoS,

02:05:35.533 --> 02:05:40.566
business. Then later you'd find out
there's such a thing called politics,

02:05:41.466 --> 02:05:49.000
and then later geopolitics. But this is
the initial stages. That it's not just

02:05:49.033 --> 02:05:52.900
about creating cool stuff.
It's having to deal with,

02:05:53.000 --> 02:05:56.900
as you now have to deal with
with Telegram, is seas of bad

02:05:57.000 --> 02:06:02.366
actors trying to test the limits of the
system, trying to break the system.

02:06:02.400 --> 02:06:06.866
- Unfortunately, if we didn't
have bad actors and pressure,

02:06:07.900 --> 02:06:12.066
it would be the best job
ever. You just get to create.

02:06:12.100 --> 02:06:15.966
- Yeah. Yeah. And so the help from

02:06:16.000 --> 02:06:21.466
your brother, like you mentioned, NGINX and
sharding the tables, some of the scaling

02:06:22.800 --> 02:06:25.966
issue is algorithmic in nature. It's
almost like theoretical computer

02:06:26.033 --> 02:06:29.866
science. So it's not just about, like,
buying more computers. It's figuring

02:06:29.966 --> 02:06:39.166
out how to algorithmically make everything
work extremely fast. So some of it is

02:06:39.166 --> 02:06:44.466
mathematics. Some of it is pure
engineering, but some of it is mathematics.

02:06:44.533 --> 02:06:48.266
- Yeah, so at that stage I could
do the basic stuff. I could

02:06:48.900 --> 02:06:56.066
understand how I implement scalability
into the codebase, how I sharded my tables

02:06:57.766 --> 02:07:03.666
in the database, where I include
Memcached instead of direct

02:07:05.800 --> 02:07:09.333
requests to the database.
That was quite easy

02:07:10.266 --> 02:07:15.666
because it was still PHP back in
the day. When my brother got back

02:07:18.000 --> 02:07:21.300
from Germany somewhere around 2008,

02:07:22.633 --> 02:07:25.666
I asked him, "Can we make it even
more efficient? Can we make it

02:07:26.733 --> 02:07:30.033
super fast, and at the same time
so that we would require even

02:07:31.133 --> 02:07:34.933
fewer servers to maintain the load?"
And he said, "Yes, but, you know,

02:07:35.000 --> 02:07:40.633
PHP is not enough. I'll have
to rewrite a big part of your

02:07:42.033 --> 02:07:47.933
data engines in C and C++. I said,
"Okay, let's do that." He invited

02:07:49.566 --> 02:07:56.166
a friend of his to help him, another
absolute champion in world's

02:07:56.200 --> 02:08:02.300
programming contest
twice in a row, and they

02:08:03.766 --> 02:08:07.466
they put together the first
customized data engine, which

02:08:07.500 --> 02:08:14.066
was far more efficient than just relying
on MySQL and Memcached, because it

02:08:15.366 --> 02:08:19.566
was, first of all, more
specialized, more low level.

02:08:19.600 --> 02:08:21.433
- So they rewrote it in C, C++?

02:08:21.800 --> 02:08:25.766
- A large chunk of it, like,
for example, the search, the

02:08:25.800 --> 02:08:29.766
ad engine, because VK had
targeted ads. They built

02:08:29.766 --> 02:08:33.733
that. It was, it was very efficient
what they did. Eventually

02:08:33.766 --> 02:08:37.666
the private messaging part,
the public messages part.

02:08:40.300 --> 02:08:47.566
At some point, we realized there are very
few websites online that load faster than

02:08:47.600 --> 02:08:48.800
VK.

02:08:48.900 --> 02:08:49.600
- Nice.

02:08:49.733 --> 02:08:53.166
- I remember in 2009, I
went to Silicon Valley

02:08:53.966 --> 02:09:01.200
and I met Mark Zuckerberg the first time
and some of the other core team members of,

02:09:01.933 --> 02:09:04.266
of early Facebook.
Remember, Facebook was just

02:09:05.166 --> 02:09:09.000
four or five years old then.
And everybody kept asking

02:09:09.033 --> 02:09:12.700
me, "How come even here in
Silicon Valley, VK loads

02:09:12.766 --> 02:09:14.600
faster than Facebook? Everything seems to

02:09:16.766 --> 02:09:19.900
appear instantly on your
website. What's the secret

02:09:19.933 --> 02:09:24.966
sauce?" It was one of the things
that made them very curious.

02:09:24.966 --> 02:09:28.900
- And that was always important to you to have
very low latency to make sure the thing loads

02:09:28.900 --> 02:09:32.633
and... because that's one of the things
Telegram is really known for. Even on

02:09:32.766 --> 02:09:36.633
crappy connections and all that kind of stuff,
it just works extremely fast. Everything is

02:09:36.666 --> 02:09:37.600
fast.

02:09:37.733 --> 02:09:43.000
- It's one of the core technological
ideas. We prioritize

02:09:43.066 --> 02:09:46.966
speed. We think that people can notice

02:09:47.033 --> 02:09:50.700
the difference even if it's
just, like, 50 millisecond

02:09:50.766 --> 02:09:58.366
difference. The difference is subconscious.
It also allows us not just to be faster

02:09:58.766 --> 02:10:03.400
and more responsive, but also
more efficient when it comes to

02:10:04.500 --> 02:10:06.700
the infrastructure, the expenses,

02:10:07.666 --> 02:10:11.733
because if your code executes
faster, it means you need fewer

02:10:13.066 --> 02:10:16.800
computational resources
to run it. So there is no

02:10:16.900 --> 02:10:20.433
way you can lose in making things
faster, and that's why we have

02:10:20.533 --> 02:10:28.233
always been very careful when hiring
people. I would only hire a person if

02:10:28.333 --> 02:10:34.433
I'm ultimately certain it's the best
option. Because if you hire somebody who is

02:10:35.700 --> 02:10:41.200
maybe a little bit distracted,
inexperienced, you may end up with

02:10:42.100 --> 02:10:45.666
inefficiencies in your codebase
that results in tens of

02:10:45.766 --> 02:10:49.200
millions of dollars of
losses. And think about the

02:10:49.233 --> 02:10:56.933
responsibility. If we jump to today
from the VK days, Telegram is used by

02:10:57.000 --> 02:11:02.333
over a billion people. They open
it dozens of times every day.

02:11:04.000 --> 02:11:07.933
Imagine the app opens with
a slight delay, say half a

02:11:08.000 --> 02:11:14.666
second delay. Multiply it by dozens
of times by a billion. It's...

02:11:15.866 --> 02:11:20.300
centuries, millennia lost for
humanity without any reason

02:11:22.833 --> 02:11:24.633
other than just being sloppy.

02:11:24.700 --> 02:11:27.900
- That is so important to
understand and so wise,

02:11:28.800 --> 02:11:32.733
that if you're just a little
bit careless as a developer,

02:11:32.800 --> 02:11:36.333
you can introduce inefficiencies that
are going to be very difficult to track

02:11:36.366 --> 02:11:40.333
down, because you don't know that
it can be faster. The code doesn't

02:11:40.400 --> 02:11:44.333
scream at you, saying, "This could be
much faster." So you have to actually, as

02:11:44.366 --> 02:11:48.300
a craftsman, be very careful
when you're writing the code

02:11:48.333 --> 02:11:52.200
and always thinking, "Can this be done
much more efficiently?" And it can

02:11:52.233 --> 02:11:55.900
be tiny things, because they
all propagate throughout the

02:11:55.933 --> 02:11:59.766
code. And so there's a
real cost in having a

02:11:59.833 --> 02:12:03.300
careless developer
anywhere in the company.

02:12:03.766 --> 02:12:07.533
Because they can introduce that inefficiency,
and all the other developers won't

02:12:07.533 --> 02:12:11.933
know. They'll just assume it kind
of has to be that way. And so

02:12:13.300 --> 02:12:16.633
there's a real responsibility for every
single individual developer that's

02:12:16.633 --> 02:12:19.833
building any component
of an app like Telegram

02:12:21.000 --> 02:12:24.133
to just always ask, you
know, "Can this be done more

02:12:24.133 --> 02:12:27.966
efficiently? Can this be
done more simply?" And

02:12:28.000 --> 02:12:32.900
that's like one of the most beautiful
aspects, the art forms of programming, right?

02:12:32.966 --> 02:12:36.766
- Oh, yes, because when
you manage to discover

02:12:37.866 --> 02:12:44.733
a way to simplify things, make them more
efficient, you feel incredibly happy.

02:12:45.066 --> 02:12:48.733
...and proud and accomplished.
And to your point, I

02:12:48.833 --> 02:12:51.900
can recall a few instances
in my career when

02:12:53.733 --> 02:12:57.033
firing an engineer actually
resulted in an increase in

02:12:57.066 --> 02:13:03.066
productivity. Let's say you have 200
engineers building the app, and then

02:13:04.833 --> 02:13:08.833
just... they just can't make
it. They're not keeping up with

02:13:08.866 --> 02:13:12.966
the pace of the feature
release schedule, and

02:13:15.033 --> 02:13:18.900
you think, "I probably have to
hire a third one." But then you

02:13:18.966 --> 02:13:25.933
notice that one of them is really weird,
falling behind the schedule, complaining

02:13:26.000 --> 02:13:29.500
some of the time, doesn't
assume responsibility.

02:13:30.233 --> 02:13:32.833
And you ask, "So what if I just fire
this person?" And you fire this

02:13:32.833 --> 02:13:36.766
person. In a few weeks, you realize you

02:13:36.833 --> 02:13:40.633
actually don't need, I mean, you
never needed the third engineer.

02:13:41.600 --> 02:13:49.666
The problem was this guy who created more
issues and more problems than he solved.

02:13:49.833 --> 02:13:55.400
That is so counterintuitive
because, you know, in developing

02:13:57.066 --> 02:14:01.033
tech projects we tend to think that
you just throw more people into

02:14:01.100 --> 02:14:07.466
something and then things get solved
miraculously by themselves just because

02:14:07.500 --> 02:14:12.133
more people means more
attention from them. No.

02:14:12.200 --> 02:14:16.000
- That's, again, extremely powerful. The
you know, Steve Jobs talked about A

02:14:16.100 --> 02:14:19.833
players and B players, and there's
something that happens when you have

02:14:20.000 --> 02:14:23.433
B players, which is kind of like
the folks you're talking about,

02:14:23.800 --> 02:14:27.433
introduced into a team. They can
somehow slow everybody down. They

02:14:27.533 --> 02:14:31.033
demotivate everybody. And
it's very counterintuitive.

02:14:31.500 --> 02:14:35.266
They basically... Part of the
work of creating a great team

02:14:36.233 --> 02:14:40.233
is removing the B players.
It's not just hiring

02:14:40.333 --> 02:14:44.200
more in generally speaking.
It's finding the A players,

02:14:44.266 --> 02:14:47.400
quote unquote, and removing the
people that are slowing things down.

02:14:48.066 --> 02:14:51.866
- Oh, yes, because the other thing
that people don't realize is how

02:14:51.900 --> 02:14:55.366
demotivating working with a B player is.

02:14:55.433 --> 02:14:59.966
Everybody can tell if the other person,
the other engineer they're working with

02:15:01.166 --> 02:15:05.100
is really competent. And
it's very visible if

02:15:05.133 --> 02:15:07.966
the person is not comfortable,
they're asking the wrong questions,

02:15:09.766 --> 02:15:14.100
they keep lagging behind.
And at a certain point

02:15:15.700 --> 02:15:23.400
if you're an A player, you get this
dissatisfaction, this feeling that you are not

02:15:23.533 --> 02:15:27.300
able to realize your full
potential, accomplish what

02:15:27.333 --> 02:15:31.966
you're really meant to accomplish
because of this person

02:15:32.900 --> 02:15:37.033
working next to you or
pretending to work next to you.

02:15:37.033 --> 02:15:39.600
And, by the way, in some cases
it's not because the person's

02:15:39.633 --> 02:15:45.466
lazy. In some cases it's just, you
know, their mental, their intellectual

02:15:45.933 --> 02:15:51.733
ability is not there. It's not about
experience. Most often it's about

02:15:53.533 --> 02:16:01.000
natural ability and persistence. In 90%
of cases, it's just the inability to

02:16:01.133 --> 02:16:08.000
focus on one task for an extended period
of time. Not everybody has this ability.

02:16:09.000 --> 02:16:15.233
So for people who do have this ability,
it's an insult to work alongside someone

02:16:16.666 --> 02:16:25.933
who is distracted and cannot go deep in
the projects that they're responsible for.

02:16:26.066 --> 02:16:29.500
- What's on this small
tangent, what's your hiring

02:16:29.600 --> 02:16:33.500
process? You've shown, you've talked about

02:16:33.533 --> 02:16:36.933
how you use competitions often, coding
competitions, to hire, to find great

02:16:37.000 --> 02:16:40.500
engineers. What- what's
your thinking behind that?

02:16:40.533 --> 02:16:44.266
- Well, it's in line with
my overall philosophy. I

02:16:44.300 --> 02:16:47.800
think competition leads to
progress. If you want to create

02:16:47.833 --> 02:16:55.366
an ideal process to- for selecting the
most qualified people for certain specific

02:16:55.366 --> 02:16:58.100
tasks you have in mind.
What can be better than a

02:16:58.133 --> 02:17:01.866
competition? A coding contest where

02:17:01.933 --> 02:17:05.866
everybody who wants to join your company
as an engineer, or just wants to get

02:17:05.900 --> 02:17:11.166
some prize money or validation,
can demonstrate their skills,

02:17:12.600 --> 02:17:15.500
and then we just select
the best. Or if we are not

02:17:15.566 --> 02:17:18.900
certain because there's not enough data

02:17:20.333 --> 02:17:25.200
to hire somebody, we just repeat
the contest with another task,

02:17:26.433 --> 02:17:29.600
get more data, get more winners. Then

02:17:30.133 --> 02:17:34.600
repeat again. At some point, you
realize, "Oh, actually, this guy

02:17:35.800 --> 02:17:42.000
has competed in 10 of our contests since
he was 16 years old, or 14 year old.

02:17:43.766 --> 02:17:47.300
Now he's 20 or 21. He
won in eight of these

02:17:47.333 --> 02:17:54.400
competitions. He seems to be really good
in JavaScript, in Android Java, and also

02:17:54.400 --> 02:17:58.933
C++. Why not hire this person?

02:17:59.966 --> 02:18:07.800
There's some consistency there. And a lot
of these people, they have never worked in

02:18:07.866 --> 02:18:12.900
a big company before, which is
priceless. Because in a big company,

02:18:14.733 --> 02:18:18.200
people tend to shift responsibility.
They have this shared

02:18:18.233 --> 02:18:23.466
responsibility wherein
nobody fully understands

02:18:25.266 --> 02:18:28.633
who can take credit for a project,
who can take blame for a project.

02:18:30.866 --> 02:18:36.766
Inside Telegram, it's pretty
clear, and these competitions are

02:18:38.200 --> 02:18:45.633
the closest experience to what people
will have when working at Telegram.

02:18:46.300 --> 02:18:50.066
So for example, we want to
implement some very tricky

02:18:50.100 --> 02:18:54.300
animation and redesign to our
profile page of the Telegram

02:18:55.600 --> 02:19:02.900
Android version. And the Android app, it's
an open-source app. Anybody can take its

02:19:02.966 --> 02:19:06.466
code and play with it.
So as a result, we would

02:19:06.566 --> 02:19:10.266
not just select the best
person and hire this

02:19:10.300 --> 02:19:14.133
person, we would also select the best
solution to the problem, because we would

02:19:14.266 --> 02:19:17.833
not suggest the contestants
to solve trivial

02:19:17.966 --> 02:19:21.800
problems. It's something that's
valuable, it saves a lot of time

02:19:21.866 --> 02:19:25.633
for us in terms of development.
And because I always

02:19:25.866 --> 02:19:29.700
had these large social
media platforms which

02:19:29.866 --> 02:19:33.266
I could use to promote
these competitions...

02:19:33.966 --> 02:19:40.266
...somehow both VK and Telegram
were very popular among engineers

02:19:41.100 --> 02:19:43.900
and designers, other tech people.

02:19:45.100 --> 02:19:48.900
I had no issue to promote
this contest and find the

02:19:48.933 --> 02:19:54.000
right people, ever. And
what can be better than

02:19:55.266 --> 02:19:58.866
for an employee of your company,
somebody who has been a user of

02:19:58.933 --> 02:20:02.266
it? If this person has no prior experience

02:20:03.166 --> 02:20:06.066
of using Telegram, their
understanding would be very

02:20:06.166 --> 02:20:10.900
limited. Why would I even try to
hire somebody from LinkedIn who

02:20:12.466 --> 02:20:20.233
worked at Google and other companies, is
used to receiving a salary for nothing,

02:20:22.600 --> 02:20:26.600
is used to shifting responsibility
and being stuck in endless meetings,

02:20:28.800 --> 02:20:35.200
and has very limited understanding
of what Telegram stands for?

02:20:36.733 --> 02:20:39.066
It's just crazy if you think about it.

02:20:39.366 --> 02:20:43.333
- Yeah, and then because of that,
you're extremely selective and slow

02:20:43.366 --> 02:20:47.266
in hiring. So the people really have to

02:20:47.300 --> 02:20:51.233
earn their spot. And as a
result, I got a chance to sit in

02:20:51.466 --> 02:20:55.100
one of the team meetings where
people discuss the different

02:20:55.166 --> 02:20:58.866
features that are being developed, the
different ideas, some of which are at the very

02:20:58.966 --> 02:21:02.800
cutting edge. So you get to see behind
the scenes how it's possible to

02:21:02.833 --> 02:21:04.200
have such a fast rate of

02:21:05.100 --> 02:21:08.966
idea generation. So you generate the
idea, you implement the prototype and

02:21:09.000 --> 02:21:12.900
then eventually it becomes an actual

02:21:12.966 --> 02:21:17.700
feature in the product. And so that's why
you have this kind of half-hilarious,

02:21:18.900 --> 02:21:24.600
half-incredible fact that for
many as compared to WhatsApp and

02:21:24.700 --> 02:21:28.600
Signal, you've led the way in many other
features. Many of the features we take

02:21:28.633 --> 02:21:35.866
for granted now, many of which we know
and love, like the auto-delete timer.

02:21:35.933 --> 02:21:41.566
That was seven years ahead of any
other messenger. Message editing,

02:21:43.000 --> 02:21:46.866
replies, these are all
obvious things you...

02:21:47.166 --> 02:21:50.766
I've even forgotten for some of
them that they were never part.

02:21:51.100 --> 02:21:54.433
I mean, I think the auto-delete
timer is a really brilliant idea.

02:21:54.466 --> 02:21:57.666
- We implemented it in 2013 in the secret

02:21:57.700 --> 02:22:01.666
chats. The funny thing about it is
then when other apps started to copy

02:22:01.766 --> 02:22:09.266
it, like WhatsApp seven years after,
and then Signal and some other apps,

02:22:09.300 --> 02:22:12.266
they initially even copied the exact

02:22:13.100 --> 02:22:17.066
timestamps. So for example, if we had like
one, three, and five seconds, they would

02:22:17.100 --> 02:22:18.800
also have one, three, and five seconds.

02:22:19.433 --> 02:22:21.833
They tried not to change it
because they were not sure

02:22:22.833 --> 02:22:24.833
what was the magic sauce
behind the feature.

02:22:25.466 --> 02:22:29.466
And ironically, it happens
with many of these things.

02:22:29.500 --> 02:22:36.166
For example, when we design how you reply
to a message, and you have a small snippet

02:22:36.633 --> 02:22:40.200
showing that you're replying to this
message and now you're typing your

02:22:40.266 --> 02:22:43.966
response, then there is a small
snippet in the message itself

02:22:44.000 --> 02:22:47.900
that, well, if you tap on it,
highlights the original message

02:22:48.100 --> 02:22:51.966
you're replying to. Seems
pretty obvious. But there are

02:22:52.033 --> 02:22:55.866
certain design decisions that we
were implementing at the time, and

02:22:55.933 --> 02:22:59.533
we got this vertical line on
the left, and all these other

02:23:00.033 --> 02:23:03.366
small things that are completely arbitrary,
right? You can do it in a different

02:23:03.400 --> 02:23:07.033
way. But somehow, the
entire industry ended up

02:23:07.100 --> 02:23:10.600
copying exactly that solution,
so now wherever you go,

02:23:11.166 --> 02:23:15.033
WhatsApp, Instagram Direct,
Facebook Messenger, Signal, it

02:23:15.100 --> 02:23:18.900
doesn't matter, you would see
exactly the same or pretty much

02:23:19.633 --> 02:23:24.133
similar experience because nobody
really wants to take the risk

02:23:25.266 --> 02:23:31.700
and innovate. If something
works, why not just copy it?

02:23:31.766 --> 02:23:35.133
- Yeah, but we should say that it's done
extremely well. The vertical line and the

02:23:35.233 --> 02:23:39.133
highlighting, I mean, all of these
are tiny little strokes of genius

02:23:40.133 --> 02:23:43.300
by highlighting the text in a
certain way that, from a design

02:23:43.333 --> 02:23:46.766
perspective, makes it very clear that

02:23:47.100 --> 02:23:50.233
this part was written before
and the thing under it is your

02:23:50.266 --> 02:23:54.233
reply. The distinction between the different
formatting of the text. I mean, there's

02:23:54.300 --> 02:24:01.733
a... listen, I know how much typography is
an art form. There's a lot of interacting

02:24:02.433 --> 02:24:08.333
graphic artistic elements inside Telegram
that all have to play together extremely

02:24:08.400 --> 02:24:12.266
well. Like you pointed out to me,
there's this thing that just blew my

02:24:12.300 --> 02:24:18.100
mind, which is the background gradient
of Telegram shifts. It changes

02:24:19.266 --> 02:24:24.233
and it adjusts really nicely to the bubbles,
the chat bubbles, and then there's like

02:24:25.100 --> 02:24:28.766
graphic elements on top of the gradient
that all interplay together. So

02:24:28.966 --> 02:24:32.233
all of that has to work really
nicely without sacrificing

02:24:32.333 --> 02:24:36.133
clarity. Everything is just
intuitive. That's very

02:24:36.233 --> 02:24:40.633
difficult to create. That is art.
And on top of that, it's super fast.

02:24:40.700 --> 02:24:44.566
- That's the hardest part. To make
it look so that designers love

02:24:44.600 --> 02:24:48.400
it is one thing. The
real challenge is make

02:24:48.466 --> 02:24:52.300
it look the way the designers
love it and make it

02:24:52.366 --> 02:24:56.066
work on the weakest device
as possible, the oldest,

02:24:56.133 --> 02:24:59.633
cheapest smartphones
you can imagine. So if

02:25:00.466 --> 02:25:04.300
you take the moving gradient
on the background of

02:25:04.400 --> 02:25:08.200
every Telegram chat, this
is something most people

02:25:08.333 --> 02:25:12.033
don't notice, but they can feel it.

02:25:12.100 --> 02:25:16.033
- Yeah, yeah. They notice it subconsciously
or something like that. There is a pleasant

02:25:16.100 --> 02:25:19.533
feeling. There's a feeling,
there's a pleasant

02:25:19.566 --> 02:25:22.733
feeling when you're
reading a chat and that's

02:25:22.800 --> 02:25:26.500
where the design contributes
to that. I think a

02:25:26.533 --> 02:25:30.233
gradient really does. I really
love that about Telegram, the

02:25:30.300 --> 02:25:34.200
gradient. Not the technical thing you
described, but the feeling of it.

02:25:34.400 --> 02:25:37.000
And then the technical aspect
of creating that feeling is

02:25:37.000 --> 02:25:40.566
incredible. I could probably come
up with all kinds of algorithms of

02:25:40.600 --> 02:25:44.500
rendering that gradient that's
going to be super inefficient.

02:25:44.600 --> 02:25:46.800
And so doing that efficiently is like...

02:25:46.833 --> 02:25:50.100
- Or efficient, but not
too beautiful, because-

02:25:50.133 --> 02:25:50.600
- Right.

02:25:50.600 --> 02:25:55.800
- ...even doing something so
trivial as a gradient can result

02:25:56.033 --> 02:25:59.900
in noticeable lines in the gradient. The

02:25:59.933 --> 02:26:03.566
person can instantly say, "Oh, no, it's
not the right thing." So you have to

02:26:03.633 --> 02:26:05.133
introduce certain randomness there

02:26:06.100 --> 02:26:09.666
and then you have the gradient, but it's
not enough. It's too plain. You want

02:26:09.733 --> 02:26:13.633
to have certain pattern
as an overlay, but it

02:26:13.666 --> 02:26:17.600
should be simple enough not to
distract you from the content,

02:26:17.666 --> 02:26:21.633
but it has to be entertaining enough
to create a good feeling about

02:26:21.700 --> 02:26:26.200
the whole app. And another
question, what kind of objects

02:26:27.033 --> 02:26:31.500
you want to include in this pattern? And
how would this pattern work? Will it be

02:26:32.500 --> 02:26:38.500
based on pixels or would it be
vector-based? And would it be vector-based

02:26:39.300 --> 02:26:41.800
so they will be infinitely
scalable and high quality?

02:26:43.033 --> 02:26:46.633
And then, I think for the
default pattern and the default

02:26:46.700 --> 02:26:50.400
background, which is based on four
colors. It's not a gradient based on two

02:26:50.433 --> 02:26:56.633
colors; it's four colors, and they're
constantly shifting. I probably looked through

02:26:57.500 --> 02:27:00.133
several thousand variations of them

02:27:01.133 --> 02:27:04.866
because this is such an important decision to make.
It's the default back. Of course, you can change

02:27:04.933 --> 02:27:09.400
it. Actually, you can set up your own
four colors for that. You can change it.

02:27:09.466 --> 02:27:10.433
- No way. Really?

02:27:10.466 --> 02:27:14.300
- Yes. You can do it. And you
want to rely on certain

02:27:14.300 --> 02:27:18.133
deeply hard-coded biological
properties of the

02:27:18.133 --> 02:27:21.000
human mind, right? So
which color do you want to

02:27:21.066 --> 02:27:25.033
use? Is it gonna be blue?
Is it gonna be yellow? Is

02:27:25.033 --> 02:27:28.833
it going to be green?
Because each color has a

02:27:28.866 --> 02:27:32.833
different meaning in our brain.
And what kind of objects

02:27:32.900 --> 02:27:35.533
you want to put there. Something from

02:27:36.666 --> 02:27:40.500
our childhood, something from
nature, or something that

02:27:40.533 --> 02:27:43.900
can create a different kind of mood.
And this is just one detail of the

02:27:43.966 --> 02:27:47.700
app. So there are many
details. When you send a

02:27:47.766 --> 02:27:51.600
message, you are done typing a
message, and you then tap send

02:27:51.933 --> 02:27:56.866
and then the message gradually appears in
the chat. How does it happen? So you want

02:27:57.733 --> 02:28:03.133
the input field to slowly
morph into the actual message.

02:28:03.133 --> 02:28:04.500
- To the message, yeah.

02:28:04.533 --> 02:28:08.266
- And, and you want this to
be done regardless of the

02:28:08.333 --> 02:28:12.000
contents of the message, because sometimes
the width would be different, sometimes it

02:28:12.033 --> 02:28:17.366
would be containing media or a link
preview or other stuff that will change

02:28:18.200 --> 02:28:25.433
the message bubble. So you go
through countless different

02:28:25.500 --> 02:28:29.400
scenarios and make sure
every one of them works

02:28:29.433 --> 02:28:36.900
great, even if this message contains
4,000 characters. And then you

02:28:36.933 --> 02:28:40.933
look at all the platforms: iOS,
Android, and all the old devices

02:28:41.200 --> 02:28:45.800
of all kinds of outdated operating systems

02:28:47.233 --> 02:28:52.333
and the hardware and you cross the
two because you can have this really

02:28:53.233 --> 02:28:57.233
bad old phone but using
the newest operating

02:28:57.333 --> 02:29:03.200
system version, so what do you do?
What kind of bugs do you get there?

02:29:04.133 --> 02:29:08.000
And then, of course, since
Telegram works on tablets as

02:29:08.066 --> 02:29:11.900
well, and our iOS version
works on an iPad,

02:29:11.966 --> 02:29:15.766
which I love a lot. You
have to understand that

02:29:15.866 --> 02:29:21.200
everything can be really big, so it can
consume a lot of space on your screen.

02:29:22.466 --> 02:29:29.066
and then it will trigger using more
computational resources to render it. So,

02:29:29.133 --> 02:29:34.066
there are a lot of nuances to it, but as
long as you obsess over every small detail,

02:29:34.966 --> 02:29:38.700
at least every detail that
really counts, you can get to a

02:29:38.766 --> 02:29:42.500
user experience. If you're really
used to Telegram, if you've been a

02:29:42.533 --> 02:29:48.333
regular user for at least a few weeks,
going back to any other messaging app feels

02:29:48.366 --> 02:29:53.500
like a serious downgrade.

02:29:53.566 --> 02:29:57.633
- Yeah, I mean, there are so many really
magical moments. Like, for example,

02:29:58.566 --> 02:30:04.466
the way a message evaporates when you
delete it. That is a really pleasant

02:30:04.566 --> 02:30:05.466
experience.

02:30:05.633 --> 02:30:07.666
- Oh, yeah. And boy,

02:30:08.733 --> 02:30:14.166
was it hard to make, particularly
on Android? This is this Thanos

02:30:14.566 --> 02:30:18.100
snap effect, right? So,
the message is broken into

02:30:18.533 --> 02:30:22.266
tens of thousands of particles
which go away like dust

02:30:22.433 --> 02:30:28.066
in the wind. It looks great,
but it was so hard to make.

02:30:28.066 --> 02:30:37.666
- probably one of my favorite GUI
graphical things. It's just art. It's

02:30:37.733 --> 02:30:41.533
it's pure art. It's incredible. So,
it's good to hear that it's been

02:30:41.566 --> 02:30:45.866
really thought over and thought through,
because it's extremely well done.

02:30:45.900 --> 02:30:49.166
- No, you can't pull it off if you're not

02:30:50.500 --> 02:30:55.000
going deep into this. And then you
don't want to distract people from

02:30:56.433 --> 02:30:59.666
their communication with all this

02:31:00.700 --> 02:31:06.100
additional information, so you want
them to be invisible in a way.

02:31:06.566 --> 02:31:08.966
- They create the feeling, but
they don't create distraction.

02:31:09.200 --> 02:31:13.833
- Yes, and in order to do that,
you have to overcome even more

02:31:14.833 --> 02:31:18.566
challenges. For example,
you mentioned this deletion

02:31:18.633 --> 02:31:22.600
effect: message evaporates.
If you do the animation,

02:31:22.666 --> 02:31:26.266
if you show the animation first,
and then the message that

02:31:26.333 --> 02:31:30.233
is preceding the deleted message
that is going after the just deleted

02:31:30.266 --> 02:31:36.066
message moves closer to each other,
then it doesn't feel right. It feels

02:31:36.866 --> 02:31:44.566
too long, too imposing. So, what you want
to do is you want the message to disappear

02:31:45.266 --> 02:31:52.633
while the messages around it go closer
to each other to fill the resulting gap.

02:31:53.333 --> 02:31:55.133
And then you imagine what that involves,

02:31:56.233 --> 02:32:04.066
redrawing the entire screen. So, on top
of this very complicated animation,

02:32:04.133 --> 02:32:07.666
you have to think about things
like which kind of messages were

02:32:09.033 --> 02:32:13.833
there before it, after. That
just adds to complexity.

02:32:13.866 --> 02:32:17.700
- And once again, on all kinds of devices,
all kinds of operating systems, all kinds

02:32:17.766 --> 02:32:21.133
of tablets, phones, desktop, all of that.

02:32:21.166 --> 02:32:25.066
- But, you know, once you accomplish
it, it gives you this immense

02:32:26.300 --> 02:32:29.933
sense of pride because
nobody's doing this.

02:32:30.033 --> 02:32:36.600
Nobody really cares. In a way, maybe they're
right not to care. Maybe nobody notices

02:32:36.666 --> 02:32:40.533
this. But there is something
about it that feels

02:32:40.533 --> 02:32:43.300
wrong when such things are neglected
because I understand that every

02:32:43.466 --> 02:32:47.900
day, tens of millions of people around
the world are deleting messages.

02:32:50.900 --> 02:32:52.333
What kind of experience do they get?

02:32:53.566 --> 02:32:58.066
Is this an experience that maybe
even subconsciously inspires them

02:32:59.833 --> 02:33:04.900
and makes their heart
sing even a little bit,

02:33:06.300 --> 02:33:15.533
fills them with joy, lightens up their
mood even a little bit by 0.001%?

02:33:15.533 --> 02:33:20.300
Or is it something that is just
basic? And I think if we can

02:33:22.666 --> 02:33:26.433
bring some value into
people's lives, even through

02:33:26.466 --> 02:33:32.233
these subtle details, we have to
definitely invest our time in it.

02:33:32.266 --> 02:33:36.200
- And some joy, not just sort of
value, value like productivity, but

02:33:36.333 --> 02:33:40.300
joy. I think Steve Jobs, Jony Ive
talked about this. They would

02:33:40.333 --> 02:33:44.066
put so much love and effort
into the design of everything,

02:33:44.133 --> 02:33:48.066
including things that weren't visible
in the initial PCs, personal computers,

02:33:48.133 --> 02:33:51.100
because they believe that somehow through

02:33:51.200 --> 02:33:55.000
osmosis, the users will be
able to feel the love that the

02:33:55.033 --> 02:33:58.500
designers put into the thing, and
you're absolutely right. I mean,

02:33:58.566 --> 02:34:02.966
it's not about deleting
messages. I feel a little

02:34:03.833 --> 02:34:10.733
inkling of joy when I see that
evaporation animation. It's just nice.

02:34:12.166 --> 02:34:16.033
I'm happier because of it, and
so I feel that effort, and I

02:34:16.066 --> 02:34:20.166
think, you know, a
billion users feel that.

02:34:21.000 --> 02:34:23.233
- People like when other people care.

02:34:23.300 --> 02:34:26.566
- Yeah. Yeah. That's exactly what it

02:34:26.666 --> 02:34:30.533
is. And of course, there are the
more sexy things like all the

02:34:30.566 --> 02:34:38.933
emojis and the stickers, the GIFs. Many
of those are just little art pieces.

02:34:39.966 --> 02:34:43.566
- That's, again, an intersection of art
and technology because you look at the

02:34:43.633 --> 02:34:48.400
stickers, which Telegram launched
way before most of these other apps.

02:34:48.433 --> 02:34:50.266
- Three years and eight months ahead.

02:34:50.433 --> 02:34:52.333
- Ahead of WhatsApp, yes.

02:34:52.366 --> 02:34:55.666
But the stickers that WhatsApp
ended up launching three years and

02:34:56.333 --> 02:35:00.166
eight months after, the
first version was not really

02:35:00.200 --> 02:35:08.466
good because they just did regular
GIFs or WebM videos, which were

02:35:08.466 --> 02:35:11.533
not based on vector graphics.

02:35:12.533 --> 02:35:17.533
What we did is vector animations.
Each of these stickers is only

02:35:18.433 --> 02:35:22.266
several kilobytes, sometimes maybe
a maximum of 20, 30 kilobytes

02:35:22.433 --> 02:35:29.400
in size. But it's 180 frames. We were
able to run them at 60 frames per

02:35:29.500 --> 02:35:33.466
second on all devices. And it's also very

02:35:33.566 --> 02:35:37.433
challenging. It was a challenging
thing to do. We had so much headache

02:35:37.733 --> 02:35:41.400
trying to make it work. Nobody even
tried to do anything like this

02:35:41.433 --> 02:35:47.466
before us because it's crazily difficult.
But as a result, you have these fluid

02:35:47.533 --> 02:35:51.466
animations, you have this really
nice user experience. Somebody sends

02:35:51.500 --> 02:35:55.166
you a sticker, you don't have to wait for
it to load because it's so lightweight

02:35:55.933 --> 02:35:59.866
and it starts moving instantly.
And then of course, it's not just

02:35:59.933 --> 02:36:07.233
engineering. You have to find designers
that are able to create the stickers

02:36:07.266 --> 02:36:10.366
using vector graphics, which
means they're based on

02:36:10.500 --> 02:36:14.466
curves described by
formulas, not just created

02:36:14.533 --> 02:36:17.766
as photographs with pixels.
Where do you find these

02:36:17.766 --> 02:36:25.133
people? Again, we did competitions but
it was not easy to assemble a team

02:36:25.200 --> 02:36:28.800
of artists, slash engineers, I would
say, that are able to do something like

02:36:28.866 --> 02:36:32.800
engineers I would say that
are able to do something like

02:36:32.833 --> 02:36:39.366
this. This is a unique form of
art. And this allowed us to

02:36:39.400 --> 02:36:43.033
do a revolution in stickers,
then another revolution

02:36:43.266 --> 02:36:47.133
in animated emoji that you
can add into messages,

02:36:47.166 --> 02:36:51.166
custom animated emoji. I don't think
anybody did that. I think Telegram is

02:36:51.233 --> 02:36:54.533
still the only one allowing
users to do that because you can

02:36:54.566 --> 02:36:57.533
include a hundred of
animated emoji in a message

02:36:58.366 --> 02:37:03.366
and they will be animated and they will
be moving and your device won't crash.

02:37:04.200 --> 02:37:08.000
It's probably unnecessary and crazy
but we think somewhere in this

02:37:08.033 --> 02:37:11.366
intersection of art and engineering true

02:37:11.700 --> 02:37:15.433
quality is created and then
of course more recently

02:37:16.333 --> 02:37:19.666
we expanded into what
we call Telegram GeMs.

02:37:20.266 --> 02:37:24.066
which are essentially blockchain
based collectibles that

02:37:24.133 --> 02:37:28.066
you can demonstrate on your
Telegram profile so that they

02:37:28.100 --> 02:37:32.066
get social relevance but you can
also use them to congratulate your

02:37:32.566 --> 02:37:37.000
friends and close ones with their
birthdays and other holidays

02:37:38.166 --> 02:37:41.566
and that was received extremely well.

02:37:41.633 --> 02:37:45.600
- Yeah, they can hold value, they can increase in value,
you could trade them for that in that aspect, but

02:37:45.733 --> 02:37:53.633
to me, still the... The vector graphics,
and it's not just simple graphics, it's

02:37:53.666 --> 02:37:57.533
incredibly intricate graphics
so the vector makes it very

02:37:57.566 --> 02:38:04.066
efficient but it also allows you to
create, maybe it incentivizes the artist,

02:38:05.600 --> 02:38:10.433
enables them, incentivizes them to
create super detailed intricate

02:38:11.333 --> 02:38:15.266
elements and then the final result like you
would think it wouldn't matter but the final

02:38:15.300 --> 02:38:18.733
result has like a lot of stuff going
on and it's, and it allows you to

02:38:19.100 --> 02:38:21.833
scale on arbitrary devices
and not, now it's like this

02:38:21.866 --> 02:38:28.800
little... you know like usually GIFs
from like back in the day and still in

02:38:28.833 --> 02:38:35.266
meme form are low resolution and so
usually people don't put details and

02:38:35.300 --> 02:38:39.000
intricate art into it but here
with vector graphics it's

02:38:39.066 --> 02:38:42.733
like, like a million things going on and
it allows you to play with different

02:38:42.800 --> 02:38:46.300
animations like you showed me
this thing where you send and you

02:38:46.533 --> 02:38:50.233
hold for a while on the send button and so

02:38:50.366 --> 02:38:54.300
you can share with the person
you sent a message to this

02:38:54.366 --> 02:38:57.766
animation that you've encoded. Like there's
a bunch of stuff going on when they read the

02:38:57.833 --> 02:38:59.033
message.

02:38:59.033 --> 02:39:02.566
- Yes, we have a lot of features
like that when we use

02:39:02.666 --> 02:39:06.200
this art to allow people
to express themselves.

02:39:07.866 --> 02:39:09.800
and most people don't even
know about these features.

02:39:09.866 --> 02:39:12.366
- I didn't know about it. That
was cool. That was cool.

02:39:12.400 --> 02:39:16.333
- The other application of the
same technology is reactions on

02:39:16.366 --> 02:39:25.266
Telegram because we made it
a goal to make sure that

02:39:25.266 --> 02:39:32.633
people feel joy when they just send
you a like. Something so trivial

02:39:32.700 --> 02:39:38.666
as just adding a like to a message
should be an action that you

02:39:39.633 --> 02:39:42.966
want to perform again and again and again.

02:39:43.033 --> 02:39:46.800
- So another feature is on the more serious
side is end-to-end encryption. So

02:39:46.866 --> 02:39:50.566
you led the industry in that. It was

02:39:50.666 --> 02:39:54.466
launched one year and three
months ahead. Can you speak

02:39:54.600 --> 02:39:58.233
to why you decided to add
end-to-end encryption, how you

02:39:58.300 --> 02:40:02.000
developed the current encryption algorithm in
the beginning? What was your thinking behind

02:40:02.066 --> 02:40:03.033
that?

02:40:03.233 --> 02:40:08.133
- So in 2013 when we were
launching Telegram,

02:40:10.133 --> 02:40:15.833
we were aware of the
serious issue with privacy

02:40:17.033 --> 02:40:19.066
that Edward Snowden made very clear.

02:40:21.400 --> 02:40:25.000
And we thought, yes, we are designing
this product in a way that is

02:40:26.166 --> 02:40:29.400
already extremely secure,
but we want to make

02:40:30.000 --> 02:40:33.133
sure that not even we can
access user messages.

02:40:34.066 --> 02:40:37.433
And we understood very clearly
that a bunch of people who

02:40:37.533 --> 02:40:41.466
were born in Russia don't
necessarily inspire trust.

02:40:42.566 --> 02:40:46.666
So that's why we made Telegram open
source. So all our apps have been

02:40:48.533 --> 02:40:51.066
open available on GitHub since 2013.

02:40:52.733 --> 02:40:59.866
And then we added end-to-end encryption in
our secret chats, which WhatsApp copied

02:41:00.266 --> 02:41:04.233
a few years after. One year and three
months ahead, they just started to test

02:41:04.266 --> 02:41:08.633
it. They rolled it out
I think 2016, which is

02:41:10.466 --> 02:41:14.433
three years after us, and the only
reason I think the rest of the industry

02:41:14.466 --> 02:41:23.300
had to do it is because
we set the standard.

02:41:23.366 --> 02:41:26.966
It was incredibly important back in the
day, and at the same time, we realized

02:41:27.200 --> 02:41:31.500
certain limitations of end-to-end
encryption. So within that

02:41:33.233 --> 02:41:37.933
design, that architecture,
you can't support

02:41:40.200 --> 02:41:44.133
very large chat communities
with consistent, persistent

02:41:44.166 --> 02:41:50.700
chat histories. You can't support huge
one-on-many channels. You'd have issues with

02:41:51.866 --> 02:41:58.433
maintaining bots that have
lots of incoming messages.

02:41:59.400 --> 02:42:03.366
Multiple device support
becomes tricky. People

02:42:03.433 --> 02:42:07.300
will end up losing some of the
documents they share, so we

02:42:07.366 --> 02:42:12.433
also saw a lot of issues, and
we ended up having this sort of

02:42:13.966 --> 02:42:20.200
hybrid experience, where
depending on your use case

02:42:20.233 --> 02:42:27.133
and your requirements, you can choose the
level of encryption that we want to have.

02:42:27.166 --> 02:42:31.133
- So that's why you chose to go
opt-in for end-to-end encryption.

02:42:31.166 --> 02:42:34.966
So the trade-off there that
you're describing is between, for

02:42:35.066 --> 02:42:39.000
people who really care about
specific messages extreme privacy on

02:42:39.066 --> 02:42:43.000
those messages, and
usability, like being able to

02:42:43.066 --> 02:42:46.700
sync across multiple devices,
having groups that are 200,000

02:42:46.766 --> 02:42:50.166
people. So all of those features,

02:42:50.200 --> 02:42:54.133
that quality of life features,
there's a trade-off between

02:42:54.200 --> 02:42:58.066
those and end-to-end
encryption. So you lean towards

02:42:58.133 --> 02:43:02.033
letting users sort of enable
end-to-end encryption for cases when

02:43:02.066 --> 02:43:04.600
they want to be super secure.

02:43:04.666 --> 02:43:08.466
- Yes, and Secret Chats are not just end-to-end
encrypted, you know. There are certain

02:43:08.500 --> 02:43:12.400
limitations that are both their
feature and a bug. For example, you

02:43:12.500 --> 02:43:18.366
can't screenshot them. You can't
forward any document, any message from

02:43:18.400 --> 02:43:23.366
them, which is not necessarily
something you need when you're

02:43:24.566 --> 02:43:28.066
trying to get some work
done and you're just

02:43:28.133 --> 02:43:34.866
communicating with your team on a project.
So it became very clear to us that there

02:43:34.966 --> 02:43:42.233
are different needs here, and if you try
to combine both in one type of chat,

02:43:43.200 --> 02:43:49.700
you will end up losing a lot of utility.
You know, we at Telegram, we don't use any

02:43:51.666 --> 02:43:55.300
collaboration tool for
teamwork. We use Telegram to

02:43:55.566 --> 02:43:59.433
build Telegram. So we felt instantly
when we were trying to switch to, say,

02:43:59.433 --> 02:44:09.100
Secret Chats to share large documents and
try to get work done, it was just not

02:44:10.333 --> 02:44:14.066
adapted for it. At the same time, if you

02:44:14.100 --> 02:44:17.400
were really paranoid, you think,
you know, "I don't wanna be

02:44:17.533 --> 02:44:21.866
screenshotted, I don't
wanna have any leaks,

02:44:23.066 --> 02:44:30.866
I don't even trust Telegram; I only trust
code," Secret Chats are the best option. I

02:44:31.066 --> 02:44:35.433
believe it is the most secure
means of communication today.

02:44:36.066 --> 02:44:39.933
- And we should say that there's a lot of
other aspects to this that are important.

02:44:40.166 --> 02:44:44.066
For example, Telegram is the
only app that has open source

02:44:44.366 --> 02:44:49.166
reproducible builds for both Android
and iOS. Why is this important?

02:44:49.200 --> 02:44:55.133
- So, you need reproducible builds
in order to verify that the app

02:44:55.233 --> 02:44:59.166
really does what it claims,
really encrypts data

02:45:00.600 --> 02:45:08.166
in a way that it is described on its website.
For that, you need to make your apps

02:45:09.333 --> 02:45:14.866
open source for any researchers
to have a look at it.

02:45:14.966 --> 02:45:21.566
So, Telegram has been open
source since 2013. Apps like

02:45:21.633 --> 02:45:25.566
WhatsApp have never been open source, so you
don't really know what they're doing and

02:45:25.600 --> 02:45:31.600
how exactly they encrypt your
messages. What's important here,

02:45:31.633 --> 02:45:38.000
though, is to understand whether the version
of the app that you download from the

02:45:38.100 --> 02:45:41.533
app store corresponds exactly

02:45:42.433 --> 02:45:48.200
to the source code that you can view on
GitHub. And for that, you need reproducible

02:45:48.266 --> 02:45:54.966
builds. As you said, Telegram is the only
popular messaging app that does that.

02:45:55.033 --> 02:45:58.033
We allow people to make
sure both on Android and

02:45:58.066 --> 02:46:01.766
iOS that the source code of Telegram on

02:46:01.800 --> 02:46:05.766
GitHub and the app you're
actually using is the same app.

02:46:05.833 --> 02:46:09.766
I think it's incredibly important, not
just to gain people's trust, but just

02:46:09.800 --> 02:46:13.500
to stay transparent and open
about it. When I make this claim

02:46:13.566 --> 02:46:17.400
that Telegram's Secret
Chats are the most secure

02:46:19.300 --> 02:46:22.900
way of communicating, I
really mean it, because I

02:46:22.966 --> 02:46:26.933
haven't seen any fact contradicting this

02:46:26.933 --> 02:46:32.600
claim. At least among the popular
messaging apps, you say WhatsApp,

02:46:33.766 --> 02:46:40.800
Signal, iMessage, none of them have
reproducible builds on both iOS and Android.

02:46:42.033 --> 02:46:47.000
None of them have, at least at the
same level, put so much effort

02:46:48.200 --> 02:46:53.833
into making sure that
the algorithms that you

02:46:53.933 --> 02:47:01.466
use in order to encrypt data are not
algorithms that have been handed to you

02:47:03.400 --> 02:47:10.766
by some agency in order
to create a honeypot.

02:47:12.200 --> 02:47:18.066
At least from what I know
about our competitors,

02:47:18.166 --> 02:47:22.633
I don't think they went
through the same process.

02:47:22.633 --> 02:47:26.600
- So, we should say that the entirety of the
software stack in Telegram is done from

02:47:26.700 --> 02:47:30.066
scratch internally to Telegram, so we're
talking about not just the encryption

02:47:30.200 --> 02:47:32.533
but everything running on the servers.

02:47:33.966 --> 02:47:36.800
So the servers are built out, the
hardware and the software are all done

02:47:36.866 --> 02:47:40.466
internally, which is one of the
ways you reduce the attack surface

02:47:41.200 --> 02:47:45.333
on the entire stack that
handles the messages.

02:47:45.400 --> 02:47:51.966
- It does make it more secure,
because if Snowden's revelations

02:47:52.833 --> 02:47:59.233
taught us anything, is that very
often open source tools, modules,

02:47:59.266 --> 02:48:02.333
libraries that are used by everybody

02:48:04.233 --> 02:48:06.966
ended up having certain
flaws and security issues.

02:48:09.266 --> 02:48:11.933
that make software
vulnerable. It's also a way

02:48:13.466 --> 02:48:18.533
to make sure you're doing things
the most efficient way possible.

02:48:21.200 --> 02:48:25.100
But it's extremely difficult to
do that. You really have to have

02:48:25.166 --> 02:48:29.966
exceptional talent on
your team to achieve this

02:48:30.766 --> 02:48:34.566
level of thoroughness, to go to a low

02:48:34.600 --> 02:48:39.633
level of coding that allows you to
recreate from scratch database engines,

02:48:41.533 --> 02:48:45.433
web servers, entire programming languages.

02:48:47.566 --> 02:48:51.133
Because the programming
language we use on the backend

02:48:53.333 --> 02:49:00.066
to develop the API for the client apps
is also entirely built by our team.

02:49:00.833 --> 02:49:04.433
- Yeah, so minimizing reliance on open
source libraries is extremely difficult,

02:49:04.466 --> 02:49:09.666
as most companies, they rely
on open source libraries.

02:49:09.733 --> 02:49:13.100
- Well, I wouldn't say we're
completely independent from that.

02:49:13.166 --> 02:49:17.133
We use Linux on the backend. There's no
way of avoiding it for us at the moment.

02:49:17.166 --> 02:49:26.733
But for the most part, we are much more
self-reliant than most other apps.

02:49:26.800 --> 02:49:30.666
- You mentioned Edward Snowden. A long time
ago, you wanted to work together with him,

02:49:31.600 --> 02:49:38.033
perhaps to share expertise to
understand the full realm of this...

02:49:38.833 --> 02:49:42.666
of what it takes to achieve cybersecurity.
What do you make of his case? What

02:49:42.733 --> 02:49:46.066
lessons do you learn from
what he has uncovered, and

02:49:47.333 --> 02:49:53.366
maybe even broadly, what impact has his
work had on the world, do you think?

02:49:53.433 --> 02:49:59.000
- Well, the main lesson is not everything
is what it seems. As you would

02:50:00.433 --> 02:50:02.000
discover, and this is

02:50:02.966 --> 02:50:06.833
something that I found quite shocking
at the time, that a lot of people

02:50:08.266 --> 02:50:15.233
who you thought were security
and cryptography experts

02:50:16.200 --> 02:50:20.533
ended up being agents of the
NSA in one way or the other,

02:50:22.033 --> 02:50:28.333
promoting flawed encryption standards.
You would end up discovering that

02:50:31.233 --> 02:50:37.300
your government that was supposed to be
limited in how it can surveil its people

02:50:38.933 --> 02:50:43.833
actually doesn't consider itself
that limited. And that was very

02:50:45.633 --> 02:50:48.266
valuable for the world to understand.

02:50:50.633 --> 02:50:54.333
I guess it also can be a
lesson demonstrating that we

02:50:54.366 --> 02:51:00.800
humans don't get the balance
right. So 9/11 created

02:51:02.500 --> 02:51:07.933
a situation when the government
had to respond, and it

02:51:07.966 --> 02:51:11.866
responded, but it overreacted. It ended

02:51:11.966 --> 02:51:15.766
up eroding certain basic rights and
freedoms, including the right to

02:51:15.833 --> 02:51:18.866
privacy, because the
government always wants to

02:51:18.933 --> 02:51:22.633
increase its powers, and
the government always

02:51:22.666 --> 02:51:26.400
tries to do it at the expense
of citizens. You have

02:51:27.233 --> 02:51:34.433
this situation when the cure is worse
than the disease. And I think it was

02:51:34.466 --> 02:51:40.033
incredibly brave to do what Edward
did. I didn't get to work with him

02:51:41.166 --> 02:51:48.166
or ever see him in person. We keep
in touch, we sometimes communicate,

02:51:50.366 --> 02:51:55.566
but we're not close. I still
think what he did is laudable.

02:51:57.266 --> 02:51:58.700
I hope someday we'll meet.

02:51:58.933 --> 02:52:06.900
- You yourself have faced the full force
of various governments, intelligence

02:52:06.966 --> 02:52:08.233
agencies.

02:52:10.366 --> 02:52:15.633
Is there any intelligence agency you're
afraid of? Any government you're afraid of?

02:52:15.700 --> 02:52:19.466
- I think they should all be equally
afraid of, or equally not afraid

02:52:19.533 --> 02:52:23.200
of in a way. It's not that this

02:52:23.233 --> 02:52:26.533
intelligence service can kill you
and the other can't kill you.

02:52:26.566 --> 02:52:27.566
- They all can kill you?

02:52:27.633 --> 02:52:31.600
- I guess they all can kill me
one way or the other, but

02:52:31.633 --> 02:52:33.933
it's a matter of whether
I'm afraid of death.

02:52:33.966 --> 02:52:37.900
- This goes back to the beginning of our
conversation, I think, multiple times.

02:52:37.900 --> 02:52:42.566
So you're in general fearless
in the face of the pressure.

02:52:42.600 --> 02:52:45.933
- That would be a very bold statement,
but I proved to be quite stress

02:52:45.933 --> 02:52:49.433
resilient. And it's not
that you don't have

02:52:49.466 --> 02:52:53.733
fear. You can have fear,
but you overcome this fear.

02:52:55.566 --> 02:53:03.433
I don't think there is anything

02:53:04.633 --> 02:53:09.766
at this point that can happen
to change the way I am.

02:53:11.133 --> 02:53:14.600
- So you went through a
lot from 2011 to 2014,

02:53:15.066 --> 02:53:19.000
government pressure that you
refused to give in to that led

02:53:19.066 --> 02:53:26.466
you to create Telegram and let
go of VK. And then in 2018,

02:53:28.166 --> 02:53:30.933
Russia and Iran decided to ban

02:53:31.033 --> 02:53:34.966
Telegram. That was another
example of pressure. Can you take

02:53:35.000 --> 02:53:37.000
me through that saga in 2018?

02:53:38.033 --> 02:53:43.166
- So in 2018, Telegram started to become
popular. I think we had something like

02:53:46.033 --> 02:53:54.700
200 million users, and it increasingly became
popular in places like Iran and Russia,

02:53:56.266 --> 02:54:01.400
and other countries where sometimes people

02:54:03.400 --> 02:54:11.833
have something to hide from the government.
In Iran, people used Telegram to protest

02:54:11.900 --> 02:54:15.600
against the government. They
had these huge channels

02:54:16.800 --> 02:54:21.300
that would use to organize the protests,

02:54:23.400 --> 02:54:29.233
and eventually the government couldn't
keep up. They decided to ban Telegram.

02:54:30.866 --> 02:54:33.900
People would still keep
using it, though, using

02:54:33.933 --> 02:54:44.800
VPNs. It didn't help. The government
invested a lot in coming up with their own

02:54:44.833 --> 02:54:47.933
messaging app. They had several teams

02:54:47.966 --> 02:54:51.933
competing for the title
of the national Iranian

02:54:52.033 --> 02:54:55.966
messaging app. All these
apps failed. People

02:54:56.033 --> 02:54:59.866
still preferred Telegram. Interestingly,
Iran banned Telegram, but

02:55:01.200 --> 02:55:06.133
WhatsApp wasn't banned, or at
least they unbanned WhatsApp soon

02:55:06.200 --> 02:55:14.300
after. At the same time, starting
in mid-2017 or late-2017, Russia

02:55:15.100 --> 02:55:18.933
demanded that Telegram hand them the

02:55:18.966 --> 02:55:22.900
encryption keys. They thought
these things exist, something

02:55:23.033 --> 02:55:26.800
that would allow them to
read messages of every

02:55:26.833 --> 02:55:30.300
person on Telegram, or at least
every person on Telegram in Russia.

02:55:30.400 --> 02:55:37.600
And we told them, "That's impossible.
If you have to ban us, ban us."

02:55:37.800 --> 02:55:44.633
And this is what they ended
up doing in spring 2018.

02:55:46.000 --> 02:55:49.666
And that was quite fun because they were

02:55:49.766 --> 02:55:54.300
trying to block our IP addresses,
but we were prepared for that,

02:55:56.300 --> 02:55:59.133
and we came up with this
technology that allowed us to

02:56:00.066 --> 02:56:08.266
rotate IP addresses, replacing them with
new ones every time the censor blocks our

02:56:09.033 --> 02:56:14.400
existing addresses. And then it
was completely automated. We had

02:56:15.633 --> 02:56:22.166
millions of IP addresses. We would be burning
through them. We set up this movement

02:56:22.233 --> 02:56:27.066
called digital resistance when
system administrators and engineers

02:56:28.033 --> 02:56:31.766
all around the world, both inside and
outside Russia, could set up their own

02:56:31.800 --> 02:56:37.500
proxy servers and their own IP
addresses for Telegram to rely

02:56:37.600 --> 02:56:41.500
on in order to bypass censorship. We

02:56:41.600 --> 02:56:45.233
ended up spending, I think,
millions of dollars on that.

02:56:46.600 --> 02:56:54.233
And as a result, the censor got crazy there.
They would ban IP addresses and larger

02:56:54.366 --> 02:57:01.200
subnets of IP addresses then. Huge
subnets which resulted in a weird

02:57:01.233 --> 02:57:05.166
situation where parts of the
country's infrastructure started to

02:57:05.200 --> 02:57:10.533
Like people were trying to pay for
groceries in the supermarkets, and

02:57:12.566 --> 02:57:13.433
nothing would work

02:57:14.433 --> 02:57:18.333
because the Russian censor blocked too
many IP addresses. And some of the

02:57:18.400 --> 02:57:26.333
subnets were used to host other
unrelated services. Even some Russian

02:57:26.333 --> 02:57:30.633
social networks and media
got affected, banks.

02:57:32.000 --> 02:57:36.500
So they had to start being more
selective in how they combat

02:57:38.166 --> 02:57:44.566
our anti-censorship tools. The biggest
resistance we got at the time was from

02:57:44.666 --> 02:57:53.833
Apple. Apple didn't allow us to update
Telegram in their App Store, saying

02:57:54.000 --> 02:57:57.866
for at least four weeks that we

02:57:57.933 --> 02:58:01.800
have to come to an agreement
with Russia first. We said,

02:58:01.800 --> 02:58:08.166
"It's not possible." They said, "We
will allow you to push your update

02:58:09.033 --> 02:58:15.266
for Telegram worldwide, except for
Russia." We didn't want to do that.

02:58:15.433 --> 02:58:19.800
Almost lost hope. You know, at some
point I said, "You know, maybe

02:58:20.966 --> 02:58:25.400
this is the only way. Maybe we should
leave the Russian market. Stop

02:58:26.400 --> 02:58:30.200
allowing users from Russia to download
the app from the App Store, which would

02:58:30.266 --> 02:58:40.200
mean it's over." We helped organize
certain protests in defense of Telegram

02:58:40.233 --> 02:58:42.900
and privacy and freedom of
speech in 2018 in Moscow.

02:58:42.933 --> 02:58:47.633
There were hilarious people
flying paper airplanes.

02:58:47.700 --> 02:58:48.400
- I saw that.

02:58:49.800 --> 02:58:53.533
- And at some point, I decided, "I
have to make a statement. I have to

02:58:53.600 --> 02:58:59.366
say that Apple sided with the censor."
That we are trying to do the right thing

02:58:59.433 --> 02:59:04.200
here, but without Apple we can't do much,

02:59:06.833 --> 02:59:11.366
'cause people can't
download your app anymore.

02:59:13.133 --> 02:59:18.200
I published it in my channel and then
the New York Times picked it up with the

02:59:19.266 --> 02:59:26.000
picture of the protestors flying paper
airplanes. Apple was criticized in that story,

02:59:27.066 --> 02:59:31.466
and I thought, "Well,
Apple should probably

02:59:33.033 --> 02:59:37.000
come back to the right side of
history here." And I waited for

02:59:37.033 --> 02:59:40.400
one day and two days. In the

02:59:40.433 --> 02:59:45.000
meantime, since we've been unable to
update Telegram for more than a month,

02:59:46.733 --> 02:59:56.300
it started to fall apart because
the new version of iOS came out,

02:59:57.833 --> 03:00:04.333
and it made the old versions of Telegram
obsolete. Some features that used to

03:00:04.400 --> 03:00:08.033
work stopped working and users all
over the world started to suffer.

03:00:08.100 --> 03:00:15.666
Like, people that had nothing to do with
Russia from other parts of the world,

03:00:15.700 --> 03:00:18.833
experienced issues with Telegram.

03:00:20.366 --> 03:00:23.633
So it was really serious, and I
said to my team, "You know what? If

03:00:24.800 --> 03:00:26.966
by 6:00 PM today," I
think it was a Friday,

03:00:29.866 --> 03:00:33.800
"nothing changes and Apple
doesn't allow us to push

03:00:33.866 --> 03:00:37.733
the version of Telegram through,
let's just forget about the

03:00:37.766 --> 03:00:41.300
Russian market. Let's keep going
because the rest of the world is more

03:00:41.333 --> 03:00:44.533
important." It's sad, but what can we do?

03:00:44.600 --> 03:00:48.400
- Which, by the way, removes all the people
that want to protest, all the people that

03:00:48.433 --> 03:00:51.400
want to talk in Russia, it removes
their ability to have a voice

03:00:52.233 --> 03:00:55.133
in the most popular messaging
app in that part of the world.

03:00:55.800 --> 03:00:59.633
- Yes. Magically, 15 minutes to the time

03:00:59.733 --> 03:01:03.700
I was planning to remove Telegram from
the Russian App Store in order to

03:01:03.733 --> 03:01:07.333
proceed globally, Apple reached

03:01:08.300 --> 03:01:14.533
out to us and said, "It's okay.
Your update is approved."

03:01:14.566 --> 03:01:20.033
And we managed to keep playing.

03:01:21.300 --> 03:01:28.000
this hide and seek game with the censor,
bypassing censorship through digital

03:01:28.033 --> 03:01:33.233
resistance. In Iran, it was a little
bit different because we realized

03:01:34.333 --> 03:01:37.666
it would've been too expensive to try to

03:01:38.933 --> 03:01:43.700
come up with all those IP
addresses. And in addition, it was

03:01:45.133 --> 03:01:49.000
not clear whether we wouldn't be in
violation of the sanctions regime,

03:01:50.600 --> 03:01:55.300
so we did something else. We
created an economic incentive

03:01:56.200 --> 03:02:01.700
for people who would set up
proxy servers for Telegram.

03:02:01.800 --> 03:02:09.666
Any person, say an Iranian engineer, could
come up with a proxy server, distribute

03:02:10.233 --> 03:02:17.833
its address among users in Iran, and
whoever connected through the proxy of this

03:02:18.733 --> 03:02:23.533
person would be able to
see a pinned chat, an ad

03:02:24.733 --> 03:02:27.933
placed there by the system
administrator, the owner of the

03:02:28.000 --> 03:02:33.933
proxy. And this is how you can monetize
your proxy, so it created this

03:02:36.800 --> 03:02:43.533
market, which resulted in Iranians

03:02:43.600 --> 03:02:49.000
fixing their own problem
and as a result, we kept

03:02:50.033 --> 03:02:52.600
millions, or maybe tens
of millions, of Iranian

03:02:52.700 --> 03:02:57.966
users. Up until this day, I think
Telegram is still banned in Iran

03:02:59.100 --> 03:03:01.766
today, but we probably have

03:03:03.066 --> 03:03:08.433
something like 50 million people
relying on Telegram from that country.

03:03:08.500 --> 03:03:09.933
- So the people find a way around?

03:03:09.966 --> 03:03:11.833
- People find a way around.

03:03:11.833 --> 03:03:15.533
- That's ingenious. That's
really great to hear.

03:03:17.433 --> 03:03:20.500
I have to ask you about this.
After having spent many days with

03:03:20.600 --> 03:03:23.933
you, I learned of something
that you've never talked about

03:03:25.733 --> 03:03:28.433
at the time, have not
talked about to this day,

03:03:29.766 --> 03:03:35.666
that there was an assassination attempt on
you using what appears to be poisoning in

03:03:35.733 --> 03:03:42.733
2018. I think to me, it showed the
seriousness of this fight to uphold the

03:03:42.800 --> 03:03:48.666
freedom of speech for everyone, for
all people of Earth that you're

03:03:48.733 --> 03:03:54.500
doing. I have to say, it would mean a
lot to me if you tell me this story.

03:03:55.366 --> 03:03:59.066
- Well, this is something I never talked
about publicly because I didn't

03:03:59.133 --> 03:04:07.033
want people to freak out, particularly
at the time. It was spring 2018.

03:04:09.133 --> 03:04:11.500
We were trying to raise funds for

03:04:12.466 --> 03:04:20.766
TON, a blockchain project, working with
all kinds of VCs and investors. In the

03:04:20.833 --> 03:04:26.633
meantime, we had a couple of
countries trying to ban Telegram, so

03:04:26.700 --> 03:04:30.666
it wasn't exactly the best
moment for me to start

03:04:30.733 --> 03:04:36.533
sharing anything related
to my personal health. But

03:04:37.833 --> 03:04:41.300
that was something that is hard to forget.

03:04:43.400 --> 03:04:49.933
I never felt ill. I believe I have perfect
health. I very rarely have headaches

03:04:51.666 --> 03:04:56.366
or bad cough. I don't take pills
because I don't have to take pills,

03:04:57.900 --> 03:05:03.866
and that was the only instant
in my life when I think I was

03:05:03.900 --> 03:05:07.333
dying. I came back home, opened

03:05:07.800 --> 03:05:12.100
the door of my townhouse, the place
I rented. I had this weird neighbor

03:05:13.033 --> 03:05:19.000
and he left something for
me there around the door.

03:05:20.466 --> 03:05:27.066
And one hour after, when I was already
in my bed, so I was living alone,

03:05:28.500 --> 03:05:42.133
I felt very bad. I felt pain all
over my body. I tried to get up and

03:05:44.266 --> 03:05:48.600
go to the bathroom. But
while I was going there,

03:05:49.800 --> 03:05:54.866
I felt that functions of my body
started to switch off. First, the

03:05:55.900 --> 03:06:02.700
eyesight and hearing, then I had
difficulty breathing, everything

03:06:02.733 --> 03:06:15.633
accompanied by very acute pain,
heart, stomach, all blood vessels.

03:06:17.300 --> 03:06:21.033
It was... It's a difficult
thing to explain,

03:06:21.100 --> 03:06:24.866
but one thing I was certain
about is, yeah, this is it.

03:06:24.900 --> 03:06:26.533
- You thought you were gonna die?

03:06:26.566 --> 03:06:30.400
- Yeah. This is it, because I couldn't
breathe, I couldn't see anything.

03:06:30.466 --> 03:06:34.300
It was very painful. I think it's over.

03:06:34.933 --> 03:06:38.800
I thought, well, I have had a good
life. I managed to accomplish a

03:06:38.900 --> 03:06:40.600
few... accomplish a few things.

03:06:42.966 --> 03:06:45.200
And then I collapsed on the
floor, but I don't remember it,

03:06:47.700 --> 03:06:53.200
because the pain covered
everything. I found myself

03:06:55.066 --> 03:06:58.566
on the floor next day.
It was already bright.

03:07:00.200 --> 03:07:05.933
And I couldn't stand up. I was
super weak. I looked at my

03:07:07.400 --> 03:07:13.033
arms and my body, blood vessels
were broken all over my

03:07:13.066 --> 03:07:16.866
body. Something like this
never happened to me. I

03:07:16.866 --> 03:07:21.500
couldn't walk for two weeks
after. I stayed at my place,

03:07:23.166 --> 03:07:27.100
and I decided not to tell most
of my team about it, because,

03:07:28.000 --> 03:07:34.000
you know, I didn't want them to worry.
But it was tough. That was tough.

03:07:35.666 --> 03:07:44.033
- Did that make you afraid of
the road you were walking?

03:07:46.566 --> 03:07:50.500
Meaning all the governments, all
the intelligence agencies, all

03:07:50.500 --> 03:07:54.466
the people. Like we mentioned,
it's like you're playing a video

03:07:54.566 --> 03:07:58.366
game. You started with VK
where you're just trying to

03:07:58.400 --> 03:08:01.466
build a thing that scales and all
of a sudden you find out there's

03:08:02.366 --> 03:08:06.200
DDoS attacks attacking the security,

03:08:06.300 --> 03:08:10.100
the integrity of the infrastructure,
and then you realize there's

03:08:10.100 --> 03:08:13.766
politics, and then you
realize there's geopolitics,

03:08:13.900 --> 03:08:20.066
and all of these forces are
interested in controlling channels of

03:08:20.133 --> 03:08:27.100
communication, and you're just a curious
guy who created a platform for everybody

03:08:27.166 --> 03:08:30.533
on Earth to talk, and all
of a sudden you realize

03:08:32.200 --> 03:08:34.833
there's a lot of people attacking you.

03:08:36.800 --> 03:08:42.700
How did that change your view? Did that
make you more scared of the world?

03:08:42.766 --> 03:08:49.266
- Interestingly, not at all. If anything,
I felt even more free after that.

03:08:50.300 --> 03:08:56.700
It wasn't the first time I
thought I was going to die. I had

03:08:59.200 --> 03:09:01.466
an experience when I assumed

03:09:03.766 --> 03:09:06.866
something bad is going to happen
to me a few years before that,

03:09:07.900 --> 03:09:12.233
also in relation to my work. But,

03:09:14.233 --> 03:09:18.133
you know, after you survive something
like this, you feel like you're living on

03:09:18.766 --> 03:09:24.133
bonus time, so in a way,
you died a long time ago,

03:09:25.366 --> 03:09:30.000
and every new day you get is a gift.

03:09:30.000 --> 03:09:31.033
- As a bonus.

03:09:31.100 --> 03:09:32.300
- Yes.

03:09:32.333 --> 03:09:35.833
- And the first time you're referring to,
is that... would that have to do with

03:09:35.866 --> 03:09:38.700
the complexity that was
happening with the pressure

03:09:39.566 --> 03:09:43.000
from the government on VK? And
then you had to figure out

03:09:44.200 --> 03:09:46.833
the increasing pressure and you
had to figure out what to do, and

03:09:48.166 --> 03:09:52.500
you understood that you're losing
control of VK at that moment?

03:09:52.533 --> 03:09:56.200
- The first of these instances
was in December 2011.

03:09:56.966 --> 03:10:03.666
In December 2011, you had this huge
protest on the streets of Moscow.

03:10:04.566 --> 03:10:09.566
They didn't trust in the
integrity of the election results

03:10:10.500 --> 03:10:14.466
to the State Duma in Russia. I
remember in 2011, I still lived

03:10:14.500 --> 03:10:18.400
in Russia, running VK.
There was no Telegram.

03:10:20.400 --> 03:10:24.266
So the government demanded
that we take down

03:10:25.766 --> 03:10:30.400
the opposition groups of
Navalny from VK that had

03:10:32.066 --> 03:10:39.133
hundreds of thousands of members and
that were used to organize this protest,

03:10:41.300 --> 03:10:45.266
and I very publicly refused to do that.

03:10:45.366 --> 03:10:48.633
I just, you know, decided it's
not the right thing to do.

03:10:48.666 --> 03:10:51.466
People have the right to assemble, and

03:10:51.533 --> 03:10:59.666
I mocked the prosecutor who handed me
that demand and put out a scan of it,

03:11:01.366 --> 03:11:07.666
and next to it, a photo of a dog
in a hoodie with its tongue out,

03:11:08.766 --> 03:11:12.766
and I said, "This is my official
response to the prosecutor's request to

03:11:12.900 --> 03:11:20.133
ban opposition groups." That was very
funny at the moment, but then I had armed

03:11:20.266 --> 03:11:24.766
policemen trying to get into my apartment,

03:11:30.100 --> 03:11:34.900
and I thought about many things
at that moment. I asked myself,

03:11:36.133 --> 03:11:42.733
"Did I make the right choice?" And I came
to the conclusion that I made the right

03:11:42.800 --> 03:11:46.066
choice, and I asked myself,
"What would be the next thing

03:11:48.433 --> 03:11:55.300
that would logically follow from this?"
And I realized, "They're probably going to

03:11:56.566 --> 03:12:01.900
put me in prison." So what am I
going to do about it? I asked

03:12:01.966 --> 03:12:05.833
myself. And I told myself,
"I'm going to starve

03:12:05.900 --> 03:12:09.966
myself, starve to death."
It's something that

03:12:10.900 --> 03:12:14.400
probably many men have.
They're ready to die for

03:12:15.000 --> 03:12:21.200
other people or certain principles they
strongly believe in. I'm not alone here.

03:12:22.000 --> 03:12:27.400
I guess Edward Snowden was ready to die as
well, or some other people like Assange.

03:12:29.400 --> 03:12:33.233
Also, at that moment, I realized there
is no way to communicate securely.

03:12:33.266 --> 03:12:37.066
I need to tell my brother what's going
on. They're probably going after him.

03:12:37.100 --> 03:12:42.900
How do I tell him without
betraying him? Because in

03:12:42.933 --> 03:12:50.166
2011, remember, WhatsApp was already
there. I think they launched it in

03:12:50.300 --> 03:12:57.600
2009, but it had zero encryption.
All messages were plaintext

03:12:57.633 --> 03:13:04.100
in transit, meaning that even your
system administrator, let alone your

03:13:04.200 --> 03:13:07.600
carrier, had access to your messages.

03:13:09.533 --> 03:13:15.833
It was only after Telegram started this
push for encryption that these other apps

03:13:16.666 --> 03:13:21.033
suddenly remembered that
privacy was in their DNA,

03:13:22.966 --> 03:13:28.566
as WhatsApp founders famously stated,
but it must have been a dormant gene.

03:13:30.100 --> 03:13:34.300
in 2011. So in 2011, there was no way...

03:13:36.200 --> 03:13:40.200
to send a message in a
secure way. And I also told

03:13:40.233 --> 03:13:44.033
myself, "If I'm going to
survive this, I'm definitely

03:13:44.100 --> 03:13:48.166
launching a secure messaging app." Somehow

03:13:49.133 --> 03:13:53.233
it ended up not being too bad. I
was summoned to the prosecutor,

03:13:55.800 --> 03:14:01.333
answered some silly questions, fewer
questions that I had to answer

03:14:02.933 --> 03:14:06.800
more recently in the
French investigation case.

03:14:08.533 --> 03:14:13.266
But it was the beginning of
the end. It was clear that

03:14:15.933 --> 03:14:21.466
there's no way I'm going to be allowed
to run VK the way I wanted it to run.

03:14:22.600 --> 03:14:27.900
That was the moment I packed my backpack

03:14:29.433 --> 03:14:35.800
and just started to
wait. I moved to a hotel

03:14:37.500 --> 03:14:41.400
and realized any day I can
leave the country. I kept

03:14:43.100 --> 03:14:48.800
running VK. I started to design Telegram

03:14:49.800 --> 03:15:01.233
and assembling the team, but I knew
my days in Russia were numbered.

03:15:01.300 --> 03:15:05.700
- Well, first, I really
have to say for myself,

03:15:06.566 --> 03:15:10.733
millions, maybe hundreds of millions,
maybe the entirety of Earth, thank you

03:15:11.600 --> 03:15:15.500
for putting your life on the line in those cases.
I think freedom of speech is fundamental to

03:15:15.533 --> 03:15:21.500
the flourishing of humanity,
so. And it depends on people

03:15:23.200 --> 03:15:26.500
willing to put everything on the line
for their principles. So, thank you.

03:15:26.533 --> 03:15:33.366
Quick pause. I need a bathroom break.
All right. We're back, and once

03:15:33.400 --> 03:15:37.100
again, we had a super long day,
and the fact that you would

03:15:37.200 --> 03:15:40.400
spend many hours with me,
thank you for powering

03:15:40.433 --> 03:15:45.100
through. We got this. It's
already late at night.

03:15:45.300 --> 03:15:46.900
- Thanks for doing this.

03:15:46.966 --> 03:15:55.133
- Okay. So there is increasing indication,
I think, from things I've seen

03:15:55.133 --> 03:15:58.900
online that Russia is
considering banning Telegram.

03:15:59.433 --> 03:16:03.400
First of all, do you think this might happen?
And what effect do you think this might

03:16:03.433 --> 03:16:07.933
have on humanity? And, in general,
what do you think about this?

03:16:08.000 --> 03:16:11.966
- It can definitely happen. As you said,
there are certain indications. There have

03:16:12.000 --> 03:16:18.933
been certain test attempts to partially
ban it. Telegram is no longer

03:16:19.000 --> 03:16:23.700
accessible in parts of Russia, such as
Dagestan. It would be incredibly sad

03:16:24.533 --> 03:16:28.700
if Russia restores its attempts to

03:16:29.633 --> 03:16:35.200
ban Telegram, because currently it's being used
by its population for all kinds of purposes,

03:16:35.233 --> 03:16:41.433
not just personal communication or economic
business activities. But also, it's

03:16:41.500 --> 03:16:47.333
communication or economic business
activities. But also, it's

03:16:48.566 --> 03:16:55.200
the only platform which allows the Russian people
to access independent sources of information.

03:16:55.233 --> 03:17:02.300
If you think about media outlets such as BBC or
any other non-Russian sources of information,

03:17:03.633 --> 03:17:09.800
they're only accessible in Russia through
Telegram in the form of Telegram channels.

03:17:09.833 --> 03:17:22.333
The websites are banned. Some other social
media sites are banned. And as you said,

03:17:22.366 --> 03:17:26.966
like, there are indications that Russia is

03:17:27.933 --> 03:17:34.600
planning to migrate users from existing
messaging apps such as WhatsApp and Telegram

03:17:34.666 --> 03:17:38.833
to their own homegrown tool,

03:17:40.200 --> 03:17:44.700
which would, of course, be fully
transparent to the government

03:17:45.733 --> 03:17:51.100
and wouldn't allow voices independent from
the government to express themselves.

03:17:51.133 --> 03:17:58.400
It's certainly an alarming trend. We see these
attempts in countries that are not famous for

03:17:59.000 --> 03:18:01.866
countries that are not famous for

03:18:04.000 --> 03:18:07.700
protecting freedom of speech, but
also increasingly in countries that

03:18:09.033 --> 03:18:13.933
have been known to protect
freedoms. And this creates this

03:18:15.633 --> 03:18:19.200
vicious circle, because in a way

03:18:20.600 --> 03:18:24.400
European countries trying
to fight freedom of speech

03:18:26.200 --> 03:18:31.233
under pretext that sound
legitimate, such as combating

03:18:31.266 --> 03:18:35.666
misinformation or election
interference. They create precedents,

03:18:36.733 --> 03:18:42.933
and they legitimize restrictions to
freedom of speech, which then, in turn,

03:18:43.333 --> 03:18:49.366
can be used by authoritarian
regimes. And they would say,

03:18:50.133 --> 03:18:55.700
in places like China or Iran that

03:18:56.766 --> 03:19:06.200
they're not doing anything different.
It's the norm now to restrict voices that

03:19:07.533 --> 03:19:10.000
that don't go in line with
the mainstream narrative.

03:19:11.800 --> 03:19:14.633
That's sad, because one
of the things that makes

03:19:15.700 --> 03:19:21.333
our life interesting is
this abundance of different

03:19:22.200 --> 03:19:29.400
viewpoints, of different people that
we get to experience. You limit the

03:19:29.433 --> 03:19:32.733
freedom of people, you
inevitably decelerate

03:19:33.400 --> 03:19:36.800
economic growth, level of
happiness, the way people

03:19:36.900 --> 03:19:40.433
can contribute to society,
the way people can express

03:19:40.466 --> 03:19:43.966
themselves. I personally think
it would be a huge mistake

03:19:45.400 --> 03:19:47.100
to ban a tool like Telegram

03:19:49.200 --> 03:19:53.133
in any country, particularly a large
country such as Russia, because

03:19:53.166 --> 03:20:00.500
the Russian people are incredibly talented
and resilient people. They are among the

03:20:00.700 --> 03:20:07.333
first to start utilizing some of these
recent innovations that Telegram

03:20:07.433 --> 03:20:13.133
implements. They are the early
adopters. I'd say them and

03:20:14.400 --> 03:20:19.000
also the Americans, perhaps other people
from Eastern Europe like Ukrainians

03:20:20.100 --> 03:20:23.766
and Southeast Asians. They're
among the first people to start

03:20:23.866 --> 03:20:32.333
using any new addition that we launch.
They're incredibly hungry for innovation.

03:20:32.400 --> 03:20:34.100
- So all that said there's

03:20:35.666 --> 03:20:39.633
as part of the propaganda, and in general,
there's attacks on you all over the place.

03:20:39.666 --> 03:20:43.566
There's misinformation. I've
read a bunch of things that

03:20:43.600 --> 03:20:51.700
are, I think, in a systematic way,
lying about you, lying about Telegram

03:20:51.766 --> 03:20:55.933
from all angles. Why do you get
attacked so much by everybody?

03:20:56.133 --> 03:21:02.766
- Well, protecting freedom of speech is
not a way to make a lot of friends.

03:21:03.533 --> 03:21:07.566
Because you would inevitably find
yourself in a situation where

03:21:10.266 --> 03:21:14.033
you would be protecting the freedom of the

03:21:14.100 --> 03:21:18.333
opposition to the current
government in any country

03:21:20.766 --> 03:21:26.600
to express themselves. And then
the initial reaction and a very

03:21:27.833 --> 03:21:36.233
basic, instinctive reaction of any government
would be to say, "Oh, our opposition

03:21:37.800 --> 03:21:41.233
shouldn't be trusted and allowed to
express themselves, because they're

03:21:42.866 --> 03:21:46.433
actually agents of some foreign

03:21:46.733 --> 03:21:50.366
rival, a geopolitical force
that wants to destroy our

03:21:50.400 --> 03:21:57.200
country." This is something that every
authoritarian regime in history used. You take

03:21:57.233 --> 03:22:05.933
Stalinist Russia or Nazi
Germany, Maoist China. They'd

03:22:07.066 --> 03:22:10.900
always use the same trick. They'd say,
"We need to limit your freedom of speech,

03:22:11.000 --> 03:22:16.133
because these people who are
masquerading as opposition are

03:22:16.200 --> 03:22:21.300
actually the agents of this
other country that wants to take

03:22:21.366 --> 03:22:25.200
over. That's why, dear
citizens, forget about your

03:22:25.200 --> 03:22:29.933
freedoms." And now increasingly,
you see similar attempts in

03:22:30.900 --> 03:22:36.300
free countries. The initial instinct
from, say, President Macron's team

03:22:37.200 --> 03:22:41.133
when they're confronted with some footage,

03:22:41.200 --> 03:22:46.366
for example, the footage of his wife
slapping him, would be to say it's all fake

03:22:48.033 --> 03:22:52.833
Russian imagery, something that is

03:22:54.000 --> 03:23:01.400
inaccurate, something that is misinformation
or interference. And then when they are

03:23:01.433 --> 03:23:08.466
confronted with more information, they
have to refine the narrative. So when

03:23:11.033 --> 03:23:14.500
you find yourself in a situation
that you're running this platform

03:23:16.000 --> 03:23:20.333
like Telegram, and then you
protect the freedom to express

03:23:21.433 --> 03:23:30.433
ideas that don't go in line with the
mainstream narrative, you often find yourself

03:23:32.500 --> 03:23:38.633
in this crossfire when the
forces in power will say that

03:23:38.700 --> 03:23:42.300
you must be working with some
foreign government that they don't

03:23:42.333 --> 03:23:45.966
like. Inevitably they would say that.

03:23:46.033 --> 03:23:50.000
"Oh, if you're protecting
these voices, it's not right."

03:23:50.033 --> 03:23:55.733
They love you when you're protecting
the freedom of speech in a country

03:23:56.833 --> 03:24:00.800
that is far from them, or
better yet, in a country

03:24:00.866 --> 03:24:04.633
that is their geopolitical rival.
They praise you for that, but

03:24:04.666 --> 03:24:09.866
then they have this bipolar attitude

03:24:11.366 --> 03:24:15.300
when you do the same in their own
country. And they say, "No, no, no,

03:24:15.333 --> 03:24:19.300
no, no. We loved you for protecting
freedom of speech, but not here.

03:24:19.400 --> 03:24:22.866
Not in my backyard. We don't
need it here. We're all

03:24:22.933 --> 03:24:31.600
right. We have free press." And then you
will find yourself in this weird spot and

03:24:31.666 --> 03:24:34.900
Ukrainians say you work for the Russians,
the Russians say you work for the

03:24:34.966 --> 03:24:43.800
Ukrainians. And all this schizophrenia
is something that we had to deal with

03:24:43.966 --> 03:24:50.300
for some time, because it's a
very easy way to attack you. At

03:24:50.433 --> 03:24:57.200
some point you don't understand
where it is coming from. Is it our

03:24:57.233 --> 03:25:01.000
competitors? And we must give
credit to our competitors if

03:25:01.066 --> 03:25:04.300
it's their invention to launch
these kind of rumors, because

03:25:06.466 --> 03:25:12.200
at a certain point they must have realized
they can't compete technologically

03:25:13.400 --> 03:25:17.333
on the product side, so they
must do something like this. Or

03:25:17.366 --> 03:25:20.800
it's just governments launching these
rumors, trying to discredit the

03:25:20.866 --> 03:25:24.833
platform, trying to scare
their citizens away from

03:25:24.900 --> 03:25:32.433
it, because they understand that their
power and grip over their own country is in

03:25:32.466 --> 03:25:39.066
danger, as long as they allow a
pro-freedom platform to operate.

03:25:39.100 --> 03:25:42.866
- And through all of this, we should
say over and over that you are

03:25:42.966 --> 03:25:46.966
simply preserving the
freedom of speech for all

03:25:47.000 --> 03:25:49.866
people of Earth, no
matter what they believe,

03:25:51.066 --> 03:25:54.233
as long as they don't call for
violence and as long as they're not

03:25:54.266 --> 03:25:58.000
doing some of the criminal activity
that we discussed, including terrorist

03:25:58.600 --> 03:26:02.400
organizing. But other than that, it doesn't
matter what their belief, left wing or right

03:26:02.433 --> 03:26:06.166
wing. You're just preserving their freedom of
speech. You think people of Ukraine, people of

03:26:06.200 --> 03:26:09.866
Russia, and people of Iran, people
of all over the world understand

03:26:09.900 --> 03:26:14.033
that, despite the propaganda against you?

03:26:14.100 --> 03:26:17.766
- I think people are
smart. Every time I meet

03:26:17.800 --> 03:26:21.366
somebody from one of these countries
you mentioned, in real life, people

03:26:21.400 --> 03:26:28.566
recognize me in the street. Say here
in Dubai, they come over, they seem

03:26:28.633 --> 03:26:35.566
incredibly grateful and understanding.
The propaganda in each of these countries

03:26:37.033 --> 03:26:41.500
would tell them a number of things,
but they learned to discount it.

03:26:42.700 --> 03:26:48.866
That's why they're so happy that Telegram
exists, is because the way they can

03:26:50.900 --> 03:26:55.166
understand the world
around them is to receive

03:26:56.033 --> 03:27:03.633
conflicting, mutually exclusive viewpoints
from sources that hate each other,

03:27:03.700 --> 03:27:07.466
and try to understand what really is

03:27:07.466 --> 03:27:10.466
true, because there's no such
thing as an unbiased source of

03:27:10.533 --> 03:27:17.966
information. When the war
in Ukraine started in 2022,

03:27:19.866 --> 03:27:23.500
I instantly realized
Telegram is going to be used

03:27:24.366 --> 03:27:27.766
to spread propaganda by both sides,

03:27:30.433 --> 03:27:34.266
and I didn't want Telegram to
be used as a tool for war.

03:27:34.366 --> 03:27:38.800
I said, and I posted it publicly,
I suggested maybe we should just

03:27:41.366 --> 03:27:44.900
suspend the activity of
all politics- related

03:27:45.000 --> 03:27:48.033
channels in both countries
for the time of the war.

03:27:50.566 --> 03:27:53.433
Maybe we shouldn't have channels
in these two countries.

03:27:55.200 --> 03:28:04.733
And then, interestingly, people from
both countries revolted against this.

03:28:07.000 --> 03:28:13.300
They told me, both people in Ukraine and in
Russia, that I don't get to babysit them

03:28:14.366 --> 03:28:19.166
and decide for them what
sources of information that

03:28:20.133 --> 03:28:25.700
they have to be granted access
to. They are grownups that can

03:28:26.733 --> 03:28:30.233
make these decisions for themselves. They

03:28:30.266 --> 03:28:32.900
understand that there
is a lot of propaganda.

03:28:33.766 --> 03:28:40.600
They learn to see through this propaganda.
They learn to be able to tell truth from

03:28:40.666 --> 03:28:45.433
lie. And in this time of war, it
was particularly valuable for them

03:28:46.900 --> 03:28:53.700
to receive as much information as possible,
because their relatives, their friends

03:28:55.333 --> 03:28:59.166
were getting affected and are
still getting affected. They

03:28:59.200 --> 03:29:00.600
want to understand what was going on.

03:29:02.200 --> 03:29:05.600
At that point, I realized people
are smart, people get it.

03:29:07.066 --> 03:29:11.066
People can see through it. If you
ask most people in any of these

03:29:11.100 --> 03:29:12.633
countries, "Do you agree that

03:29:15.000 --> 03:29:19.300
access to Telegram should be restricted
for whatever reason?" They would say no.

03:29:19.366 --> 03:29:21.000
- They hunger to have a voice.

03:29:21.500 --> 03:29:27.166
- They need a voice and they need a
place to share their opinion securely.

03:29:28.166 --> 03:29:31.666
- I have to ask you, on the
question of leadership in

03:29:31.766 --> 03:29:39.366
the LaPointe interview, the journalist said
that you're often compared to Elon Musk.

03:29:39.466 --> 03:29:43.300
And you highlighted some interesting
nuances around that, that you're

03:29:43.300 --> 03:29:47.100
quite different; that Elon
runs several companies at once

03:29:47.166 --> 03:29:50.800
while you only run one, and Elon can

03:29:50.866 --> 03:29:54.833
lean more on the emotional
side while you deliberate and

03:29:54.866 --> 03:29:57.800
think deeply before
acting. Can you expand on

03:29:57.833 --> 03:30:01.466
this? Also, there's an interesting
point that you made that

03:30:01.533 --> 03:30:05.133
everybody's weakness is also a strength.
Everybody's strength is also a

03:30:05.133 --> 03:30:11.800
weakness. There's a dual nature to
all our characteristics. So, on the

03:30:11.833 --> 03:30:15.766
topic of Elon, what have
you learned from his style

03:30:15.866 --> 03:30:20.066
of leadership? What do
you respect about him?

03:30:20.100 --> 03:30:25.133
- First of all, I don't think
there is such thing as

03:30:26.466 --> 03:30:29.966
a negative personal
trait. In most cases, our

03:30:30.966 --> 03:30:34.300
bad traits and our good traits are the
same trait or at least have the same

03:30:34.400 --> 03:30:38.800
source. Of course, there are
some extreme examples, but

03:30:39.600 --> 03:30:46.933
I'd say 99% of people, if you analyze
their character, their bravery can be seen

03:30:46.966 --> 03:30:50.566
in recklessness in other situations.
Depending on circumstances,

03:30:51.733 --> 03:30:59.633
you would see exactly the same personality
trait, and it would be either a good

03:30:59.666 --> 03:31:03.400
thing or a bad thing,
because humanity is perfect

03:31:04.333 --> 03:31:10.566
as a whole and each of us is different
for a reason. We have evolved to be

03:31:10.600 --> 03:31:18.700
different, to complement each other's
abilities so that together we are invincible.

03:31:20.666 --> 03:31:23.633
And even if you take a person

03:31:25.100 --> 03:31:31.866
as complicated as Elon, I believe
that certain traits that Elon

03:31:31.933 --> 03:31:37.866
demonstrates, that people
criticize about him, are

03:31:37.900 --> 03:31:43.300
also the sources of his strength.
For example, his emotionality

03:31:44.900 --> 03:31:48.500
is derived from the fact that
he cares about issues deeply

03:31:50.100 --> 03:31:55.066
and he is willing to start as many
wars and as many fights as it takes

03:31:56.066 --> 03:32:00.833
to change the world in the
direction that he thinks is right.

03:32:01.766 --> 03:32:05.166
He also seems to be able
to extract motivation

03:32:06.166 --> 03:32:12.466
from all these wars and personal conflicts,
which is, again, not something to be

03:32:13.200 --> 03:32:18.900
underestimated. At a certain
point in the life of a

03:32:18.966 --> 03:32:22.733
successful entrepreneur, the question of

03:32:22.800 --> 03:32:27.666
motivation starts to be the primary
question. If we're talking about the most

03:32:29.100 --> 03:32:33.066
richest person in the world and
the most famous entrepreneur in

03:32:33.100 --> 03:32:42.200
the world, you have to wonder, how does
he motivate himself? And if starting

03:32:45.466 --> 03:32:49.700
a war on X, debating certain issues,

03:32:51.300 --> 03:32:58.700
or becoming personal with other CEOs,
criticizing them, if these activities help

03:32:59.200 --> 03:33:02.233
Elon to innovate

03:33:04.633 --> 03:33:11.466
and start new projects, he should be doing
more of it. There's nothing wrong in

03:33:13.266 --> 03:33:20.100
being non-agreeable. Actually, it's
one of the main traits of a successful

03:33:20.100 --> 03:33:23.366
entrepreneur, not agreeing with things.

03:33:24.833 --> 03:33:28.600
And every time somebody like Elon... But
there is no somebody like Elon. There's just

03:33:28.700 --> 03:33:32.500
Elon. I think, at least
from the entrepreneurs I

03:33:32.500 --> 03:33:39.233
know and I personally interacted with,
he's unique in the sense that he keeps

03:33:40.066 --> 03:33:43.666
launching new things,
running them in parallel,

03:33:45.266 --> 03:33:51.466
and he doesn't seem to be stretched too
thin. Well, some people think he is, but

03:33:53.166 --> 03:34:02.533
he manages to still demonstrate success
in all or most of his endeavors.

03:34:03.666 --> 03:34:07.433
So again, you can criticize Elon for
being emotional, but would he be the

03:34:07.500 --> 03:34:12.466
same person without this? I doubt that.

03:34:12.500 --> 03:34:15.966
- and the incredible teams he's motivated
too. There's an element of that,

03:34:16.666 --> 03:34:21.533
which you've spoken about. The
team at Telegram, you know,

03:34:23.000 --> 03:34:26.900
assembling a team of A-players, as

03:34:26.966 --> 03:34:30.833
we've talked about, is a skill
in itself. And that's also a

03:34:30.866 --> 03:34:36.000
big part of the the leaders that
we've discussed, is like what,

03:34:36.833 --> 03:34:39.366
judged in part by the team you assemble.

03:34:39.400 --> 03:34:44.100
- Yes. And one of the necessary
character features to enable that is

03:34:45.166 --> 03:34:50.666
to be ready to be unpleasant. You have
to be ready to insult some people

03:34:51.700 --> 03:34:56.833
if their work is inferior.
You have to be ready to fire

03:34:56.866 --> 03:35:02.266
them without remorse. So in order to be

03:35:02.400 --> 03:35:06.766
an efficient and great entrepreneur
and enrich the world of innovations,

03:35:08.666 --> 03:35:13.366
you have to do unpleasant things. Most
people will shy away from it. And

03:35:17.066 --> 03:35:23.800
in a certain sense, entrepreneurs
sacrifice their peace of mind in order to

03:35:26.266 --> 03:35:31.566
contribute to the world around them.
And Elon is a great example of that.

03:35:31.633 --> 03:35:34.466
- I have to ask you about the
big picture of Telegram.

03:35:34.566 --> 03:35:39.066
We've already talked about the
fact that you own 100% of it,

03:35:40.033 --> 03:35:43.733
and there's a lot on the business side of
it. The business structure of Telegram is

03:35:43.800 --> 03:35:51.066
fascinating. You've invested 100, maybe
hundreds of millions of dollars of your own

03:35:51.100 --> 03:35:56.166
money. As far as I know, you
take a salary of what? $1?

03:35:57.200 --> 03:36:01.200
- One dirham is one-third of that.

03:36:01.266 --> 03:36:06.900
- One-third of a dollar. And
in 2024 was the first time

03:36:07.100 --> 03:36:10.833
Telegram was profitable. So one
of the interesting questions is,

03:36:10.866 --> 03:36:14.866
here, that we could talk for many hours
about, but I'd love to get a high-level

03:36:15.133 --> 03:36:17.966
view picture. You've left what I

03:36:18.633 --> 03:36:22.266
understand, what I think is a
huge amount of money on the table

03:36:22.833 --> 03:36:26.633
by sticking to your principles. For
example, not doing advertisement

03:36:27.133 --> 03:36:30.966
that's based on user private
data, which basically every

03:36:31.066 --> 03:36:34.766
social media company does. So,
the only advertisement that

03:36:34.766 --> 03:36:38.633
Telegram does is based on channels
and groups, based on the topic,

03:36:38.700 --> 03:36:42.666
not the private data of the
individuals. And the other thing

03:36:42.766 --> 03:36:46.666
is, which is also gangster
and incredible, is you

03:36:46.666 --> 03:36:51.133
don't do a news feed, which
is the most addictive and

03:36:52.166 --> 03:36:59.866
engagement-inducing aspect of social media,
which feeds the very kind of addictive

03:36:59.933 --> 03:37:05.333
downside of the internet: the distraction,
the engagement drama, farming

03:37:06.633 --> 03:37:10.433
aspect that we've talked about in the
very beginning that you try to resist,

03:37:10.533 --> 03:37:14.400
that you think is damaging the human
mind at scale. So anyway, that's

03:37:14.466 --> 03:37:18.333
just speaking to the fact that you're
leaving a lot of money on the table. So,

03:37:18.366 --> 03:37:22.233
how the hell were you able to be
profitable? What are the ways that Telegram

03:37:22.266 --> 03:37:23.466
makes money?

03:37:23.500 --> 03:37:29.433
- Yeah. We had to innovate a lot in order
to reach a point where we are profitable

03:37:31.400 --> 03:37:37.966
without having to resort to dubious
business activities involving

03:37:38.333 --> 03:37:46.833
exploiting personal data of users,
something that most of our competitors do.

03:37:47.966 --> 03:37:55.366
Because money has never been the primary
goal, at least not for me. When I sold

03:37:56.733 --> 03:38:03.633
the remaining share of my first company,
I had to do it below market price because

03:38:05.533 --> 03:38:08.766
I didn't leave Russia completely
without any pressures, you know?

03:38:11.233 --> 03:38:16.600
I reinvested the vast majority
of everything in Telegram.

03:38:18.933 --> 03:38:22.800
Telegram is an operation that is
losing money for me personally. I

03:38:22.866 --> 03:38:27.766
never... I didn't extract more from
Telegram than I invested in it. I never

03:38:28.566 --> 03:38:31.766
sold a single share.

03:38:33.333 --> 03:38:36.933
But I also didn't want to sell Telegram,
so how do you reach a point when you're

03:38:36.933 --> 03:38:40.766
profitable without
sacrificing your values?

03:38:40.866 --> 03:38:43.866
One of the ideas we
explored was a subscription

03:38:43.933 --> 03:38:47.666
model, but only for certain additional

03:38:47.766 --> 03:38:54.666
features. We wanted to keep all the
existing features free, and just add

03:38:55.766 --> 03:39:02.133
more business-related tools
or tools for advanced users

03:39:02.666 --> 03:39:07.600
that they would have to pay for,
say, four or five dollars a month.

03:39:09.366 --> 03:39:14.166
It was quite unprecedented at
the time. It wasn't considered

03:39:14.466 --> 03:39:18.400
a viable option for messaging
apps to do that. We launched the

03:39:18.466 --> 03:39:24.633
premium subscriptions
for Telegram in 2022,

03:39:25.633 --> 03:39:32.033
and now we have over 15 million
paid subscribers. This is

03:39:33.566 --> 03:39:38.733
some very significant recurring
revenue. So, we would

03:39:38.766 --> 03:39:45.633
receive more than half a billion
dollars from premium subscriptions

03:39:45.700 --> 03:39:50.266
alone this year, and it's growing fast.

03:39:51.766 --> 03:39:56.466
For that, we had to innovate a lot. We
included over 50 different features

03:39:57.466 --> 03:40:01.166
into the premium package.
And then, how do you

03:40:02.166 --> 03:40:06.000
make an app that is already
more powerful than any other

03:40:06.066 --> 03:40:07.433
messaging app on the market

03:40:10.566 --> 03:40:13.866
even more useful so that people
would be ready to pay for this

03:40:14.633 --> 03:40:18.666
extra? That wasn't easy.
That took a lot of effort.

03:40:18.966 --> 03:40:20.666
- And you're constantly adding features.

03:40:20.700 --> 03:40:21.900
- We're constantly adding features.

03:40:21.933 --> 03:40:25.533
- That's actually fun to watch, just the
rate of adding... And some of them are

03:40:25.566 --> 03:40:32.400
subtle, like the updates to improvements,
expansions of polls, for example.

03:40:32.433 --> 03:40:35.733
- Yes. You keep improving the
existing features and adding new

03:40:35.800 --> 03:40:40.733
ones, and every time when you add a new
feature, you don't want to clutter the app.

03:40:41.333 --> 03:40:47.433
So, in a way, they're not in your
way. They're invisible. That's not an

03:40:47.466 --> 03:40:51.066
easy thing to do. And most of the
features maybe are not even known

03:40:51.766 --> 03:40:55.700
to the majority of our users. But
when you need them, they're there.

03:40:56.533 --> 03:41:00.466
So, premium is one source of
our revenue. We also have ads,

03:41:00.533 --> 03:41:06.400
but they're context-based, not
targeted. Of course, we leave probably

03:41:07.666 --> 03:41:14.533
80% of value on the table because we are
not ready to engage in all those practices,

03:41:14.566 --> 03:41:15.833
exporting personal data.

03:41:15.866 --> 03:41:19.266
- Just to be clear,
targeted ads is what most

03:41:19.933 --> 03:41:23.600
social media companies, most tech companies
that do any kind of advertisement

03:41:23.633 --> 03:41:29.733
do, and that's the kind of advertisement
that uses personal data from users,

03:41:30.600 --> 03:41:34.633
just to clarify. And when you
said 80%, that's a lot of money.

03:41:34.666 --> 03:41:38.266
- Of course, because we would never
use, for example, your personal

03:41:38.366 --> 03:41:42.333
messaging data, or your
contacts data, or your

03:41:42.400 --> 03:41:46.400
metadata, or your activity
data to target ads.

03:41:47.666 --> 03:41:53.166
It's sad that it became
synonymous with the internet

03:41:53.266 --> 03:42:00.066
industry, this kind of
exploitation, but we are happy

03:42:00.066 --> 03:42:03.800
with the fact that we managed to
make Telegram profitable despite

03:42:03.833 --> 03:42:07.800
that. We're also experimenting a lot with

03:42:08.700 --> 03:42:12.366
blockchain-based
technologies. We're the first

03:42:12.900 --> 03:42:16.133
app to allow people to directly
own their username and

03:42:16.233 --> 03:42:19.400
their digital identities using smart

03:42:19.500 --> 03:42:23.366
contracts and NFTs, removing
Telegram from the picture.

03:42:23.400 --> 03:42:27.100
So, for example, Telegram
cannot confiscate

03:42:27.300 --> 03:42:34.766
your username from you. It's impossible.
We do a lot of things related to

03:42:35.900 --> 03:42:39.600
the ecosystem of Telegram.
We have a thriving mini app

03:42:39.866 --> 03:42:47.866
platform, millions of mini app developers
launching their own bots and applications.

03:42:48.266 --> 03:42:52.933
- So, a lot of people are making millions
of dollars on the Telegram platform?

03:42:53.000 --> 03:42:56.700
- Yes, we enable them to receive

03:42:58.300 --> 03:43:05.633
payments from the users through in-app
purchase mechanisms provided by Apple and

03:43:05.666 --> 03:43:10.966
Google, which I think was the
first attempt of this kind

03:43:12.566 --> 03:43:16.366
to allow that both on iOS and
Android, and on a big platform, so

03:43:16.400 --> 03:43:19.700
that third-party developers of
mini apps, which are basically

03:43:20.033 --> 03:43:23.666
websites so deeply
integrated into Telegram

03:43:24.033 --> 03:43:27.933
that you can't tell whether
they're standalone or

03:43:27.966 --> 03:43:33.400
they're part of the overall
experience. And by providing this

03:43:34.466 --> 03:43:43.533
payment option, we are able to extract a
commission from these transactions. But

03:43:43.566 --> 03:43:50.200
it's a very low commission. Presently,
it's 5%. So we aren't greedy

03:43:50.266 --> 03:43:53.800
here. We want people to succeed

03:43:54.733 --> 03:44:01.100
in building these tools for our users. We
understand that mini apps bring us users.

03:44:03.266 --> 03:44:06.966
The more users we have, the
more successful and relevant

03:44:07.500 --> 03:44:11.466
Telegram becomes. We need
third-party developers. I think,

03:44:11.500 --> 03:44:18.900
at this point, Telegram gives
developers, by far, the most powerful

03:44:18.900 --> 03:44:20.966
tools to create.

03:44:21.000 --> 03:44:24.966
- Plus, there's a bot API, and you
have to tell me about the TON

03:44:25.066 --> 03:44:28.266
blockchain and the crypto
ecosystem available through

03:44:28.333 --> 03:44:34.200
Telegram. So what is TON, also known
as, the Open Network Blockchain?

03:44:34.266 --> 03:44:40.233
- TON is a blockchain technology that we
initially developed in 2018 and 2019,

03:44:41.066 --> 03:44:45.000
and we started to develop it because
we needed a blockchain platform to

03:44:45.066 --> 03:44:48.800
be integrated deeply into
Telegram because we believe in

03:44:48.800 --> 03:44:52.300
blockchain. We think it's one
of the technologies that enable

03:44:52.400 --> 03:45:00.233
freedom. But, at the time, if you look at
Bitcoin, if you look at Ethereum, they were

03:45:00.300 --> 03:45:04.166
not scalable enough to
cope with the load that

03:45:04.500 --> 03:45:09.366
our hundreds of millions of users would
create. They would just become congested.

03:45:10.833 --> 03:45:14.766
And I asked my brother, "Can we
create a blockchain platform

03:45:15.466 --> 03:45:21.866
that would be inherently scalable so that
no matter how many users or transactions

03:45:21.900 --> 03:45:25.900
there are, it would split
into smaller pieces, which we

03:45:25.933 --> 03:45:29.400
call shard chains, and
would still process all

03:45:29.466 --> 03:45:33.200
transactions?" And he thought
for a few days and said,

03:45:33.233 --> 03:45:37.000
"Yes, it's possible, but it's not
easy." When we started building it,

03:45:37.100 --> 03:45:41.733
we ended up succeeding in developing that
technology, but we couldn't release it

03:45:43.066 --> 03:45:50.100
because the SEC, the Securities
and Exchange Commission in the

03:45:50.166 --> 03:45:53.933
United States was unhappy with the way

03:45:56.700 --> 03:46:02.633
the fundraise for TON was
conducted. So we had to abandon the

03:46:02.666 --> 03:46:10.366
project, and the open-source community
took over. Luckily, because we

03:46:10.400 --> 03:46:14.400
constantly conducted those
contests with third-party

03:46:14.433 --> 03:46:19.100
developers, there was a
thriving community around TON,

03:46:20.833 --> 03:46:24.766
which now stood for The
Open Network as opposed to

03:46:24.800 --> 03:46:30.233
its prior name, Telegram Open
Network. And so this project

03:46:31.633 --> 03:46:38.733
got eventually launched without
our direct involvement, and it's

03:46:38.966 --> 03:46:46.433
thriving now because everything we
do, like I said, is blockchain-based,

03:46:46.466 --> 03:46:50.466
tokenized, usernames, Telegram accounts

03:46:51.400 --> 03:46:54.933
are all based on TON and
its smart contracts.

03:46:54.966 --> 03:46:59.566
It's the only way for third-party
developers and creators

03:47:02.300 --> 03:47:04.833
to withdraw the funds that they

03:47:05.933 --> 03:47:09.733
earn through our revenue-sharing
programs. For example, with

03:47:09.766 --> 03:47:15.500
channel owners, we do a
50/50 split of ad revenues.

03:47:16.633 --> 03:47:19.600
It's also the only way to
transact on Telegram. For

03:47:19.666 --> 03:47:24.500
example, if you want to buy ads
on Telegram, you should use TON.

03:47:25.600 --> 03:47:31.466
All the new things we launch, for
example, gifts that we mentioned

03:47:31.500 --> 03:47:40.200
earlier, which you can define as a
reinvented socially relevant NFT

03:47:41.266 --> 03:47:48.133
integrated into a billion-user ecosystem,
but at the same time, available on-chain,

03:47:48.200 --> 03:47:55.500
transferable, which you can own
directly, also based on TON. Incredibly

03:47:56.200 --> 03:48:03.433
fast-growing space. We only launched them
about half a year ago. And now as a result of

03:48:03.500 --> 03:48:11.233
these Telegram gifts, TON has become, I
think, the largest or the second-largest

03:48:12.300 --> 03:48:18.466
blockchain in terms of
daily NFT trading volumes.

03:48:19.033 --> 03:48:23.000
- So, like you mentioned, it is a
Layer 1 technology as opposed

03:48:23.033 --> 03:48:26.833
to being built on top of Ethereum
or Bitcoin. And it's able to

03:48:26.933 --> 03:48:30.466
achieve the scale and the
speed of transactions that's

03:48:30.533 --> 03:48:33.966
needed for something like
Telegram. And like you also

03:48:34.000 --> 03:48:37.333
mentioned, the gifts.
You recently launched

03:48:39.233 --> 03:48:46.466
some Snoop Dogg gifts. Are there going to
be some other celebrities in the pipeline?

03:48:46.533 --> 03:48:50.466
- Yeah, I'm a big fan of Snoop, and
that's why when they reached out,

03:48:50.533 --> 03:48:53.266
suggesting to do something together,
I said, "Let's launch some

03:48:53.566 --> 03:48:59.400
Snoop-related gifts." And it was
really fun. We managed to sell

03:49:00.333 --> 03:49:04.466
12 million worth of
gifts within 30 minutes.

03:49:04.500 --> 03:49:08.933
- 30 minutes. Well, there you go.
I even got a few, but yeah.

03:49:08.933 --> 03:49:15.600
- After this, we have many requests from
many really high-profile influencers

03:49:17.000 --> 03:49:19.000
that, in a way, are lining up.

03:49:19.066 --> 03:49:22.800
- So, from my perspective as a fan, it's just
interesting to see what kind of art you create for

03:49:22.866 --> 03:49:26.833
any kind of celebrities,
athletes, musicians, because the

03:49:26.866 --> 03:49:30.500
the Snoop, the Snoop gifts are all

03:49:30.566 --> 03:49:33.933
just, like, going back to our previous
conversation, just beautiful pieces of art

03:49:35.366 --> 03:49:42.366
that, like, encapsulate certain memes,
certain aspects of Snoop that everybody

03:49:42.433 --> 03:49:46.033
knows. These cultural icons
that he represents. That's

03:49:46.066 --> 03:49:49.800
cool. That's just... and they're,
the detail, the incredible

03:49:49.900 --> 03:49:53.600
detail of the art of the individual
gifts is just incredible.

03:49:53.666 --> 03:50:01.500
- And each of these gifts is scalable
because it's vector-based. It references

03:50:01.533 --> 03:50:08.633
certain points in Snoop's creative
biography. And each of them has countless

03:50:08.966 --> 03:50:15.833
different versions. We had to create
over 50 distinctive versions of each.

03:50:16.200 --> 03:50:19.833
And then each individual piece is
unique because it also has unique

03:50:19.866 --> 03:50:23.633
background, unique icon
in the background. It's

03:50:23.666 --> 03:50:26.366
something that we reinvented because we

03:50:27.233 --> 03:50:30.133
didn't like the old school
NFTs. First of all, they were

03:50:31.133 --> 03:50:37.066
not relevant socially because, okay, you
have an NFT. Where do you demonstrate

03:50:37.133 --> 03:50:40.166
it? In a Telegram, a Telegram gift is

03:50:40.900 --> 03:50:44.233
there next to your name. It's
part of your digital identity on

03:50:44.266 --> 03:50:48.233
Telegram. And then you can create
collections of gifts and show it off on your

03:50:48.266 --> 03:50:54.733
profile page. But it also... The other
thing that we wanted to reinvent is

03:50:55.666 --> 03:50:59.666
the aesthetic part of it.
Most NFTs are just ugly

03:51:01.466 --> 03:51:09.066
and they're not based on any sophisticated
technology. So, what we did with

03:51:09.133 --> 03:51:15.800
Snoop's gifts, I think
represents an example of

03:51:18.600 --> 03:51:25.033
beautiful, aesthetically pleasing, and
at the same time very accurate in terms

03:51:25.100 --> 03:51:29.933
of references to this
specific artist's biography,

03:51:30.833 --> 03:51:34.466
a mixture between art and
technology, which I think is quite

03:51:34.533 --> 03:51:38.200
rare. I'm quite proud of it. I
think it's a new trend, a new

03:51:38.233 --> 03:51:42.166
phenomenon. It's only half a year old. So,

03:51:42.233 --> 03:51:47.766
let's see where it goes. We're
gonna select our next influencer

03:51:48.933 --> 03:51:51.433
or artist to be part of it.

03:51:51.466 --> 03:51:55.133
- Hey, listen, I'm really proud I
got a Snoop gift next to my name

03:51:55.666 --> 03:51:59.533
and I figured out that you can add
even more by pinning them. It's like a

03:51:59.600 --> 03:52:01.966
cool little art icon.

03:52:02.033 --> 03:52:05.766
- We didn't expect it, by the
way. We just had a lot of fun

03:52:05.833 --> 03:52:09.600
launching these things
and then we realized

03:52:09.666 --> 03:52:15.133
that one of the first collections we issued,
we sold each piece at something like

03:52:16.766 --> 03:52:25.266
$5. And then the minimum price of any
items in these collections currently is

03:52:25.333 --> 03:52:30.233
something like $10,000.
And it keeps going up.

03:52:31.133 --> 03:52:36.566
So, I was quite surprised with the
reception. I realized, you know, when

03:52:36.700 --> 03:52:40.533
you are trying to monetize a
social media platform in a way

03:52:40.600 --> 03:52:48.133
that is consistent with your values, you're
forced to find ways that benefit your

03:52:48.233 --> 03:52:55.533
users, not exploit them. People love these
gifts. People love the fact that they can

03:52:55.566 --> 03:52:59.400
congratulate a person close to
them with something valuable

03:52:59.766 --> 03:53:03.566
and at the same time something
beautiful. Also, some people

03:53:03.633 --> 03:53:06.666
make a business out of it, which
is funny. They resell these

03:53:06.733 --> 03:53:11.933
gifts. We recently met a guy who
earned several million dollars

03:53:14.500 --> 03:53:16.566
just from buying and selling gifts.

03:53:16.566 --> 03:53:17.666
- It's a real market.

03:53:17.700 --> 03:53:20.900
- It's a real market. And it's just
something that he did in a few

03:53:20.966 --> 03:53:24.366
months. And last year when we launched

03:53:26.033 --> 03:53:30.100
many new features for the
mini apps on Telegram and,

03:53:31.000 --> 03:53:33.866
payments options for them
and the other monetization

03:53:33.933 --> 03:53:38.633
options, the same guy earned $12 million

03:53:40.700 --> 03:53:44.100
from mini apps. And I
know several people who

03:53:44.133 --> 03:53:47.566
anecdotally, like, "I earned
$10 million, I earned $3

03:53:47.633 --> 03:53:51.033
million," just in a matter
of months single-handedly.

03:53:51.100 --> 03:53:53.600
Sometimes they would have
a team of two, three

03:53:53.666 --> 03:53:58.566
people. So, whenever I hear
stories from people who

03:54:00.366 --> 03:54:05.833
were able to build businesses on top of
Telegram, this makes me incredibly proud.

03:54:05.866 --> 03:54:09.733
- And mini apps include games,
they include tools, services of

03:54:09.766 --> 03:54:13.733
any kind. It's an app within
the ecosystem of Telegram. Let

03:54:13.766 --> 03:54:17.266
me ask you about crypto in general.
So, you've been an early supporter of,

03:54:17.366 --> 03:54:24.200
cryptocurrencies, Bitcoin. You
bought into Bitcoin early on.

03:54:24.233 --> 03:54:28.200
You kept buying. Maybe you could speak

03:54:28.266 --> 03:54:30.466
to the reasoning why you
kept buying Bitcoin.

03:54:31.433 --> 03:54:35.133
Do you think Bitcoin will go to a million
dollars? Do you think it'll keep increasing?

03:54:36.066 --> 03:54:40.033
And Bitcoin and all the
other cryptocurrencies.

03:54:40.066 --> 03:54:43.800
- I was a big believer in
Bitcoins since more or less the

03:54:43.866 --> 03:54:49.233
start of it. I got to buy my
first few thousand of Bitcoin in

03:54:50.133 --> 03:54:54.000
2013, and I didn't care much. I

03:54:54.033 --> 03:54:57.700
think I bought it at the local
maximum. It's something like

03:54:57.700 --> 03:55:01.600
$700 per Bitcoin, and I just threw a

03:55:01.633 --> 03:55:08.700
couple millions there. And a lot of people
after Bitcoin, later next year, went

03:55:08.733 --> 03:55:15.066
down, somewhere close to
300, 200, started to express

03:55:16.366 --> 03:55:22.100
their sympathy to me. They say,
"Oh, you're a poor fellow. You made

03:55:22.133 --> 03:55:27.366
this horrible mistake investing in this
new thing, but don't feel bad about it.

03:55:28.400 --> 03:55:31.900
We still have some
respect for you." And I,

03:55:32.300 --> 03:55:34.466
my response to them was, "I don't
care. I'm not going to sell

03:55:34.533 --> 03:55:41.300
it." I believe in this thing. I
think this is the way money should

03:55:41.366 --> 03:55:48.500
work. Nobody can confiscate your
Bitcoin from you. Nobody can

03:55:48.566 --> 03:56:01.066
censor you for political reasons. This
is the ultimate means of exchange,

03:56:02.666 --> 03:56:06.266
and again, I'm now talking
about Bitcoin but it relates to

03:56:06.900 --> 03:56:12.600
cryptocurrencies in general.
So I have been able to fund my

03:56:13.533 --> 03:56:18.066
lifestyle, so to say, from my Bitcoin
investment. Some people think

03:56:18.966 --> 03:56:21.766
if I'm able to rent nice locations or

03:56:23.300 --> 03:56:26.633
fly private, it's because I
somehow extract money from

03:56:26.700 --> 03:56:33.500
Telegram. But like I said, Telegram is a
money-losing operation for me personally.

03:56:35.100 --> 03:56:39.800
Bitcoin is something that allowed me to

03:56:41.466 --> 03:56:48.666
stay afloat, and I believe it will come
to a point when Bitcoin is worth $1

03:56:48.700 --> 03:56:52.366
million. Just look at the trends. The

03:56:52.400 --> 03:56:55.900
governments keep printing
money like no tomorrow.

03:56:56.700 --> 03:56:58.466
Nobody's printing Bitcoin. There is

03:57:00.133 --> 03:57:06.133
a predictable inflation and then
it stops at a certain point.

03:57:07.966 --> 03:57:13.000
Bitcoin is here to stay. All the
fiat currencies, remains to be seen.

03:57:13.100 --> 03:57:17.066
- Let me ask you a deeply philosophical, serious
question. In your first Tucker interview

03:57:17.133 --> 03:57:21.100
you had two interesting chairs
in the background. I think they

03:57:21.166 --> 03:57:28.900
reference a now legendary meme the
choice is Пики точеные или хуи дрочёные.

03:57:29.866 --> 03:57:33.433
What is the philosophical wisdom
in the dilemma that these two

03:57:33.566 --> 03:57:37.733
chairs present? Have you had to face
the dilemma yourself personally?

03:57:37.800 --> 03:57:45.266
- Not this exact dilemma. I think this is a
riddle that people have to face in Russian

03:57:45.366 --> 03:57:50.100
prisons. And metaphorically,

03:57:52.066 --> 03:57:57.233
it's describing all the situations
where you're presented a choice

03:57:58.400 --> 03:58:02.366
between two suboptimal
options. When you're running

03:58:02.500 --> 03:58:06.100
a big business or when you're
running a large country, it is

03:58:06.133 --> 03:58:09.833
similar. You sometimes face this
dilemma. What are you going to do?

03:58:11.100 --> 03:58:14.400
This very horrible thing
or this also very horrible

03:58:14.466 --> 03:58:19.100
thing? So I think the right
answer to this riddle is

03:58:20.600 --> 03:58:27.133
not to do any of these
things. Reframe the question.

03:58:28.866 --> 03:58:38.500
Design a solution that turns a disadvantage
into an advantage, and then use it

03:58:39.700 --> 03:58:41.933
to cope with the other
side of the problem.

03:58:43.000 --> 03:58:44.966
So, do you know the answer to that riddle?

03:58:45.000 --> 03:58:51.500
- No, somebody on the internet said: "Не ходи
туда, где задают такие вопросы." Which is

03:58:51.566 --> 03:58:58.666
basically, "Try to avoid
the situations where such

03:58:58.700 --> 03:59:02.366
dilemmas present themselves,
where there's no right answer."

03:59:02.466 --> 03:59:05.700
- This is one of the ways
