without applying effort

04:20:24.000 --> 04:20:32.600
in the direction that seems to be
logical. Maybe some people exist

04:20:33.400 --> 04:20:35.466
that can just sit on the bank of a river

04:20:36.733 --> 04:20:44.066
and materialize things by the power of
their thought, but I'm not sure I'm one of

04:20:44.100 --> 04:20:51.833
these people. I always found it more
easy to believe that if you couple this

04:20:51.900 --> 04:21:04.733
optimism and faith with logical action,
then it is bound to be successful.

04:21:04.766 --> 04:21:12.966
- Prolonged effort, hard work coupled with
positive focus, thinking about the thing.

04:21:13.066 --> 04:21:17.033
- Oh, yes, over many, many,
many days. It is possible

04:21:17.166 --> 04:21:21.533
to imagine our world as a
high dimensional universe

04:21:22.400 --> 04:21:29.100
where humans have the ability to
navigate through it with the power of

04:21:29.900 --> 04:21:34.000
belief, which is coupled with

04:21:36.666 --> 04:21:43.566
positive emotion and logical
thinking. But we are getting into

04:21:43.600 --> 04:21:47.633
an esoteric realm. We don't
have any proof of that,

04:21:49.300 --> 04:21:55.466
but we also know that we probably, at
this point, haven't discovered even 1%

04:21:58.000 --> 04:22:00.600
about this universe.

04:22:00.666 --> 04:22:04.566
- I agree with you fully, and I
like what you said in the way you

04:22:04.600 --> 04:22:08.033
were thinking about it. You've told me

04:22:08.066 --> 04:22:12.033
before that maybe there's a
way that with effort and with

04:22:12.033 --> 04:22:16.000
a focused mind, you can
shape, you can morph the

04:22:16.066 --> 04:22:19.433
sort of landscape of probabilities around

04:22:19.500 --> 04:22:23.766
you, and it's a nice way to visualize
it, that somehow our effort

04:22:24.666 --> 04:22:34.266
and our focus changes the things
that are likely and less likely, and

04:22:34.300 --> 04:22:38.100
by focusing on it, we make the thing
more and more likely. At least as an

04:22:38.200 --> 04:22:42.900
estimate, as a kind of field that we through
our thoughts and through our actions

04:22:43.866 --> 04:22:49.766
change that field, and there's
eight billion of us doing so. And

04:22:49.833 --> 04:22:53.333
together there's this collective intelligence
that creates the world we see around

04:22:53.400 --> 04:23:01.966
us, like the mice. And like you
said, us as a humanity together are

04:23:02.033 --> 04:23:04.700
perfect. I like that you said that.

04:23:05.800 --> 04:23:10.200
- I admire your belief in the
fact that we get to experience

04:23:12.000 --> 04:23:15.033
this together, because it's not obvious.

04:23:15.833 --> 04:23:20.200
Maybe each of us experiences
his own or her own universe,

04:23:21.266 --> 04:23:25.033
and maybe every second the universe
splits into a billion of different

04:23:25.066 --> 04:23:30.933
universes, and everything that can happen,
happens. And there is a universe where,

04:23:33.166 --> 04:23:40.833
say I died in 2013, maybe every time I
die I actually get to shift to a parallel

04:23:40.900 --> 04:23:46.433
universe where I don't die and then
it keeps going and at certain points

04:23:47.666 --> 04:23:52.533
we achieve this quantum immortality
when we are 1,000 years old,

04:23:54.100 --> 04:24:04.600
but a lot of people from other versions
of reality think we are long gone.

04:24:04.666 --> 04:24:08.566
- Yeah, this is something that you explained to me,
the idea of quantum immortality, which is a thought

04:24:08.633 --> 04:24:11.800
experiment, which I find deeply
fascinating. People should look into it.

04:24:11.833 --> 04:24:15.333
it. Which is very crisp, clean

04:24:15.466 --> 04:24:18.800
consequence of the many worlds
interpretation of quantum mechanics,

04:24:19.566 --> 04:24:23.533
that we as conscious beings can't
experience our death. We can

04:24:23.566 --> 04:24:31.300
only... As we branch into these
many worlds, only the living

04:24:32.000 --> 04:24:35.733
consciousnesses get to
experience it. So in some sense,

04:24:35.800 --> 04:24:39.600
yeah, there's many universes. If we're to

04:24:39.633 --> 04:24:43.600
seriously take the many worlds interpretation of
quantum mechanics, there's many universes where you

04:24:43.633 --> 04:24:47.500
died many times, especially you. And I'm glad we're
in a universe where we get to share the table

04:24:47.533 --> 04:24:50.600
we're the, in a universe where
we get to share the table

04:24:51.866 --> 04:24:54.900
with this impressive bond,
a little humor and a lot of

04:24:55.000 --> 04:25:02.066
serious topics covered today. Once again,
I can't say enough, a giant thank you from

04:25:02.100 --> 04:25:05.966
me and a giant thank you from hundreds
of millions of people that follow your

04:25:06.066 --> 04:25:13.866
work for you fighting for the freedom
of all of us to speak and creating a

04:25:13.866 --> 04:25:17.700
platform where we can do so. And
thank you so much for talking

04:25:17.700 --> 04:25:21.600
today, brother. It's been an honor
getting to know you and to be able

04:25:21.666 --> 04:25:22.833
to call you a friend.

04:25:22.900 --> 04:25:27.566
- Thank you for saying that.
I'm also incredibly grateful

04:25:29.200 --> 04:25:32.833
to you and to the fact that I happen
to be in this version of reality.

04:25:33.800 --> 04:25:38.266
When I haven't died, at
least yet. And hopefully

04:25:39.933 --> 04:25:44.000
we'll get to spend more fun moments
in the years to come together.

04:25:44.000 --> 04:25:44.666
- Thank you, brother.

04:25:45.666 --> 04:25:47.266
Thank you for listening
to this conversation

04:25:47.266 --> 04:25:48.700
with Pavel Durov.

04:25:48.700 --> 04:25:49.800
To support this podcast,

04:25:49.800 --> 04:25:52.333
please check out our
sponsors in the description.

04:25:52.333 --> 04:25:53.566
And now, let me try

04:25:53.566 --> 04:25:56.000
to articulate some things
I've been thinking about.

04:25:56.000 --> 04:25:59.600
If you would like to submit
questions or topics like this

04:25:59.600 --> 04:26:01.400
for me to talk about in the future,

04:26:01.400 --> 04:26:04.500
go to lexfridman.com/ama.

04:26:05.666 --> 04:26:08.900
I'd like to use this opportunity
to talk about Franz Kafka,

04:26:08.900 --> 04:26:10.333
one of my favorite writers.

04:26:11.166 --> 04:26:12.833
The reason he has been on my mind

04:26:12.833 --> 04:26:15.000
is that his work, "The Trial,"

04:26:15.000 --> 04:26:17.466
and the case of Pavel Duro in France

04:26:17.466 --> 04:26:20.366
has, let's say, eerie parallels,

04:26:20.366 --> 04:26:23.300
both metaphorically and literally.

04:26:23.300 --> 04:26:25.666
Of course, "The Trial"
is a work of fiction,

04:26:25.666 --> 04:26:27.133
but I think it is often useful

04:26:27.133 --> 04:26:29.700
to go to the surreal world of literature,

04:26:29.700 --> 04:26:32.566
even of the over-the-top dystopian variety

04:26:32.566 --> 04:26:35.233
like "1984," "Animal Farm,"

04:26:35.233 --> 04:26:37.133
"Brave New World," "The Trial,"

04:26:37.133 --> 04:26:39.000
"The Castle," "Metamorphosis,"

04:26:39.000 --> 04:26:42.033
even "The Plague" by Albert Camus,

04:26:42.033 --> 04:26:44.800
all to better understand our real world

04:26:44.800 --> 04:26:46.433
and the destructive paths

04:26:46.433 --> 04:26:49.400
we have the potential to go down together,

04:26:49.400 --> 04:26:52.666
which also hopefully helps us understand

04:26:52.666 --> 04:26:54.366
how to avoid doing so.

04:26:55.400 --> 04:26:58.233
So lemme zoom out and
speak about Franz Kafka.

04:26:58.233 --> 04:26:59.500
Who was he?

04:26:59.500 --> 04:27:02.966
He was an insurance
clerk who wrote at night.

04:27:02.966 --> 04:27:05.600
He died young and almost
completely unknown,

04:27:05.600 --> 04:27:09.633
and he asked for his
manuscripts to be burned.

04:27:09.633 --> 04:27:12.566
Luckily for us, his friend, Max Brod,

04:27:12.566 --> 04:27:14.033
refused to do so,

04:27:14.033 --> 04:27:16.500
giving us the work of what I consider

04:27:16.500 --> 04:27:19.233
to be one of 20th
century's greatest writers.

04:27:20.200 --> 04:27:21.800
In his work, Kafka wrote

04:27:21.800 --> 04:27:24.033
about the cold, machine-like reduction

04:27:24.033 --> 04:27:25.766
of humanistic case files

04:27:25.766 --> 04:27:28.500
through the labyrinth
of institutional power.

04:27:28.500 --> 04:27:32.266
He wrote about an
individual's feeling of guilt

04:27:32.266 --> 04:27:35.066
even when a crime has not been committed.

04:27:35.066 --> 04:27:36.300
Or more generally,

04:27:36.300 --> 04:27:38.100
he wrote about the feeling of anxiety

04:27:38.100 --> 04:27:39.966
that is part of the human condition

04:27:39.966 --> 04:27:42.500
in our modern, chaotic world.

04:27:42.500 --> 04:27:46.233
His writing style was to use
short, declarative sentences

04:27:46.233 --> 04:27:48.633
to describe the surreal and the absurd,

04:27:48.633 --> 04:27:51.100
and in so doing, effectively, I think,

04:27:51.100 --> 04:27:53.200
convey the feeling of an experience

04:27:53.200 --> 04:27:56.866
versus simply describing the experience.

04:27:56.866 --> 04:27:58.566
For example, famously,

04:27:58.566 --> 04:28:00.266
his work, "The Metamorphosis"

04:28:00.266 --> 04:28:02.066
opens with the following lines,

04:28:03.200 --> 04:28:06.966
"As Gregor Samsa awoke one
morning from uneasy dreams,

04:28:06.966 --> 04:28:09.566
he found himself transformed in his bed

04:28:09.566 --> 04:28:11.366
into a gigantic insect.

04:28:12.433 --> 04:28:15.100
He was lying in his
hard, armor-plated back,

04:28:15.100 --> 04:28:17.233
and when he lifted his head a little,

04:28:17.233 --> 04:28:19.366
he could see his dome-like brown belly

04:28:19.366 --> 04:28:22.733
divided into stiff arched segments,

04:28:22.733 --> 04:28:26.700
on top of which the bed quilt
could hardly keep in position

04:28:26.700 --> 04:28:29.566
and was about to slide off completely.

04:28:29.566 --> 04:28:31.066
His numerous legs,

04:28:31.066 --> 04:28:33.100
which were pitifully thin

04:28:33.100 --> 04:28:34.866
compared to the rest of his bulk,

04:28:34.866 --> 04:28:37.366
waved helplessly before his eyes."

04:28:38.566 --> 04:28:41.866
Kafka, I think,
effectively uses this image

04:28:41.866 --> 04:28:45.466
of being transformed into a
giant bug stuck on his back

04:28:45.466 --> 04:28:47.733
to convey a feeling of helplessness

04:28:47.733 --> 04:28:50.666
and uselessness to his family,

04:28:50.666 --> 04:28:53.666
to his job, to society.

04:28:53.666 --> 04:28:56.000
The feeling of being a burden to everyone,

04:28:56.000 --> 04:28:59.866
dehumanized, alienated, and abandoned,

04:28:59.866 --> 04:29:02.633
the feeling of being
only temporarily valued

04:29:02.633 --> 04:29:04.966
as long as he served some function

04:29:04.966 --> 04:29:07.966
for his job or for his family,

04:29:07.966 --> 04:29:09.833
and quickly discarded otherwise.

04:29:10.966 --> 04:29:13.600
I will probably talk about
this work in more depth

04:29:13.600 --> 04:29:14.900
at another time,

04:29:14.900 --> 04:29:16.666
because it is so haunting

04:29:16.666 --> 04:29:19.600
and I think it is such
a profound description

04:29:19.600 --> 04:29:22.433
of the burden of existence
in modern society

04:29:22.433 --> 04:29:23.433
for many people.

04:29:24.333 --> 04:29:27.966
But here, lemme talk about
another of his work, "The Trial."

04:29:28.966 --> 04:29:31.800
In this novel, the main
character, Josef K,

04:29:31.800 --> 04:29:33.900
is a successful bank officer,

04:29:33.900 --> 04:29:38.700
and he's arrested on his
birthday for an unspecified crime

04:29:38.700 --> 04:29:41.866
by a kind of amorphous court

04:29:41.866 --> 04:29:44.566
whose authority is everywhere and nowhere.

04:29:44.566 --> 04:29:46.966
He navigates a labyrinth-like legal system

04:29:46.966 --> 04:29:49.066
where everyone knows about his case,

04:29:49.066 --> 04:29:51.366
but no one can really explain it.

04:29:51.366 --> 04:29:53.100
The so-called trial

04:29:53.100 --> 04:29:56.133
never actually occurs in
any conventional sense.

04:29:56.133 --> 04:29:58.366
Instead, Josef K's entire life

04:29:58.366 --> 04:30:01.700
becomes the proceedings
leading up to the trial.

04:30:01.700 --> 04:30:04.200
In a sense, "The Trial" is the state

04:30:04.200 --> 04:30:05.866
of being accused itself,

04:30:05.866 --> 04:30:09.200
a permanent condition rather
than a singular event.

04:30:10.300 --> 04:30:13.333
Kafka's genius in this work was to show

04:30:13.333 --> 04:30:16.833
that modern institutions
don't need to hold trials,

04:30:16.833 --> 04:30:18.333
they just need to hold you

04:30:18.333 --> 04:30:20.600
in the permanent looming
possibility of one.

04:30:21.633 --> 04:30:22.966
Public attention to this case,

04:30:22.966 --> 04:30:24.266
both positive and negative,

04:30:24.266 --> 04:30:25.433
gives Josef K a feeling

04:30:25.433 --> 04:30:28.366
of constantly being judged
by people around him.

04:30:28.366 --> 04:30:30.500
This wears at his mind

04:30:31.366 --> 04:30:34.533
and his psychological wellbeing
begins to deteriorate.

04:30:35.400 --> 04:30:38.200
In a sense, the trial
doesn't need to convict him.

04:30:38.200 --> 04:30:40.066
The internal psychological turmoil

04:30:40.066 --> 04:30:43.500
and the external social
scrutiny performs a conviction

04:30:43.500 --> 04:30:45.166
and the eventual execution.

04:30:46.233 --> 04:30:48.200
And exactly one year after his arrest,

04:30:48.200 --> 04:30:51.066
Josef K is visited by two men

04:30:51.066 --> 04:30:54.300
who walk him courteously through the city

04:30:54.300 --> 04:30:56.266
to an abandoned quarry

04:30:56.266 --> 04:30:58.666
and stab him in the heart,

04:30:58.666 --> 04:31:00.200
without Josef K resisting.

04:31:01.800 --> 04:31:03.766
To me, "The Trial" shows

04:31:03.766 --> 04:31:07.866
that tyranny's final victory
isn't when it kills you,

04:31:07.866 --> 04:31:10.333
but when you hold still for the knife,

04:31:10.333 --> 04:31:12.166
not because you're forced,

04:31:12.166 --> 04:31:14.933
but because you've been
exhausted into submission.

04:31:16.666 --> 04:31:18.966
Once again, it is a haunting story

04:31:18.966 --> 04:31:21.500
of the soullessness of bureaucracy

04:31:21.500 --> 04:31:23.866
and its suffocation of the human spirit.

04:31:24.900 --> 04:31:26.933
I highly recommend this short book,

04:31:26.933 --> 04:31:29.733
and I'll probably talk about
it even more in the future.

04:31:30.866 --> 04:31:32.833
I don't think it's
especially useful for me

04:31:32.833 --> 04:31:35.266
to speak any parallels between "The Trial"

04:31:35.266 --> 04:31:36.800
and Pavel Durov's case,

04:31:36.800 --> 04:31:40.066
because after all, "The
Trial" is a work of fiction.

04:31:41.100 --> 04:31:44.400
But on a positive note, let me
report that as far as I saw,

04:31:44.400 --> 04:31:46.700
Pavel has maintained optimism

04:31:46.700 --> 04:31:48.100
and a general positive outlook

04:31:48.100 --> 04:31:49.966
throughout this whole process.

04:31:49.966 --> 04:31:52.066
What I always fear in such cases

04:31:52.066 --> 04:31:54.933
is that a bureaucratic
system can wear people down,

04:31:54.933 --> 04:31:57.266
exhaust them into surrendering.

04:31:57.266 --> 04:31:59.866
I saw none of that with Pavel.

04:31:59.866 --> 04:32:02.666
I don't think he knows
how to give up or give in,

04:32:02.666 --> 04:32:05.400
no matter how much pressure he's under.

04:32:05.400 --> 04:32:08.066
Again, this is truly inspiring to me.

04:32:09.166 --> 04:32:11.933
Also, now that we're talking about it,

04:32:11.933 --> 04:32:14.000
let me mention some other of Kafka's work

04:32:14.000 --> 04:32:17.566
that was moving to me, "The Castle."

04:32:17.566 --> 04:32:19.800
A similar description as "The Trial" does

04:32:19.800 --> 04:32:22.800
of the absurd inaccessibility
of those in authority

04:32:22.800 --> 04:32:25.100
of the nightmarish bureaucracy.

04:32:25.100 --> 04:32:27.566
The character in "The
Castle" is also named K.

04:32:27.566 --> 04:32:30.466
Both bureaucracies operate
through exhaustion,

04:32:30.466 --> 04:32:34.066
endless deferrals,
procedures, waiting rooms.

04:32:34.066 --> 04:32:37.366
Again, highly relevant to modern times.

04:32:37.366 --> 04:32:39.300
I can also highly recommend Kafka's

04:32:39.300 --> 04:32:42.733
"In the Penal Colony" and "Hunger Artist."

04:32:42.733 --> 04:32:46.266
Both are too interesting and weird

04:32:46.266 --> 04:32:49.300
to explain in depth here.

04:32:49.300 --> 04:32:51.200
But let me say, "The
Hunger Artist" is a story

04:32:51.200 --> 04:32:52.066
that I think is relevant

04:32:52.066 --> 04:32:54.166
to our modern-day attention economy,

04:32:54.166 --> 04:32:56.700
where so many people want to be famous.

04:32:56.700 --> 04:33:00.466
It tells the story of a,
let's say, professional faster

04:33:00.466 --> 04:33:04.633
who performs starvation in
a cage as entertainment,

04:33:04.633 --> 04:33:08.100
and he slowly loses his
audience to newer spectacles,

04:33:08.100 --> 04:33:08.933
so much so

04:33:08.933 --> 04:33:11.066
that eventually when he starves himself

04:33:11.066 --> 04:33:13.200
to death, nobody cares.

04:33:14.766 --> 04:33:16.933
Kafka's work is heavy.

04:33:16.933 --> 04:33:19.200
It serves as a warning for the nightmare

04:33:19.200 --> 04:33:21.166
that civilization can become,

04:33:21.166 --> 04:33:23.933
and yet I think it is
also a source of optimism,

04:33:23.933 --> 04:33:25.866
because when we can recognize elements

04:33:25.866 --> 04:33:28.200
of our own world in Kafka's stories,

04:33:28.200 --> 04:33:30.233
when we can see elements
of our institutions

04:33:30.233 --> 04:33:31.966
in "The Trial" or in "The Castle,"

04:33:31.966 --> 04:33:34.766
when we can see ourselves in Gregor Samsa,

04:33:34.766 --> 04:33:37.166
we're not just diagnosing the disease,

04:33:37.166 --> 04:33:39.800
we're proving that we're still human

04:33:39.800 --> 04:33:42.066
and wise enough to see it and name it.

04:33:42.933 --> 04:33:44.866
Kafka gave us the goal

04:33:44.866 --> 04:33:48.500
to resist against such systems
that try to dehumanize us

04:33:48.500 --> 04:33:50.833
and to ensure that individual freedom

04:33:50.833 --> 04:33:53.400
and the human spirit keep flourishing.

04:33:53.400 --> 04:33:54.866
I think it will.

04:33:54.866 --> 04:33:57.466
I have faith in us humans.

04:33:57.466 --> 04:33:58.400
I love you all.

